      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Only Woman Saved.—The tragedy of the sinking ef the great German-Lloyd steamer Elbe. is recalled by the reverse of fortune which is stated to have overtaken Miss Anna Bocker.. She was the only woman saved out of 350 passengers who were voyaging to New York in the Elbe, when the steamer was rrn into in the North</p>
        <p>Sea on a bitterly cold Is inter's night, and sunk, some ten years ago. Alias Brooker was then only nineteen years of age, and after her terrible experience she was t • received at Osborne and congratulated by Queen Victoria, and the late Empress Frederick of Germany on her escape. She is now said to have suffered the loss I of all her savings in consequence of having entrusted them to a defaultins." ' solicitor, and her friends are ap- pealing for financial on her behalf. The Teacher of "Jenny Lind."—The King is always ready to honour those distingished in the art of music I and with this desire his Majesty received at Bucking- I ham Palace Senor Manuel Garcia on the accesaion of his hundredth birthday last week. The privilege of celebrating the hundredth anniversary of a birthday is given to few, and in the case of this famous musician it is the more notable because of his great services, not only to the art of music but also to the science of medicine. Seiler Garcia, who is a Spaniard by birth, ' was the teacher of "Jenny Lind," whose fame as a singer is now only a tradition to all but the oldest generation, but her husband, Mr. Otto Goldschmidt, is still living and was present at the banquet and celebra- tion in Seflor Garcia's honour. Many of his pupils who achieved fame in the concert room, have passed away, but M. Santley was one of several who were present to congratulate their old teacher—SeSor Garcia' who can remember the time when Napoleon was at the height of his glory, is now feeble with age, but he took' an active part in the day's celebrations, and at the ban- quet, made a speech in which he amused the ladies by a genial reference to the peculiarities with which they are supposed to approach the question of their age. Spendthrift Romances.—To Lady Chetwynd belongs the distinction, and also the misfortune of having been connected by marriage with two of the greatest spend- thrifts of their time, the late and last Marquis of Hastings, and the late Marquis of Anglesey. Her first marriage when she was Lady Florence Paget was one of the wildest romances of the Victorian era' as while engaged to Mr. Henry Chaplin she married her cousin Lord Hastings. After his death, and her marriage with Sir George Chetwynd, she became the mother of Miss Florence Paget Chetwynd, who is the late Marquis of • Anglesey's widow. Lady Chetwynd, was therefore the wife of Lord Hastings, and the mother-in-law of the late Marquis who achieved the distinction of going bankrupt on an income of over £lOO,OOO a year. The new Marquis is a youth of twenty, the son of Lady Alexander Paget, who married an uncle of the latelLord Anglesey. all her children have succeeded to brilliant destines. Formerly it could hardly have been expected that her son would become Marquis of Anglesey, but such is the case, and of her two daughters one is married to the son and heir of the Earl of Pembroke and the other to the son and heir of the Earl of Shrewsbury. Church Bazaars and Entertainments.—lt is all very well for the Bishop of Durham to denounce con- certs, dances, and bazaars, but what is to take their place if such such entertainments are to be abandoned as a means of raising money for Church purposes? As to dances and bazaars, the Bishop has nothing to say against them, but their associations he says are essenti- ally other than religious, and it would appear that he regards them as unsuitable for the purpose of raising Church funds. We all know that a certain amount of frivolity and firtation goes on in connection vvi th Church bazaars and entertainments, that they even encourage the vice of gambling, and are frequently, the cause of jealously and intrigue between neighbours, and of extravagance in dress. Such is the indictment, which in many instances could be drawn up against bazaars, entertainments, and schemes " for working for the Church" generally, in which women and girls take a leading part. But after all it is not very terrible, and if they are to be condemned it will be asked in many a country vicarage what is to take their place in raising the necessary wherewithal to pay off debts, to repair the steeple, or make up the curates' fund, and so on? A Silken Season.—This is to be a silken year,the silks ranging in substance from brocades that stand alone to the most airy fairy taffetas chiffons. Silk linings too, are again in request, though the rustling varieties are still tabooed. For the handsome home manufactured brocades with rich Louis XV. designs of flowers and ribbons, there is at the moment, a great demand also for the pompadour printed chiqe silks, while the craze for tiny over-checked designs, shows no sign of diminution. About Linens.—A great modiste has said that when we are not clothed in silk, this corning summer, we shall be wearing linen, and apparently this prophecy is to come to pass. Linen " robes "abound, the skirts ready to be seamed up at the back, and sewn into the waistband, while the hodice material already ornamented with em- broidery, needs only quite amateur skill in the making up. The more dressy linen robes are elaborately orna- mented with !Irish crochet, or the modish broderie Anglaise, and these will form ideal toilettes for garden parties, races, and indeed all fashionable outdoor func- tions. The White Sales.—Underskirts of white cambric with deep flounces of broderic Anglaise are in the very height of fashion, and at the moment, when the white sales are in full swing, the possibilities of obtaining such treasures at a reduced price, are considerable. Again, nearly all great emporiums include lace in their White Sales, and the woman who neglects an opportunity to secure a bargain in lace, is capable of any act of foolish- ness. To wear with a new spring coat and skirt, the lace vest is most desirable, and a remnant of good lace mounted over chiffon, and built up over soft white satin, makes an ideal garment of this description. The Craze for Embroidery.—All the best woollen, linen, and cotton shirts show band embroidery, and the clever home worker will find ample scope in this direc- tion for her ingenuity. Shirt lengths, traced ready to commence upon, may now be had, also sets of fronts, collars and cuffs, in linens, muslins, cottons, and divers other fabrics. A noted nerve specialist is of opinion that the new fad for embroidery is one of the best cures for "nerves," and is recommending all patients so afflicted to devote themselves to this occupation, Schools for Happiness.—Happiness is an elusive pur- suit and Lady Warwick's idea that we should start School for Happiness for Children, seems rather chimerical. People, whether young or grown up, differ- ently constituted, and therefore cannot be made happy in the same way. Many regard happiness as identical with pleasure, bat it is well known that there are none so miserable as those who live solely a life of pleasure. Others there are who would never be happy if they were restrained from the opportunity of grumbling at fate, or the small worries of life. An American editor once asked his lady readers to say which was the happiest day of their lives, and though each one had different ideas, many agreed that matter of dress had a good deal to do with a woman's happinss. Probably most of us find that happiness lies more in anticipation than in present reality and that when we arrive at the things upon which we have set our hearts, they too often are but ashes in the mouth. Still it is possible that children of adverse temperament might be trained to look habitu- ally on the bright side of things, and if one is able to find happiness in small things be or she is pretty sure of a contented mind, which at any rate, will take one a long way on the road to the desired end. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>STOKCTON HEATH v. TIMPERLEY.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday Stockton Heath played their return game with Timperley at Grappenhall-road. Timperley attacked from the bully-off, but Johnson finished , weakly. Still, Timperley kept on the offensive, and hesitating play by the Stockton backs almost let the visitors in. The home team then pressed, but Richard- son finished badly when well-placed. From the bully on t the 25 line, Davenport carried the ball well down the field, and the home backs failing to clear his centre, t Timperley scored from close quarters. Stockton at I once attacked, and just before half-time, after some beautiful combination, S. Baxter shot through, but was I ruled off-side. Half-time score : Timperley, 1 ; 1 Stockton Heath, 0. In the first ten minutes of the ' second half, the Stockton forwards overplayed the visiting backs, and it was not long before W. Richard- son put the teams level. Following this the visitors attacked strongly but wore unable to score. Final score : Stockton Heath, 1; Timperley, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LATCHFORD v. SOUTH MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD v. SOUTH MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>Flayed at Brookes Bar. From the bully off the home team went away with a rush, the visiting goal-keeper being called upon in the first few minutes. The South team kept up the attack for sometime. Their right out- side secured and put in a good shot which Unsworth stopped, but failed to clear. Soon after this reverse the visitors had a spell of attacking. Spenseley made a good run down the wing and put in a beautiful centre frem which Albinson equalized. Iramediately after this Leßoy scored another for the komesters with a beauti- ful shot. On restarting after the interval the home team had all their own way for a time, making their lead more substantial by adding two more goals, but during the last quarter of an hour the visitors were continually attacking, and eventually Banks added a second goal after a neat bit of play. Directly after this Albinson scored again from a scramble in the goal mouth. Nothing further was added,and Manchester ran out winners of a hard game by four goals to three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>ZAM-BUK EXPELS DISEASE.</head>
        <p>ZAM-BUK EXPELS DISEASE.</p>
        <p>Severe Abscess Completely Cured.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk heals the skin and expels disease as no ordinary preparation can. Take the experience of Mr. James Clinton, a Corporation pavior, of 13, Wriggles- worth Place, Richmond Road, Leeds, who was for five years afflicted with an abscess. Growths of this nature prevail in their worst form at this season. Said Mr. Clinton,to a Leeds reporter : The first thing I noticed was a slight itching sensation on my leg. Soon the place beceme so swollen and painful that I was only able to walk with the greatest difficulty, It tortured me to such an extent that I could hardly go on with my work. I could not sleep for it at night, and lost my rest dozens of times. I went to five doctors, who told me to try poultices, lotions, powders. salves, and all sorts of things, but nothing seemed to affect the growth. At last a friend told me to try Zana-Bak, and I did so. With the first application it gave more signs of doing me good than the doctors' and chemists' pre- parations had ever done. Zarn-Buk eased me at once. With repeated applications of the balm I noticed that further development of the abscess was arrested, the pain died away entirely, the inflammation was allayed, and the swelling got less and less. Finally after proper perseverance, the abscess completely dis- appeared, and I have never had any trouble since. The place is quite healed, and 1 have Zam-Bak alone to thank for ridding me of such a long-standing source of torture and weakness. lam more than satisfied with.Zamßak."</p>
        <p>When the skin loses "tone," or gets out of health, as not infrequently is the case after winter's indoor con- finement, Zam-Buk is Nature's remedy. It stimulates the action of the pores and eradicates disease. For abscesses, pimples, ulcers, chronio sores, eczema, scalp irritation, chafing, soreness, weak or diseased ankles, bad legs, piles, cuts, bruises, burns, scalds, sprains, inflamma- tion of all kinds, and for dispelling aches and pains, Zam-Buk is without equal. Price Is. lid. per box; or 2s. 9d. for large family size (2s. 9d. box contains nearly four times the Is. 11d.) ; of all chemists, or post free for same prices direct from the Zam-Buk Co., 4, Red Cross-street, B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHILDREN'S GYMNASTIC DISPLAY. Prizes Distributed at the Gymnasium.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S GYMNASTIC DISPLAY. Prizes Distributed at the Gymnasium.</p>
        <p>The townspeople of Warrington were on Monday even- ing afforded a further opportunity of estimating the valuable work which the Borough Gymnasium is doing in encouraging and facilitating physical training among the school children. The occasion was the annual musical drill and gymnastic display, and opportunity was also taken to distribute the prizes gained by the youthful pupils—as well as the adult "champion eight"—during the past session. There was a large attendance, the Mayor (Alderman Bolton), who was accompanied by the Mayoress, presiding. A programme of exceptional interest was submitted, and it Is due to the director of exercises (Mr. J. Kinsley) and to the assistant instructor (Mr. A. Mottrarn) to at once state that the display reflected the utmost credit upon them. Not only in muscular development has the excellent system of training had its effect, but the children have acquired considerable ;kill in a varied series of _gymnastic exercises. Dumb-bells, bar- bells and Indian clubs were cleverly manipulated and both boys and girls acquitted themselves surpris- ingly well on the row of rings, parallel bars, and vault- ing horse. Excitement ran high during a couple of splendidly-contested tugs of war. In the first the little boys just managed Co triumph over the little girls. The struggle between the elder boys and girls resulted in a victory for the girls, who were naturally in high glee over their meritorious achievement. An exhibition on the horizontal bar by the members of the men's class proved a popular feature of the entertainment, and great merriment was created by a blind-fold boxing contest between C. Sinclair and S. Price. After this little diver- sion the handkerchiefs were removed from the eyes of the competitors, who tro 'ted the audience to a" round" in earnest. During the interval the Mayoress distributed suitable prizes to the following.—Girls' class: 1, Adeline King; 2, Doris Parkinson; 3, Florrie Bennett. Boys' class : 1, Fred Jolley ; 2, John Sanders; 3, John Gallon. Men's class (champion eight) : 1, C. Sinclair; 2,T. McWhirter; 3, C. Doncaster; 4, S. Price; 5, H. Holloway; 6, 0. Corbett 7, T. Blower ;8, F. Walker. Mr. Sinclair' it may be mentioned is deserving of special credit as he has carried off the first prize three times in succession. A vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress was proposed by Councillor TINNION who, as Chairman of the Gymnasium Committee, said he was exceedingly pleased to see the large attendance. He thought he might take it that their sentiments were similar to his own, and that they were all delighted to see the children taking such an interest in gymnastics. The vote was heartily carried and the MAYOR briefly responded. He cordially congratalated the champion eight who; he was sure, could hold their own with any champion eight in Lancashire. A word of praise is due to Miss A. Clayton who supplied the pianoforte accompaniments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Increase in the Rates,</p>
        <p>PROTEST AGAINST UNNECESSARY EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present Messrs. H. Woodward (presiding), C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, G. Egerton Warburton, J. Whitlow, T. Baxter, H. M. Davies, J. H. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, H. Mawdsley, T. Guest, H. Woodward, W. Harrison, G. Pilling, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, E. Brether- ton, C. Horton, R. A. Chrimes, J. Fryer, J. Spruce, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, C. E. Linaker, jun., J. Mounfleld, A. G. Guest, T. Antwis, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical officer.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LOAN SANCTIONED.</head>
        <p>LOAN SANCTIONED.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote sanctioning the application of the Council for permission to borrow the sum of £4OO for the erection of an acetylene gas plant at the infectious diseases hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PROTEST AGAINST EXPENDITURE.</head>
        <p>PROTEST AGAINST EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that he had received the following resolution which had been adopted at the annual parish meeting at Grappenhall :—We the ratepayers of Grappenhall deprecate the excessive expenditure by County and Rural District Councils and considering the hardness of the times and the suffering to which the poor are exposed through lack of work and employment, we implore the Councils to curtail as far as practicable all expendi- ture so that the rates may assume a normal state.' Mr. Hallows said he was pleased to see that the little village of Grappenhall had had the courage to speak out It was time something was done. The County Council spent a lot of money. There were men on the County Council who did not know the meaning of self-denial. He proposed that the resolution be sent to the County Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ashton : The resolution has already been sent to the County Council. Mr. Hallows continuing said it was not the District Council which was spending money. There was nothing but retrenchment, but people put their "pet " friends on the County Council who spent money right and left. Mr. Woodward said he sympathised very much with the resolution. The District Council however were bound by certain rules and found it most difficult to curtail expenses. Mr. Hallows: I have been waiting for someone to send a resolution to the County Council and as no one came forward I felt almost inclined to send one myself. The estimates for the half year ending 30th September next, were submitted. The probable balance in hand was £1196 and the estimated expenditure was : —General expenses (public health) £1374, highway ex- penses £3435, balance and contingency fund £1563, total £6372. It was recommended that a rate of 'rid. in the £ an increase of id. be levied.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce asked if it was not possible for any curtail- ment of the expenses. He thought it very desirable that they should keep the expenses as low as possible. He had intended to move that it be an instruction to the surveyors to take special care with special expenses during the next half Year. People were groaning and crying out against the burden of the rates. Mr. Hallows: They are talking about getting up a petition in Stockton Heath. Mr. Wright: They must petition the County Conn cil Mr. Woodward: Our increased expenditure is due to the isolation hospital which we were required to build by the Local Uovernment Board. Mr. Whitley: Are there no means whereby we can request the County Council to get out their estimates a little earlier? Overseers are sometimes only allowed a fortnight to get in the rate. The clerk explained that the County Council had already been approached in the matter but had expressed their inability to get the estimates out any earlier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FURNISHiNG OF THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE FURNISHiNG OF THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The following firms were requested to send in tenders for the furnishing of the isolation hospital : Messrs. R. Garnett and Sons,Messrs. Greenhalgh and Company,Mr. Ralph Johnson, Mr. J. G. Weaver, and Messrs. Ray and Miles (Liverpool). The Longford Wire Rope Company and Messrs. Monks Hall and Company were requested to send in tenders for the sppply of wire beds. MR. DIGGLE'S HEATH. Mr. Diggle stated that his medical attendant had advised him to go for a sea voyage for a month. The Council unanimously agreed to grant Mr. Diggle leave of absence for that period.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Increase in the Rates.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Bolrd of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, when there were present :—Messrs. A. R. Norman (in the chair),C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, G. Egerton Warburton, J. Whitlow, T. Baxter, H. M. Davies, J. H. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, H. .Mawdsley, T. Guest, H. Wood- ward, W. Harrison, G. Pilling, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, C. Horton. R. A. Chrimes, J. Fryer, J. Spruce, W. M. Morris, J. Douglas, J. W. Timperley, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, C. E. Linaker, jun., J. Mounfleld, A. G. Guest, T. Antwis, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Cunnah (workhouse master). T. Cook. T. Riley, and DePrez (reliving officers),</p>
        <p>TENDERS ACCEPTED:</p>
        <p>The following tenders were accepted :—Fish, H. W. Hills; drapery. P. R. Wright; clothing, T. G. Lee: grocery &amp;c. E. S. Collinson: butcher's meat, .H. Singleton; boots &amp;c., A. W. Bosley; drugs &amp;c., H. A. Gibson; coffins, T. Borrow; coal and coke, R. Evans and company; milk, J. E. Richardson; funerals T. Booth and Sons.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF RELIEVING OFFICER. The committee who were appointed to consider the applications for the position of relieving officer to till the vacancy caused by the resignation of Mr. Riley, reported that they had received 25 applications for the post. They recommended that the following gentle- men be requested to attend before the next meeting of the Board :—Mr, Wesley Coventry, Runcorn; Mr. W. A. Lewis, Frodsham; Mr. J. Pearson, Grappenhall ; Mr. A. Robinson, Frodsham; Mr. T. Saunders, Runcorn; and Mr. W. H. Whalley, Frodsham. Mr. Norman thought the copies of the testimonials of each of the selected candidates should be sent to every member of the Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitley: I have already received copies of the testimonials sent direct by three of the candidates. Mr. Woodward moved that the Clerk should send out copies of the testimonials he had received. The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>MR. RILEY'S SITPERANNVATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward said Mr. Riley bad served the Board very faithfully for over 29 years. He was entitled to claim £77 ss. 9d. 'per annum superannuation, but the committee specially appointed to consider that question recommended that the sum be increased to £9B.</p>
        <p>I The recommendation of the Committee was approved ESTIMATES FOR THE NEXT HALF YEAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward presented the estimates for the coming half year. The balance in band was £3,218, and in addi- tion the sum of £13,647 would be required. He recom- mended that a rate of is. 3d. in the .£ be levied, an increase of 2d. in the .C, as compared with the rate during the past half year. ! Mr. Ashton explained that the increase in the rates , was due to education.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter : The increase is a very serious matter for the ratepayers. Mr. Norman: I think that as we are paying so much money we should have full control of it.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF MR. T. S. SWALE, WIDNES. ' The funeral of Mr. T. S. Swale,Highfield-road,Widnes whose death occurred on Monday, after a long illness, took place on Saturday at Farnworth Cemetery,. The deceased gentleman was the proprietor of the "Widnes Weekly News." which he established over thirty years ' ago, and during the whole of his connection with the chemical borough he was prominently associated with Victoria-road Wesleyan Chapel. Previous to the ; interment a service was held in the latter place of worship, and an eloquent add-ess was delivered by the Rev. C. D. Newman. The procession was of great length,and included Lacey-street Sunday school officials , members of deceased's society and Bible classes and members of the staff of the "Weekly News." ; The chief mourners were Mrs. Swale (widow), Joseph, Jack, William and Thomas Swale (sons), Mrs. C. Poole (daughter), Mr. William Swale (brother), Mrs. W. Chadwick (sister), Mrs, Joseph Swale (daughter-in- law), Mr. W. L. Bolshaw (brother-in-law), Mr. S. Beesley, Mr. W. Chadwick, Mr. Vincent (West Brom- wich), and Mr. Bryant. Amongst those also present at the graveside were Alderman G. Neil, Alderman G. W Wareing, Councillors Owens, Peters, Bullough, Boyle Pankhurst, Lewis, Clarke, Wood, Laws, and Smith ; Mr. B. Brown, J.P.. the Revs.—Marriott,Douglas E. Morton, (vicar of St. Paul's), J. Byrant. F. Pickup, J. Pedlar, J. ; A. Canon, and J. Wright Williams (vicar of Farnworth); Superintendent Strickland, Messrs. I. Carr, T. Dodd, Nicklin, Walker, F. Williamson, and others. The coffin was covered with a large number of beautiful wreaths.</p>
        <p>.„ • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>miner, Saturday, March 25, 1905. I WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>miner, Saturday, March 25, 1905. I WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PRINCESS'S PENCHANT.</head>
        <p>THE PRINCESS'S PENCHANT.</p>
        <p>It is stated that the only expensive penchant of the Princess of Wales is for rare and costly lace. Not long ago, says To-Day, the Princess sent a trusty messenger to Amsterdam to purchase a rare handkerchief, said to be the oldest pattern of Mechlin lace, from an impoverished Flemish family, and she paid £3,000 for the piece.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SPRING COSTUMES.</head>
        <p>SPRING COSTUMES.</p>
        <p>Fine days at a smart seaside resort tend to bring out spring toilets which will not see daylight in town for another week or two; and if these early models are to be relied on, a few notes on costumes recently seen braving the fitful March sunlight by the white-crested waves may be helpful to some of our readers. Short, pleated skirts were much in evidence, one Norfolk coat in shepherd's-plaid being worn with a short sunray-pleated skirt. A skirt</p>
        <p>in dark tartan had the fulness arranged round the hips in a multitude of minute box-pleats' and was worn with a smart tartan coatee much trimmed with tan suede. Our sketch shews yet another costume with a pleated skirt, this time in thin red cloth, with a close-fitting cutaway coat, adorned with black suede revers and buttons, and with vest and strappings of buff cloth. The red straw hat worn with it was trimmed with trails of shaded red flowers.</p>
        <p>„. tipry, 11.6- . '''",-.......1 - - , , 44,.... I -- 10----,,-- r,•.,,,,, ~ /i - , ~ , , ~,, •v 1 I • \k, ,c 0 ,z / y , • _ .fill, / / • , , •:- ' -.•-- \ , . t _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MRS. KENDAL.</head>
        <p>MRS. KENDAL.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Xeridal, who celebrated her birthday the other day, was the youngest of a family of twenty- two children, the elder of whom was the late Mr. Tom Robertson, ,the dramatist. Her debut was made in London on July 29th, 1865. She married William H. Grimston on August 7th, 1869, Kendal being only the nom de guerre of the clever couple. In addition to her brilliant histrionic talent, Mrs. Kendal has exceedingly literary tastes, is devoted to reading, and has contributed "Dramatic Opinion" to Murray's Magazine. She has travelled a great deal in the pursuit of her profession, one of her greatest successes being her tour with her husband in the United States of America and Canada. Mrs. Kendal has always been the happy possessor of a beautiful skin, and has been known to give the following as her complexion recipe: "Ten hours' sleep every night, a good mile walk every clay; vigorous rubbing in cold water; brown bread; no sweets; and no coffee."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>NOVEL BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A NOVEL BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>The blouse shewn in our illustration is fashioned in a way somewhat out of the ordinary, and for this fact may appeal to some of our more originally- minded readers. The upper part of the blouse is in the form of a coatee, and is of soie-de-chine in a pale Parma-violet tint. It is finely tucked on the shoulders, and the full, loose sleeves are set in with a suggestion of prim little tucks. The coat and sleeves are hemmed. and outlined with French knots</p>
        <p>worked in violet silk. The unaer-blouse is of soft white lustred chiffon, entirely covered with minute hand-sewn tucks, alternating with lines. of delicate embroidery in mauve silks. The short sleeves end in a knife-pleated frill, caught in by mauve silk cords, which finish with little pompons. The draped belt is of shaded mauve chine silk, and fastens in front with small, round crystal buttons. The blouse may fasten at the side just under the edge of the coatee.</p>
