      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>LENTEN FARE.</head>
        <p>LENTEN FARE.</p>
        <p>It is one of the difficulties of the Paris house- keeper to make sufficient variety in the menus for Lent. In most families the Friday in each week is a jour maigre, but the nearer we approach Good Friday the less meat is eaten in orthodox circles, and the cooks have to shew their Capabilities. in making substantial and tasty dishes out of the pre- scribed fare. Vegetables at this season of the year are not plentiful; ,spara.gus at the present price is not to be thought ot. Tinned beans and peas are served in the restaurards, but the Parisienne turns her attention to salads. Anything in the way of green salad can be cooked, and the amount- of endive and watiircress eaten now is fantastic. It, may interest some of our readers, says the Gentlewoman, to know how these salads are prepared as hot vegetables. A dozen "heads" are washed and thrown into boiling water, well salted. When quite tender they are strained and passed through a colander, peppered and salted again, and well mixed with a little milk and 'a good lump of butter. They are now in a puree that can be served with hard eggs cut in slices, or halves, and make an appetising and nourishing dish in a menu where meat is barred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>TO-DAY S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>FLLirsxJSOLIiS CATALANE. —Take the fillets from two medium-sized soles, trim them, fold them in two, place them in a fiat stewpan, slightly salt them, add three or four tablespoonfuls of water (or cry white wine may be 'used instead of water), six tablespoonfuls of eatalane sauce, and one tea- spoonful of chopped parsley; put the lid on the srewpan and cook ,the soles for about twelve minutes. Arrange the fillets on a serving dish at once, add to the sauce 2oz. of fresh butter and a squeeze of lemon juice, shake the stewpan so that the sauce is well mixed, pour it over the fillets, and serve them at once. Turbot, brill, fresh cod, whiting, and salmon are prepared in the same way; the flesh of the fish is cut in pieces or \jn small fillets sr scalloped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>BIG FLAT-IRON IDEA</head>
        <p>THE BIG FLAT-IRON IDEA</p>
        <p>On a triangular plot of ground in the very heart of New York City stands the most extraordinary piece of architecture in the world. The triangle is not equal in measurement on all three sides, but is shaped something like a flat-iron and so the structure, before it was finished, came to be known as "The Flat-Iron Building It is 23 storeys or about 210 feet, high, built of stone and iron and the nose of the flat-iron points to the North, exactly where Fifth Avenue and Broadway 'cross each other at an acute angle. It is idle to call the building pretty, or to try to admire it. It simply astonishes people; shocks some of them. It towers over all other buildings near it, and when a strong wind blows from the West, the gale whirls around the angles of this building with such accumula- ted violence as to upset passing waggons and blow in the plate-glass windows of other ordinary stores and shops. I Stalwart policemen are always on duty there to help people, especially the ladies, who get caught in this whirlwind, and to make the fellows who admire smart lane and hosiery "move on, please ! " Mrs. Emma Mole, a laundry keeper, living at 92 'Latimer Road, London, W. said: I felt as if a great oad—a flat-iron I called it, to my husband—had been I lifted from my chest, and I was free and active again. This phrase shows how one's business influences their ideas and forms of thought. She was describing in a letter, dated October 27th, 1904, the miseries she had endured for many years from chronic bronchitis. muscular rheumatism, indigestion,headaches, drowsiness and all other aches and pains that come from " stomach troubles." She had had doctors and took various medicines and used liniments and embrocations, includ- ing some mustard oil brought from India—but all to no avail.</p>
        <p>At last she read in a little pamphlet, about Mother Seigel's Syrup, " and I have never ceased to bless that day," she says in that same letter. The first bottle gave her relief, and a few more cured her completely, and and she was never been troubled with aches or pains since. That is why she told her husband what she did about the flat-iron idea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>-LABOUR DEMONSTRATION. Councillor Stott and a Labour Candidate</head>
        <p>-LABOUR DEMONSTRATION. Councillor Stott and a Labour Candidate</p>
        <p>for Warrington. SPEECH BY MR. BEN TILLETT.</p>
        <p>The claims of labour were advocated at a meeting I which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening, under the auspices of the Warring- ton Labour Representation Committee. The Committee I has for some time, though unsuccessfully, sought to obtain a labour candidate to contest Warrington, and at the meeting Councillor Stott showed he has not yet given up hope of assisting in a labour fight in the borough. His remarks were brief, but during the course of them he advocated the taking of steps to secure the candidature of Mr. Clynes, district secxetary of the Gas Workers and General Labourers Union, than whom, he said, no better man could be secured. Mr. E. HORROCKS presided over a fair attendance and said that for two years the Labour Representation Committee had struggled along but their business had been chiefly to build up their organization. They recognized that it was hard to break a man of his political opinions, just as it was to break him of his religious views, and had decided, therefore, to launch I out in the way of publicly stating their policy and 1 taking political aation. (Applause.) Trade Unionists had stood up for a living wage and for the making of brighter and happier homes and now they came forward I and asked for public support for the Labour Party. It was said that there was no need for a Labour Party in Warrington but, he said " There will always be need for a Labour Party so long as we have the sweater and his victim ; there will be need for a Labour Party so long as we have the loafer in the street and the loafer in the brougham ; so long as we have willing men,strong men, men able to work, but unable to get work; so long as we have women and little children going about our towns and cities without that with which to keep body i and soul together. The need for the Labour Party s end always will be great and as it will be great we must be united upon one common platform—the workers' platform." (Applause.) Mr. BUCKLEY, secretary of the local L.R.C., moved "That this meeting of Trade Unionists and working men of Warrington, recognizing the necessity of direct and independent representation in Parliament, approves of the formation of a National Labour Representation Committee, and the forming of a Labour Party in Parliament whose policy is the rights of labour, and urges upon all present to support the local L.R.C. for the purpose of assisting the national movement." He pointed out that employers had combined and were much stronger than Trade Unions, and therefore it was neces- sary that labour should combine to protect its own interest. The employers, in fact, had a Parliamentary Committee, one of whose objects was, irrespective of party politics, to seek to oppose all the claims of Labour, and all measures of reform that came before the House of Commons. That was an example for Trade Unionists and the workers generally, and showed the necessity for direct and independent representation in Parliament. The present political parties were divided amongst them- selves with the result that labour got nothing. A change was necessary, not of a Tory into a Liberal but of Tories and Liberals into labour men. (Applause.) Mr. J. R. CLYNES seconded the resolution. It was, he said, regrettable that the working classes would not move in the right direction until they were forced to do so by circumstances which further enslaved them. The claims of labour were not considered in the House of Commons and it was because of that that it was necessary they should form an independent party for the protection of the interests of labour. (Applause) They were seeking direct legislation for the class which had been truly described as the class which was really the nation. While Labour was the chief factor in the land it was the least thought of in Parliament. That was because of the capitalist interest, and the obvious remedy was to have a direct Labour Party to curb monopoly, to drive out the personal and class power exercised even to tyranny. Their party would be an economic instrument and power for redeeming the en- slaved millions of to-day. They deserved the right not merely to live but to work and unless they had that light they were a degraded people. They desired im- mediate reform which would give the working classes the chance to live better lives than was possible at the present time and their only hope was in an independent labour party. (Applause). Mr, BEN TILLETT, the prospective labour candidate for Eccles, in a spirited address said the workers in the past had left others to do the thinking, but that they were now beginning to realize that it was best they should do their own political thinking. (Applause.) They were realizing now that they should not be kept in the depths of poverty at the will of the classes who had o object but to make profit out of them. Nothing but class interest was involved in party politics and the parliamentary life of the nation. Parliament</p>
        <p>at Present Nsia a piece of machiusry to be used by ODO section or the other which was the most puwerf ul at the time. We had 12 millions starving; five millions going to death and degradation in the bottomless pit of poverty and wretchedness, a wretchedness that no man could picture, however lurid his language. We were beginning to realize that all was not well. "Why these inventions; why the energy and skill; why the cry of th.l children and the homes ; why bring children into the world with the halter of drudgery round their necks; well-knowing that they and their children's children will be in the same position as they are to-day if things ware not altered" ? We saw now that the vampires who were taking the life's blood out of us must be removed, and we were massing our forces to remove them. (Applause.) Councillor STOTT in moving a vote of thanks to the chairman remarked that Mr.Clynes was as yet not placed on any constituency 33 a labour candidate and he believed they might be able to get him to come to Warrington as a labour candidate. He was sure they could not have a better_ man.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. PODMORE jun., seconded. In Warrington he said they wanted a strong Labour Representation Com- mittee and wanted the workingmen to rally round them. Mr. J. SEDDON, prospective Labour candidate for Newton supported the motion which was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>ThE BIATALL.</p>
