        <p>cleverly to the far left corner, where Boardman and Cook were in attendance. They waited calmly till the wing man put them on side and then Boardman dropped on the ball to the accompaniment of a yell of satis- faction from the Warrington men. Preston tried for goal, but missed. With a five point lead Warrington seemed masters of the situation and were soon attack- ing again. Keighley responded gamely and Hallam was cheered to the echo for a brilliant clearance. Jagger tried a drop at goal but was yards off the mark. Davies punted back to the home quarter where Berry tackled Jagger and a fine round of passing by the Warrington backs looked like ending in a score, but the narrowness of the ground gave Fish no chance to get through. Half- time : Warrington 5 points, Keighley nil. On resuming, Hallam made a poor return to the kick from centre, and Bradley marked in good position. Myers placed for goal, but failed to judge the angle accurately. Keighley remained on the aggressive, and Walker tried a penalty shot but was no more successful than his captain. Fish was fouled in relieving, and the free kick to Warrington put them on the attack. The forwards dribbled over the line, and Brooks racing up grounded fairly, but his claim for a try was disallowed. Keighley worked their way back again, and Myers tried his short cross-punting to no avail, Hallam being always equal to the emergency. Warrington soon drove the home men to the line, and from a scrummage near touch Davies got hold and dropped a fine goal. Warrington appeared to slacken their efforts after this, and Keighley attacked desperately. Isherwood was tackled over the goal-line and passed back to Fish, who raced round the home backs to centre. Warrington got possession and passing between Brooks, Davies, and Dickenson should have ended in a score but Dickenson threw the ball over Fish's head. Keighley made another assault on the Warrington line, and Thomas came out to assist in the tackling, which was sufficient to keep the aggressors out. Time came with the ball in the Warrington quarter, but with victory safely secured. Warrington, 2 goals, 1 try (7 points); Keighley, nil. The proceeds amounted to £256 Os. 4d., which con- stitutes a record for the Lawkholme enclosure. The previous best gate was on the occasion of thd match against Broughton Rangers in 1902, when the takings were a little over £lBO. WAKEFIELD v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>This re-played cup tie took place at Wakefield on Wednesday. before 12,000 spectators. Bennett scored for Trinity after a rim by Newbould, ani Measor added the goal. In the second half Newbould drdpped a splendid goal. It was a fine game throughout, the for- wards on both sides being good, while the backs found touch with judgment. Wakefield, two goals and one try (7 points) ; Huddersfield, nothing. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WIGAN v. LEIGII.</head>
        <p>WIGAN v. LEIGII.</p>
        <p>There were 10,000 spectators present at this first round replayed tie at Central Park, Wigan, on Wednesday. The game was finely contested, and was mainly confined tto the forwards. At half-time neither side had scored. The game in the second portion went all in favour of , Wigan, who proved the better side, their passing and tackling being one of the features of the closing stages. , Trezise scored a try, which Mason converted. Wigan, one goal and one try (5 points) ; Leigh, nothing. Wigan , now meet Halifax in the second round on Saturday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Widnes had got together the strongest team thex have had for some time to cross the Mersey to Runcorn, and the Cestrians were weakened by changes. The veteran Farmer, after a whole season's rest, was reintroduced to the team. Widnes were superior in the packs, in tackling, and in passing, and almost throughout the first half they were carrying ,Nut attacking movements that taxed Runcorn's utmost resources to stop. Lally opened the score with a very clever dropped goal, and the same player was quickly credited with a try, A. Taylor adding the goal points. Jolley improved Runcorn's position by a nice dropped goal. Not another point was scored, however, in the second half. Final :—Widnes, 2 goals 1 try (7 points) ; Runcorn, 1 goal (2 points.) WIDNES v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>These teams met on Wednesday to replay their post- poned fixture at Widnes. About 2,000 spectators were present. The Rangers were at their full strength, but Widnes had four important absentees. The Range's from the outset were by far the better team, and had matters mostly their own way. Wilson scored two tries.— Broughton Rangers, 2 tries (6 points) ; Widnes, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>CHESHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>„</p>
        <p>ROBERTSONS V. CASTNER. KELLNER.—At Latchford on Saturday last the home team kicked off, and the visitors immediately took play to the home half, and after a few minutes play scored at the corner. The goal kick failed. The home team then roused up, and a little later from a good rush half the length of the field, Warburton scored, Ashurst placing the goal. Half-time Robertsons 5 points, Castner Kellner 3 points. No further score was added in the second half,the homester having the best of the game,the result being Robertson's 5 points, Castner Kellner, 3 points.</p>
        <p>II WARRINGTON JUNIOR LEAGUE. ORFORD HORNETS V. WOOLSTON.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Orford on Saturday. Hornets won the toss, and played with the wind and sun. A smart passing movement by the home forwards nearly ended in the right wing getting over. The Hornets continued pressing until Bramhall, the visiting full- back, sent them up the field with a kick that found touch at half-way. A period of mid-field play followed. The Hornets again got on the attack and</p>
