      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Warrington v. Keighley.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Keighley.</p>
        <p>About 6,000 spectators assembled at Wilderspool on Saturday, in the expectation of seeing Warrington triumph easily over Keighley, but the result caused some surprise, the Neighley men playing Warrington to a draw, and being indeed unlucky not to win. The experiments of the home team at half-back were the cause of the poor performance given by Warrington. Pine weather prevailed, and the conditions were ideal. About 800 supporters of the visiting club were present, and the gate money totalled £137 10s. Teams and officials :</p>
        <p>Warrington : Hallam, back ; Fish, Dickenson' Berry, and Harris, threequarter backs ; Thomas and Bate, half backs ; Preston, Shugars, Boardman, Cook, Belton, Jolley, Swift, and Naylor, forwards. Keighley : Walker, back ; Jagger, Crocker, Batesen, and Stacey, threequarter backs; Myers and Craven, half backs : Blades, Bradley, NlcNicholas, Tempest, Wilkinson, Fearnley, Ruby and Pearson, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Oakland, Barnsley ; touch judges, Messrs. J. Edwards. St Helens, and P. Taylor, Leigh. The Keighley men made matters very warm at the outset, and for quite ten minutes Warrington were com- pelled to defend. Myers and Stacey both made bold efforts to cross the line, but were held outside. War- rington eventually took play to the other end, and Bate Making a clever opening, passing between Berry and Dickenson ended in a try to the latter, Preston failing at goal. This was the only score up to half-time. On resuming, Warrington played rather worse than before, the halves and forwards displaying poor form. Keighley were very persistent and a smart run and punt by Stacey Put them on level terms, the wing man being awarded a try at the corner. Myers failed at goal. It the closing stages, Kieghlev looked like scoring again, and got very close to the Warrington line, The home forwards, ho wever, cleared them out by a stupendous effort and the game ended in a draw of one try, 3 points, each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RE-PLAYED TIES.</head>
        <p>THE RE-PLAYED TIES.</p>
        <p>-Warrington Turns the Table on Keighley. As a result of the poor display at Warrington, several changes were made in the team selected to go to lieighley on Tuesday. Bate and Thomas were replaced at half-back by D. Davies and Brooks while Isherwood came in the three•quarter line vice Harris, and Thomas took Belton's place forward. Keighley relied on the same. side as at Warrington, with the exception of Pickles for Pearson in the forwards. The Warrington men left by special train from Bank Quay at 1-15 and Were supported by over 400 followers. Slow progress 'was made on the long journey through Huddersfield, Halifax, and Queensberry, and Keighley was not reached Until 4-25. The kick-off should have been at 4 o'clock but it was 5 minutes past 5 o'clock when Warrington entered the arena. There was an excited crowd of nearly 10,000 people awaiting the contest, and local ,oPinion inclined to the belief that Warrington would "..,c'se. The day was fine and still, and the small ground looked in very good playing order, though its narrow dimensions surprised the visiting spectators. Mr. E. IL Smirk, of Wigan, was the referee. Keighley won the toss end Preston set the ball in notion with a long kick which found touch and the teams had to scrummage at centre. Myers put in a Short kick and the homesters at once rushed down to Hallam who cleared finely. Warrington soon changed the venue of play, butt Myers sent them back. Jagger making a dangerous run on the left only to be check- Mated by the Warrington full back who marked cleverly off a short kick. Warrington, led by D. Davies, trans- ferred play to the home quarter where Isherwood burst through at top speed. When challenged he passed across to Fish who was coming up but could not gather the ball in time to seize a glorious opportunity. Warring- ton attacked hotly, Davies passing to Fish who was upset at the corner. Keighley were eager to clear and Rot penalised for obstruction. Preston took the kick a Yard from touch, and landed a fine goal amid cheers sorn the Warrington supporters. This reverse caused eighley to make desperate efforts, and a severe onslaught on the Warrington goal was repelled by Brooks who marked with a crowd of opponents round hita. The play quickly travelled back to the home half. the speed of the Warrington backs beiug too much for ICeiglaley. Berry secured in a scramble and punted</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>P 1</head>
