      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Examinet. Saturday, March 25, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examinet. Saturday, March 25, 1905.</p>
        <p>(Au 'tiaras Ussuann.J GRANDMOTHER'S DELUSION,</p>
        <p>after a spell of aggression Brown managed to scramble over. A goal resulted. Close on the interval Fogg scored, but the goal-kick was a failure. Half-time : Hornsts, eight points; Woolston, nil. in the second half neither side gained any advantage, and the Hornets won by eight points to nil.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE. A mesting of the Committee of the Northern Union League was held at the Grosvenor Hotel, Manchester, on Tuesday evening, Mr. Joseph Platt (Oldham) presid- ing. The following matches were re-arranged and will take I:ilace• on the dates named. :—Widnes v. Broughton Rangers, March 22; Batley V. Warrington, March 28? The trees were all bare in the big Weak yara. Runcorn v. 4,eeds, March 29; Salford v. Broughton The last few leaves that were not unaer the earth Rangers, April 21; St. Helens v. Leigh, March 30 ; and that were not blown away, lay like brown Hunslet v. Salford, April 2a; \ Vanington A. v. Chadder- mould on the sodden ground. The hills were ton, April 29; Morecambe v. Huddersfield, April 25; hidden with mist ; only the few nearest green Swinton v. Halifax, April 24; Lancaster V. KeighleYi fields stood out vividly around the farm, giving April 21; York v. Huddersfield, April 21; Dewsbury v. it the appearance of an island surrounded by gray Pontefract, April 15; Pontefract v. Dewsbury, April 24. sea. The milk cans rolled backwards and for- W. Ashton. ‘Varrington, match Rochdale Hornets (A) v. Warrington (A) for striking was suspended to March 25 wards on the mossy wall that encircled the bit of garden. A big sheep dog sat within the shelter I of the open door, and occasionally got up and I walked after the servant girl when she came out ' for water or coals. Although it was rain which was coming in such a saturating downpour, the dampness was of that penetrating cold—far worse than frost or snow. It was not an ideal Christ- mas out of doors ; but there was a huge fire in the kitchen. It went right up the chimney with a crackling roar. The kettle hung on a reckon which glittered brilliantly with polish ; and there was the scent of well-baked loaves just tumbled out of their tins. A very bonny woman in a blue I dress and white apron was busy putting mince meat into little blocks of paste ; and her daugh- ter, Tamar, a well grown school girl, was scrap- ing the tins that had held the cakes : while her chubby little brother of four as busier still, eating the scrapings with a tea si cot. Two little girls of about six or seven, were stoning raisins, and eating them at the same time ; and a boy of ten was arranging holly over the dresser and hook case, with loud exclamations of delight, at his own good taste and method of work. The only one who troubled to answer him was the grand- mother who sat in the corner chair. She always looked up when he called out, and said : " Eh, yes, Johnnie, to be sure !" And then she added after a few moments' thought : " Uncle Tom will be rare and pleased to see't." This reference to Uncle Tom was continual, and came in more ways than one. When Mrs. Coates put the mince pies into the oven, and shut the door, and stirred up the fire, the grandmother immediately opened the door and peered in. " Nay, nay, I'se only just to say gotten them in !" said Mrs. Coates. "Let 'ern bide, Granny." " But Torn niver likes 'err, hard baked," said Granny, peering up mysteriously int..) her dough- ter-in-law's face—" bey them cot, lass, afore they're burned." Then the children all smiled at one another, as if it was an old joke, and Mrs. Coates said undli... her breath : " Power Torn ! We'se niver see h;),1 ag,ain !" And when the children hod stirred the Christ- mas pudding, where it stood on the kitchen table in a big yellow bowl, it was Cranny who lied remarked, as she twisted her cheek apron nrounil her withered fingers, " Fetch Toni in ! 0 lie for Uncle Toni ! Let Uncle Toni Lae a stir at it." But no one went to fetch Uncle Toni, only Johnnie said in a rather low voice :—" Uncle Tom's dead, Granny." Granny looked at Johnnie, and began to mys- teriously beckon. The boy came to her side, and she whispered in his ear : " Thee Uncle Tom will be back in t'inorn !" Then she drew away to see the effect of her words. The boy laughed : " You're dreaming, Cranny.'' " Nay, lad, had a letter : lie's writ it his. self ; and he's be back in t'inorn." The boy looked at his sisters, and some signs passed between them. Then he said : " All right, Grimes', shall I wake you early ?" " Aye, aye, lad ; I inaun be oop at t' dawn,. for titer's a lot to do. I Inallll musk' t'spare led - room ready : and lie inaun hey' all t'best feathers in t'lloos, puttee into his mattress. I'se tab' them oot of all your'n, ye lile tykes ! Ye're nob- but youngish and ye can sleep on owt ! But Tom's hail a long journey and lie's be tired—aye, aye, a terrible journey. Whist ! Hearken ! What was that ?" " It is Jerry bringin' t'inilk in," said Sally. lie's putting t'cans into t'elopetone," said Patty. The little boy who was eating the pince of cake rubbed Ids hands on his holland pinaforo, and said solemnly :—" It's me Uncle Toni cooni- in' uop t'lligh pastur' : and he's gotten a big- gish pair of boots on, and it maks' a clinkiii" noise.'' " Aye, aye that's it. nay lad !" said Granny. " God bless my lite Tom ; he's always on elook out for his uncle !" ' " 1 see'd him in bed last night," said Tom. " Whist !" said Tamar, in an ordering vole, " How'd thee noise !" said little Patty. " Be whyat, prythee!" said plump little Salle. and help me to fetch the things out, istead of clutterin' and talking like that ! Pee going t,, get tea ready." " And be coomed like a ghost !" continued Torn. "Because lie was really deadened, Granny : and I was frightened, and I klied, and Sally cooni to bed, and bringed a lamp into t'room, arid awl. I didn't kly." " Will thee whisht or will thee not whisht cried little Sally, putting a tray down will, ii bang. " I'll tell thee mother aboot thee, thou lile had lad." " Mother's in t' dairy, and I'se be tellin' SOlllO more while thou's going," said Torn, putting his linger in his mouth. Happily at this moment the door opened, the farmer entered, and the old woman immediately spoke : " John !".she said, " Is% raining, thinksta ?" " Aye, it's stopped raining," said John, hang- ing up his coat. " Then I'se hap mysel' oop in me cloak, and tale it walk into t" lane.'' "Nay, t' ground's ower damp." " But I'se to meet thee brother Tom. I said that rd gang half way oop t' lane to meet him." And the old body rose unsteadily out of her chars, straightened herself up by bolding on to the back of it, and tried to reach the cloak which hung on a peg. " Nay, nay, now it down !" cried Tamar. " Why, Granny, you said that it was to-morrow Uncle Tom was coming !" "He uebar eooms on to-day," said Torn. " It's always t' next, and when that moms it's t' next again. He's deadened, and he'll !river coorn !" " I wish thou'd be whyat !" cried Sally. " Sit down, mother,' said John, and he begAn to stroke her hand very gently. "It wasn't to- night that Torn wanted thee to meet him—it's ower cowd—and Tom wad be sorry to see thee riskin' it. Sit down." " But I promised Tom, and he's mebbe for gotten t' road. T' lane's been altered, sin those days : it's selfish of thee, John, to think see mooch of ither folks' comfort, and forget Tom ! Tom'll feel t' cowd, poor lad : and it's been a tirrible long journey, and none o' ye giving t' power boy a welcome !" " Tea's ready, father," cried the rosy Sally. ' The round table was covered with a snow white cloth, and laid with cups and saucers, an an ample spread of bread, butter, and hot tea- cake. A large glass jug of thick cream stood close to Granny. Her thoughts took another channel : with a shaky hand she poured a cup full of cream into a blue basin—then very slily opened the cupboard door, and began to push it in—hooking anxiously back to see if she was observed. " Nay, nay, what ista doing that for ?" cried "Don't let her do it, Sally," said the grave Tamar. " Some one will upset it in the cup- board_" Sally put her hand into the cupboard, and got a firm bold of the basin; but Granny still clung to the other side. " Let go of it, Granny." " Thou wards a right gude punishment, thou naughty lass !" " Let go, Granny." Tamer came up and firmly took the basin from both of them. " Tamar," said her grandinother in a low voice. " Prithee listen, while I tells thee sum- mat." Tamar bent her ear with well practised pa- tience. " It's for thee Uncle Tom's supper ! Pop off vvr t, to t' dairy, before all t' folks drink it oop. Fee determined we shall hey' a drop of gads cream, when he cooms in, if he lies nobbut else !" Tamar made signs to the children and carried the cream into the dairy ; where she poured it into another jug and brought it back again. All the children kept looking at Granny and then at the cream. She was muttering to herself, half under her breath :—" They might hue gotten a bit o' fresh meat for him —t' butcher was here in t' morn and she got t' suet : but what's suet after sike a tirrible journey ? He could a' done wi' a plate o' beef ! Ave it's not right, power Tom ! Thar's nobbut 111, care for him now." Granny was again ri• but Mrs. Coates came into the kitchen, diately put a cup of tea into her bands.</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WATSON DYKE (Author of" Craiktreos," ie.)</head>
        <p>WATSON DYKE (Author of" Craiktreos," ie.)</p>
        <p>" Worse than usual," said John. " ! done nowt but ramble sin' I (mom in."</p>
        <p>" They think that Irse daft," said. Granny, " and. they try to deceive me! I knaw that they've brought t' cream back ; and—they think I'se blind 21nd that I can't see !" And the old body refused to take anything more to eat ; but sat looking into the fire, and saying things in a low, plaintive voice. These scenes often omared ; and the children had got so used to them that they were not de- pressed by them. Perhaps to-night it troubled them more than usual because it was Christmas Eve. Tamar thought that she would change the conversation :—" T'- Wesleyans will be coomin' round wi' t' organ, singing hymns 1" she said. "If ye nobbut gang into t' dairy, ye can hearken to them through t' winder. They're singin' at Foss Gate Farm."</p>
        <p>; " And it's a bad night for them," said John. " It's that raw and cowd ; and znizzlin' a nasty kind of wet rain—it fairly mak's a body creep." The old body freshened up. " Ist Kenorms Eve Pt she asked. "My certy ! I'se glad to bear't ! Tom'll tnebbe coom along •with them : and it'll be a bit o' coompauy : and ' he'll bear all t'dale noos —who's to be married, land who has gotten laid under t'sod—aye, it's a wonder l'gude Lord besn't thought fit to a' I puttin' me into t'kirk garth lang sin' ! But He's spared me to see Tom,—aye, aye, He bath not I dealt wi' oos according to our sins nor rewarded ! oos according to our iniquities.' That's a grand ; text now, and it cooms oot t'buke o' t' psalms. I Fetch it down, Tamar lass, and read it to thee grandmother." Tamar gave a weary little sigh ; and looked wistfully towards the back kitchen where the I little ones had trouped. John stroked his oldest 1 daughter's golden head, but said nothing. She immediately smiled into his face, and going for the Bible brought it to her grandmother, and read it aloud. The kitchen was now only lit by ; the fire. It was quite dark outside mil dl tie I family were on the doorstep, listening for the I singers. Tamar's grandmother thew her close to her, and murmured in her ear :—" Be gude and whyat, lass ; and git my cloak frae t' wall, and pop it on—and t'bounet and all : and we'se gang down elane to meet thee Uncle Toni. It's time lie finished that tirrible journey ; lies been ower laug at that job !" I Tamar's young fingers closed warmly around her grandmother's :—" But, Granny," she said, I " Uncle Tom's dead, you know." I • Nay, lass, not he !" " It's nineteen years since Uncle Tom Jest</p>
        <p>wrote to us," said Tiirnar. "He must be dead."</p>
        <p>" Aye, that's what they all say. They like to try and maddle me, Tamar : but rse not to be waddled.. Gie me t'eloak, and be whyat The old woman rose to her feet. She bad never been so bad to manage since Tamar Lad begun to look after her.</p>
        <p>" Granny, wouldn't you like to see thr3 goose ?" she said. " It's big enough for Uncle Tom to have some,"— she added this in a thoughtful voice. The old woman attentively, and then said :</p>
        <p>" I'd like to see' t, lass : and thou mann mind and say nowt, if tlia' sees 'tic bits under ury apron in t' morn. l'so been it for thee Uncle Tom, in ease he's ower late ; and then they'll leave him nowt but bones, for they're a greedy lot !" " 0, hush, granny." " Aye, it's truth . They fiver troubles aboot power Toni oomnin' in frae slice a tirrible long journey : I feels wearied o' thinkin' o't 1"</p>
        <p>amar led Granny into ihe big cold dairy, where the fat goose bang from a string. " Isn't it a big one ?" she said. " Aye, aye," said Granny, " and let's hey' a taste o' cheese. Gie me a bite o' cheese, Tamar." " Nay, Granny, you're better without it."</p>
        <p>"".:1 Y'v.ant a bite, nobbut a bite! Tha grudges me a bit o' cheese. It's not nice o' thee, my lass. I raver grudged a body a bite of nowt !" Tamar brought the cheese on to the dairy table, and taking a knife broke a tiny morsel off, which she put into Granny's hand. Granny immediately laid it on a plate, and odd —" Niver let on to thee mother ! Pop some more cheese on to the plate, and we'se get it put. ten away for thee Uncle Tom !" " 0, Granny, why are you always doing these things !" cried Tamar, with tears in her eyes. " None o' thee naughtiness !" cried Granny, peering anxiously around. " I'll tell thee what, pop off wi' t' plate o' cheese and put it behind t'cliest o' drawers i' my room, before thee mother spies oos oot !" " Then if I do, you must come to bed I" said Lunar, eager to strike a bargain. " All right, my lass," said Granny, waving her arm. "but let's hey a look at those dishes first. What's i' that big bag, Tamar ?" " It's t'Christinas pudding.'' " Ist ? Pull it down, and let's pop a bit on to a plate for thee uncle Tom. Be sharp, lass, I think 'at I hear thee mother's step." " spoil the puddin".' " Not it ! Fetch it down, and pick t'middle out, mid lay it on a big saucer for thee Uncle Tom, and do it when I bids thee. What's in that jug, Tamar ?" " It's celery," said the girl. " Oh, do come away, Granny—you'll be starved to death." " Starved! I'm as warm as toast. Pick cot t'white inside bits, them lile tender pieces, and lay them on t'plate by t'cheese. He's fond o' celery is Tom ; and it's been a tirrible long jour- ney." "Oh, Granny, do come! I'll have to call mother." " Whist / whist! And keep howd o' that cheese. Is thar ony mince-pies ? Aye, I see some mince pies !" And Granny's hands imme- diately took hold of one and laid it in her apron pocket. Then another and another, and another, with sly cunning glances towards the door. "Eh, granny," said Tamar, "you're an awful woman to do with !"</p>
        <p>Granny took little notice of such observations ; but put a queenscake in her apron, and snatched up another piece of cheese. " Now, l'se gang to bed," she said. " Gae first, Tamer, and keep thee mother off ray tracks. She's that greedy she'll hey me put 'em all back nglain</p>
        <p>Tamar led the way, carrying the blinking little candle very carefully up the staircase_ Granny's bedroom was over the kitchen, on account of the warmth. Her bed was covered with a checked counterpane. which looked rather liko a draught- boar.l. She was chuckling to herself, and pull. jug out her treasures. There was a baby's, wooden cradle. She laid her things in it, cover- ing them with paper, and then with a little blan- ket. Tamar undressed her, and then felt that the old body's hands were icy cold :—" Oh, Granny, you are starved !" " Nay, lass, not a bit ! Mind tha doesn't let on to thee mother aboot these things. Tell her that I'se as warm as a toast ; and that she needn't come to tuck me U. Aye, tell her that, my lass, tell her that !•' Tamar fetched her grandmother a basin of hot gruel, and then with a promise to keep the visit to the dairy a profound secret, she went off to join the others on the flags. The singers were just coming through the gates. It was bitterly cold. The wind was moaning and sighing, as though there might yet be a snowstorm : now and then a few dead leaves whirled up into the air, and went rustling along the walls with a dry crackling noise. Yet nothing could be felt In t a few sharp drops of cold water, which came and went withi. the occasional gusts of wind. The singers drew near to the warm lights of the kit- elle., and shook themselves, and began to stamp the ground. " A merry Christmas to you all," said John. "Aye, John, and to you," said someone. 44 Hoo's t'owd body? Is she middlin', eh ?" t, Tell her to hap herser oop in a shawl, and coom and hear oos sing." " Nay, she's niver done that, Jack, sin' Tom stopped writing I" "Power thing She'll niver see Tom on this side of eternity. Fetch her oot, Tamar, lass : it'll do her gude. ' "Nay," said Tamar very decidedly. "I se putten her to bed: and I shall hae none o' ye disturbin' her." And as Tamar was the most important being, on the question of her grandmother, no one de- murred—the organ began to play, and the Wes- leyan to sing. The kitchen was left deserted. The warm firelight flickered across the thick hearthrug, and lit up all the bright things on the wall. The big clock ticked steadily on amidst the glad voices of the singers. The cat buried itself more deeply into the rug, and looked rather suspicioqsly up at the windows every time the wind brought a few rain drops. ~.By and by the singers closed the organ and, were beard to retrace their steps. The servant girl came rushing in for caps and shawls, and the whole family followed their friends into the lane, shouting merrily as they went. And now the figure of a man came quickly from the shadow of a barn door, and entered the garden. He stood for a moment with a dazed look, as if trying to recollect something. Then, very gently, he lifted the latch, and went in. Again he stood in silent perplexity, and again roused himself. This time he walked into the kitchen. and took hie stand on the bearthrtm A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>HIGHER EDUCATION RATES.</head>
        <p>HIGHER EDUCATION RATES.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Anxious to go Beyond the</p>
        <p>Second Limit.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Education Committee having asked th Board of Education for sanction for an increase over a 2d. rate for secondary education, the Board's reply was read at Monday's meeting. .</p>
        <p>It is stated that ihe Board could not give the County Council a general power of rating beyond 2d. in the 2. They could only consent to the fixing of a definite rate in excess of 2d. "It is not explained why the County Council, with their existing powers of rating and aided by the grant from the Customs and Exercise duties, should not be able to arrange for the gradual de- velopment of secondary schools in the county. The operations of the local education authority in the sphere of higher education should be conducted with due regard to the burden which they will impose upon the ratepayers, and the Board consider that before acceding to an increase in the rating powers, full information should be furnished of the necessity of the step, and a local inquiry should take place." Dr. Hodgson (chairman of the Higher Education Committee) asked why county boroughs should have unlimitecrpowers of rating for secondary education and county Councils no power to go beyond 2d: He pro- tested in the strongest way against the comment in the Board's letter about "having due regard to the burden which it will impose on the ratepayers." Heaven help the committee when they had to levy an unlimited rate ; for elementary education which was not worth the ! money, and yet they were prevented from laying a rate for education which was worth the money. (Hear, hear.) MAR. T. Richardson (Chester) said the rates were heavily pressing upon the ratepayers of the county. Only two counties had reached a 2d. rate—Cheshire and Glamorgan; many others spent only a penny in the 2 ! on higher education, and some only a halfpenny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>STOMACH AND LIVER DISEASES.</head>
