      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DANGERS DANGERS OF SANKEY STATION</head>
        <p>THE DANGERS DANGERS OF SANKEY STATION</p>
        <p>A Further Request to the Railway Company. •</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Committee has not yet seen its way to introduce new booking arrangements at Sankey Station to obviate the danger attendant upon the system which most people at present adopt of crossing the metals in order to reach the Warrington platform after booking. The matter was again strongly com- mented upon at the meeting of Great Sankey Parish L'ouncil held on Monday evening and at the subsequent annual parish meeting. Mr. G. Thompson presided over the deliberations of the Parish Council and there were also present :—Messrs. W. Taylor, A. Birch, H. Pair- clouch, G. Higgs, and J. Smith (clerk). The Clerk read a letter from Mr. Pinnion, the general manager for the Cheshire Lines Committee, in reply to a communication with regard to the unsatisfactory booking arrangements. He said the matter had been reconsidered, but it appeared to him that while he would like to meet the wishes of the Council in the matter, he regretted that he could not see any justifica- tion in recommendinz his committee to iincur the expense which would be involved in carrying cut the suggestions which had been submitted, having regard to the volume of traffic dealt with at Sankey. The Chairman considered the letter most unsatisface tory. The more he thought of Sankey Station and th more he was afraid of a serious accident. It was for the Parish Council to decide whether they would write again to Mr. Pinnion or whether they would communi- cate with the Board of Trade requesting them to take the matter up. Either a sub-way should be placed on the station or else another booking office should be placed on the opposite side to the present one. Mr. Birch remarked that it was a most dangerous place as the express trains came round the curve at such a quick rate. On the suggestion of the Chairman it was decided to refer the matter to the parish meeting to be subsequestly held.</p>
        <p>At the Parish meeting the Chairman again mentioned the matter. The company, he said, had no bridge of their own to cross the line and it was much too far to cross by the road bridge. He moved that the Clerk send a further tter to Mr. Pinnion to inform him that the letter,was most unsatisfactoryand that unless some- thing was done to avoid the danger of an accident on the line the Council would approach the Board of Trade.</p>
        <p>Mr. Higgs seconded and said that as a proof of the danger which existed a number of accidents had occurred.</p>
        <p>The resolution was passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>Sr. HILDA'S V. LATCHFORD BAPTISTS (IST TEAMS.) —Played on Monday at St. Hilda's. Score :</p>
        <p>- , ST. HILDA'S. LATCHFORD BAPTISTS. A. Tulberg J. Grounds H. Hammond 75 E. Cliffe 7O E. Leigh H. Critehley Griffiths Tonge Roberts - Total 540 Total 451</p>
        <p>Majority for St. Hilda's 79.</p>
        <p>As far as Cornwall is concerned the cry is "still they come." The visitors of 1904 have, like the swallows, returlied in 1905 and they have brought with them not only their" cousins and their aunts," but their friends and neighbours. If Cornwall is agreeable in the dark days of November aud December, it is eauallv so in March and April, while throughout the whole summer it is thronged by appreciative holiday-makers. During the dreariest portion of the now expiring winter Cornwall has been blessed by almost continuous sunshine and thousands have found on her beautiful Riviera at a minimum of trouble, expense and fatigue, those com- forts until quite lately erroneously supposed to be only attainable ln the South of Prance and Italy, or, in Egypt and the North African littoral. The Groat Western Railway Company, who have done so much to popularise Cornwall, will gladly send upon application a beautifully illustrated booklet descriptive of the of the attractions of the Duchy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>minute later he went to a cupboard, and putted out a candlestick, as if he had not to look for it. He lit it by the fire and then looked anxiously towards the corner chair. "They have all gone," he said, half aloud. "My mother is dead. He gave a long deep sigh, and went to the window. He lifted the cushion from the broad seat, and looked underneath. "It is not there," he said. "As far back as I can remember she put her apron there, before she went upstairs. I am too late; I have been too slow in my love." Still hope dies slowly, and often takes fresh breath when we have thought her gone. He turned swiftly to the dresser door, opened it, and looked in. Yes, there was one of her caps. He knew the funny little black net hood, set in with black beads, and shiny black ribbon. Yes, and he knew that purple crushed flower—perfectly well. He took it up with trembling fingers, and then it went back again with a sudden drop :—" She does not want it now," he said, shutting the drawer. " And—and lhave returned too late." He heard the voices of the family, evidently returning and a spasm of pain crossed his face. He could not enjoy the thought of facing them. There is something- cruel in returning home to find things the same, in so many unimportant little details, and changed in a way that strikes to the roots, in some vital matter. He felt un- fitted to face the family. The door opened, and they all trooped in. There, on the hearthstone, stood an unknown man, with knit brows and deep set eyes ; his face buint with the sun ; his beard, long, jagged, and turning gray. Yet John knew him instantly. "Tom," he said. " Yes, Tom," said the man, with an intense quietness which frightened the children. " It's twenty years sin' thin last writ to us, Tom."</p>
        <p>‘,‘ Twenty," said. TOM. "IL sounds a long time,</p>
        <p>, but it was gone directly. Life's short altogether, and while we're planning for it, we're going through with it. And are you all well ?" " All well ? Yes, thank God. But thou looks tired and nobbut weak. Did thou walk from t' btat ion ?"</p>
        <p>" You're all well, you say F Then, you're prosperous and satisfied. And how's the farm doing F" " Fair and off ! It's right eneuf ! We'se not shovelling t' gowd, but—" "And you're all well you say ? All ?" " Aye, aye ; thar's my wife just gone out—" " All well—except—perhaps—it isn't to be ex- pected, of course, that the old mother—could pull through—a hard winter." " Aye," said John. " I was just going to talk aboot her. She's just, gone to bed this half hour back : she was talkin' aboot thee all Cnight." Tamar had gone as white as a sheet. She non came forward, and put her hand into her uncle's. It was a real hand—there was no ghost about this.</p>
        <p>" Let us go silently upstairs, for I feel I must look at her," said Tom.</p>
        <p>" Nay, it down !" said John. " It'll scare t'owd body ower much, and she can't bear a deal : Mary will be back directly : and then, efter supper, she'll mebbe tak' thee by t'door—"</p>
        <p>, - But at this moment there was a strange noise on the hall matting ; and there, in the doorway, stood Granny with a long fawn shawl straggling behind her, and two withered hands upraised in profound joy; though the wanderer's return was not a shocking surprise, but the glad fulfilment of expectation. Tom rushed to her, and folded her into his arms. The old body began to sob and cry and feel his hands, as if to see that they were the hands of her own Jacob. Mrs. Coates lifted the latch, and came bustling in. " A merry Christmas ! ' cried the children "For Uncle Tom's come home; and Granny's come downstairs to see him." " Tamar, my lass I" said. Grandmother. "I roomed down because tba'd forgotten to pop t'apron under t'winder seat, sae mind tha does it ! And now, be quick, my lass, oop. wi' thee, as fast as tha can step it ; and down wi' t' cream and ecelery and feheese ! Under t' baby's blanket, Tamar, and be spry shoot it ! It's been sike a long journey • and he ninon be starved for automat to eat. Eli! it was a blessing and a miracle that I popped off wi' a few things. Folks is that carless, they'd hey bad nowt for him, if it hadn't been for me. Tamar, my lass, be sharp aboot it—it's time he got a bite o' sioninat ! she is fond lass, she's' going to be all t'day aboot t'job 1"</p>
        <p>Warrington Field Club. At the annual meeting of the Warrington Field Club, held last night week. Mr. Madely presided, Mr. Fairhurst, the borough treasurer was unanimously elected president for the coming year. Mr. A. J. Jolley was again elected hon. secretary, and warmly thanked for his past services. A vote of thanks was also accorded to Mr. May, the retiring president. Mr. W. Mounfieid &amp;hibited a number of sections of timber; also five sets of exotic beetles, specially mounted in double-glass cases. Mr. J. Fuller exhibited and presented to the Museum a leaf of the lattice ' plant ; also as illustrating the development of certain roots and bulbs, specimens of coltsfoot,water fig-wort, and hyacinth. Mr. Kenworthy exhibited a very beauti- ful series of sheets of plants collected and mounted by him last summer. Mr. Howarth read a paper on " A dragon fly's wing." He illustrated his subject with specially prepared diagrams. He also exhibited a number of the insects in both the larval and adult forms:</p>
      </div>
