      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MARCH 25tb. 1905.</p>
        <p>The attitude of the THE GREAT Government, in Wed- "SCUTTLE." nesday night's debate</p>
        <p>is the culmination of the Parliamentary farce.Of course,all:the Minis- terial papers congratulated Mr. Bersoun on Thursday on his refusal to take any further stand on the fiscal question. Mr. AINSWORTH'S resolution was represented as an impertinrnent waste of the public time. Mr. BALFOUR had already said all he had need say, and the Opposition might be left to talk and vote as they pleased. But two aspects of the case were con- veniently overlooked. One is that Mr. BAnFoun's strategy no longer obscures the critical principle of Free Food; and the other is that, while leaving Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S policy in the lurch, he thought to leave his own there, too. For the decision of Government to pay no further attention to fiscal resolutions moved by private members of the Liberal party applies just as much to next Thursday's resolution against Retaliation as it applied on Wednesday night to :Vlr.A.nrswonTß's motion. The Cabinet had to make a bargain with M. CHAMBERLAIN'S supporters. The Tariff Re. former's at their meeting on Tuesday, decided to ' follow Mr. BALFOUR'S lead, but that was only on the understanding that they should be allowed to exercise the same practice next week. They said to Mr. BALFOUR, ill effect, " if you won't defend Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S policy, then we won't trouble to defend yours." The net result of all this diplomacy is that both policies, Preferential Trade and Retaliation, dis- appear from the House of Commons. Why do they disappear ? Simply bocause public opinion is too strong for them. In spite of Mr. BALFOUR'S clever manceuvring, he does not ask the Parliament in which he has a majority to affirm anything in favour either of his fiscal views or of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S. Mr. BALFOUR says, of course, that this is exactly what he wants, because the present Parliament, in his opinion, has nothing to do with either policy. But it has something to do with the existing commercial system; and it can discredit attempts to under- mine the work of the last sixty pears. Mr. BALFOUR remembers perfectly well that when Mr. GLADSTONE proposed to change the statuq of the Irish Church he did not wait for the General Elec- tion, but expounded the principles of his policy to the House of Commons, which could not carry them into effe2t. They became law in the succeeding Parliament, hut their author laid them boldly on the table of the house before he submitted them to the constituencies. This is the course which Mr. BALFOUR is afraid to take, and he is afraid because he has neither the support of public opinion nor the support of an undivided party. His sole object is to keep his Ministry going some. how. A Ministry which presents, as Mr. MORLEY has said, the extraordinary spectacle of the Board of Trade with a Free Trade President and a Protectionist Under-Secretary. The Unionist party is at sixes tind sevens, and it is no secret that the Prime Minister has lost all faith with Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S partisans. Although they hold meetings and profess to approve Mr• BALFOUR'S device of turning his back both on his principals and on theirs, they know that such a a situation discredits him and them in the public eye, and is tantamount to a confession that the fiscal game is up. That is certainly the const ruc- tion which the average elector will put upon Wed- nesday night's proceedings, and upon the deter- mination of the Government to run away from every encounter on the same issue.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee has lost two valuable members by the resignation of Mr. F. Monks and Alderman Pierpoint. Mr. Monks has been chair- man since the authority was constituted and has taken great interest in the work of organising the local educational scheme. For a man of his years, the acceptance of so onerous an office constituted a sacrifice in the public interest of no inconsiderable a nature and one which has been highly appreciated. With regard to Alderman Pierpoint we notice an increasing tendency on his part to plead advancing years and multifarious duties as reasons for relinquishing some of his public work. We must really remind the Alderman that he is not an old man yet, and he cannot be permitted to escape the obligations of a prominent public position yet awhile. There is real service for men of his stamp, and we hope he will re-consider the decision he announced on Monday. If Mr. Monks could also be prevailed upon to resume office it would be a source of gratification, but we are compelled to recognise that he, at any rate, can lay good claim to a peaceful retirement.</p>
        <p>The townspeople will be glad of the assurance that the disappointing nature of the King's Scholarship examination results has received the "earnest consideration" of the School Manage- ment Committee. The successes achieved by local students undoubtedly fell short of the expectations</p>
        <p>of those who had looked for considerable improve- ment under the new system of training. It would appear from the Rev. Morley Stevenson's opportune statement that unforeseen changes in the staff have handicapped the students somewhat, and in addition the general opinion is that the standard of the examination has been raised. Under Mr. Robson's energetic supervision and with the committee's co-operation it is confidently hoped that Warrington will figure more creditably in the lists next year.</p>
        <p>The great Tory meeting has come and gone. The Parr Hall was not crowded and the enthusiasm was not great. The hall was tastefully decorated with banners, mottoes, and Union Jacks, and prominently displayed were the mottoes " One empire fought for by our forefathers" and 1" Britain for the British." How these can be made to harmonise with the fact of the Govern- ment having handed over our South African colonies to alien Jews and Chinamen is a matter still unexplained. The speeches were significant, not only on account of what was said, but still more on account of what was left unsaid. Several questions of great importance now agitating the public mind were altogether ignored. The recent endeavour of the Tory Government to smuggle through some measure of Home Rule for Ireland was not referred to, although this manoeuvre has resulted in the resignation of Mr. Wyndham, the chief secretary, and the practical censure of Sir Antony Mac Donnell, who has not resigned but who " sits tight " as a kind of challenge to the Government to take extreme measures against him if they dare. That sweetest of all subjects—sugar—was left untouched,although the speakers must, of course, b., aware that this is a question that is causing a great deal of serious thought in the homes of the working•classes at the present time. But, as we understand that the great apostle of preferential trading with the colonies is about to visit our town,it may be that this subject was left for him and that when Mr. Chamberlain ccmes amongst us he may be able to explain the glorious advantages of dear sugar for the working people.</p>
        <p>Mr. Boner Law, who is an excellent speaker and a gentleman of considerable ability, was the star of the evening, and if ai.yone could make out a good case for the Government he is the gentle- man to do it. He plunged at once into the ques- tion of preferential trading with the colonies, although he gave us the reassuring declaration that he saw no danger of the Colonies breaking away from us or of a repetition of the mistake which caused the loss of the American colonies—now the United States. But Mr. Bonar Law forgot to tell his audience that the mistaAe which lost us the American colonies—which, by the way, were the grandest we ever possessed—was caused by a Tory Government who insisted upon our American brothers being taxed without giving them any representation. That Tory Government was com- pelling the American people to take from us near tea, just as the present government is now com- pelling the people of this country to take dear sugar. The Bostonians pitched the cargo of taxed tea into the Boston harbour, and thus the revolu- tion commenced. The Tory Government of that day mismanaged that war just as the present Government mismanaged the South African conflict, with the result that we lost the colonies, and if that is the result of interference with colonial management it had better not be repeated.</p>
        <p>The speaker next tried to stir up our imperial enthusiasm. He said that "As regarded imperial sentiment we had our hot fit of enthusiasm while the South African war was raging. A reaction was inevitable. Everyone knew it would come. It had come. We had our cold fit." It is always safe to prophesy after the event. Everyone knew our hot fit was to be followed by a cold fit, but what every one did not know was that when the Government told the electors they were fighting to give the white man a vote in South Africa, they were fooling them. When Mr. Chamberlain told 1 them that it was a miners' war and that the policy was " South Africa for the British " the people were again being gulled. When a war which was commenced with ten millions of money finally cost over 200 millions, the people realizect that the hot fit of enthusiasm had better cease, and the "cold fit," as Mr. Boner Law calls it, was the result of the Government's policy uf mismanagement, deception, and lavish expenditure. It would have been singular indeed, if such results as these had not put a damper upon the public spirit. The wonder 1 is that Mr. Boner Law's" cold fit" did not develop into an apoplectic fit when the nation started paying for the tune the Government had called.</p>
        <p>The issue to be placed before the electors by the Liberal party, added Mr. Law, was "Do you wish your bread to be cheaper or dearer ?" It was, he continued, a simple issue but it was not the issue. The issue was rather "Are you willing, without committing yourself to anything, to have a confer once called together ? " Mr. Chamberlain, at the commencement of his fiscal agitation, told the nation that it would be necessary for us to consent to a scheme of preferential treatment with the colonies, and he also said "If vou give a preference to the colonies you must tax the people's food." Therefore, it is Mr. Chamberlain's issue of taxing the people's food that we propose to put before the electors at the next election, and when Mr. Boner Law tries to obscure the issue by endeavouring to repudiate the real facts, it becomes a matter which he had better settle with Mr. Chamberlain. Mr. Bonar Law admits that it is proposed to put a tax of 2s. on corn. But he says this will not increase the price of food because a tax of is. per quarter on importe,. grain was imposed a short time back and the price of bread did not rise. This is one of the Tory fictions which has been so often repeated, that we had better remind Mr. Boner Law of the statement made by Mr. Ritchie who, when speaking in the House of Commons as Tory Chancellor of the Exchequer, said "it was a very extraordinary thing if two and a half millions should be collected without anybody feeling it. Flour had undoubt- edly risen, and to a larger extent than the amount of the duty. The baker might bear the increase for a short time but either in meal or in malt, either in pries or in quality, he would soon transfer his burden-to the consumer. The baker would certainly con- sider a permanent addition of is. as on a totally different footing from the rise and fall of the market which he was willing for a limited period to bear. The price of bread had undoubtedly risen in certain quarters, in some far more than the amount of the tax. Again, home baking was not an extinct art. It existed in many parts of Lancashire and in other 'districts, and flour having undount- edly risen, it could not be contended' that the consumers in those places did not pay the tax." On this matter we leave Mr. Boner Law to settle his difference with the straight-forward ex- Chancellor of his own Government; but the people who paid the increased price know very well what the additional burden meant for them. It may be that in Mr. Boner Law's household the increased price did not visibly affect the domestic budget, but in a number of homes in Warrington and other large centres the case was entirely different.</p>
        <p>We were further told by Mr. Bonar Law that this country required a market for our manu- factured goods, and Mr. Asquith was criticised for saying that there was not oneof our colonies which gave us an effective entry into its markets. Last year, said Mr. Boner Law, our total export of manufactured goods to our colonies was 42 per cent. and,he asked,was not that an effective entry? But surely Mr. Boner Law must see that that leaves 58 per cent, to go to other countries, and that must be a still more effective entry. Mr. Asquith was right. The colonies, like other pro- tective countries, build up walls of tariffs against our goods. If we are to have a conference, as Mr. Boner Law repeatedly suggested, let us know before-hand what we are going to confer about. We are giving the colonies perfectly open ports. Will they do the same, so that we may go into the conference in something like a position of equality? This point Mr. Bonar Law carefully avoided.</p>
        <p>Next we were treated to 90030 interesting .dbservations re the iron trade, as between this country and Germany. The condition of this industry in Germany was painted ill glowing colours. Incidentally referrin4 to A,cerica, Mr. Boner Law said "It was no use talking about America. She had got so far ahead that it was no use thinking about her a 4 ,LII. Tnat was due to her advantages of raw material which we did not Possess. But what about Germany? Germany had no such advantages. Last year our total pro- duction of iron fell nff. 111 Germany their exports fell, but their total production increased. . . . In Germany the works were kept going in foil time, giving the workmen full employment, -n 1 the surplus came over here." What was the explana- tion? One "theory", said Hr. Law, was that it Was owing to the lower wages in Germany. Low Wages in Germany are by no means a theory— they are an unpleasant reality for the worke there and the Protectionist here. Is it not marvellous that these speakers fail to see howt While lauding the prosperity of a foreign industry, they, in the next sen:ence, give the whole case, away? Trade good in Germany, works going full time—with ihe result that the German workers get low wages. If this supposed good trade in Germany brings low wages and the supposed less trade in Frngland brings higher wages, the workers of this country will have very little difficulty in making up their minds as to whish of the two systems to support. As a matter of fact, this part of Mr. Bonar Law's protective argument was his weakest.</p>
        <p>Germany we were told,despised our fiscal system. "There was not more than one university io Ger- - many" said Mr. Bonar Law " where our system was taught at all. They had got beyond it. They looked down upon it as a stage of development. They had reached another stage, and looked down upon the way in which our system was taught.'' And yet, Mr. Bonar Law knows that our system has placed us in our present position of being the greatest foreign traders of the world, of being the greatest carriers in the world, the greatest ship• builders in the world! Those learned German Professors who look down with disdain upon our stage of development have in their own country as the result of their system, workers underpaid, badly fed, and in a general chronic state of revolt as was evidenced by the last General Election When the great bulk of the workingmen followed the Socialist leaders in trying to bring about an abolition of the very system which Mr. &amp;mar Law * tells us the German philosophers extol so much</p>
        <p>The Borough Member treated us to his views on the alien question and the matter of redistribution. His idea of the necessity for a re-arrangement of electoral areas is evidently not the removal of the 'inequalities which exist in all parts of Great Britain but the disfranchisement of Ireland. He proposes to take away thirty of Ireland's repre- sentatives, and if this is to be his programme on the Irish question in this constituency at the next election there will be another injustice to Ireland 7;vhich local Irishmen will have an opportunity ot immediately avenging by their votes. We protest most strongly against any proposal of this sort being called a scheme of redistribution of electoral areas. We presume our member knows that the number of Irish representatives to be sent to the 'British House of Commons was fixed by the Act of TJnioti, and that any alteration in this respect would be the breaking of that legislative enactine-t. Now we have the so-called -Unionists proposing to ignore the Act upon which the Union was based we see a distinct advance being made by them in the direction of a new order of things which must result in some arrange. ment of Home Rule. If the Irish are to be denied the right of m inaging, their own local affairs, and also deprived of representation in the Imperial Parliament, the Irish people are in for some :strange times.</p>
        <p>Our member also told us that the British empire must live on "empire and liberty." We had been, he said, for hundreds of years the naissioners of liberty and progress. Liberty and progress are • excellent words coming from Tory speakers who have inaugurated and are advocating freedom for the Chinaman to take possession of our colonies, freedom for the alien million- aires to grow rich on cheap labour, free- dom for Irishmen to pay taxes without repre- sentation, freedom for the British people to buy dear food instead of cheap, and progress brought about by the reactionary measures of the present Government, as instanced by the creating of a vested interest for the liquor trade,the taking away of genuine popular control from our educa• tlonal system, and the general stagnation in many of our industries by the enorrnons burden of taxa- tion so thoughtlessly and recklessly placed upon the income of the weekly wage earners.</p>
        <p>The meeting in the Parr Hall has certainly afforded interesting matters for reflection, and we have no doubt that after the visit of Mr. Chamber- who has boldly admitted that 'els programme lavnives the taxinq of the people's foorl, the liberals of Warrington will be as confident of the 18ana as they are eager for the fray. Mr. Chamber- lain has, on a previous occasion, spoken " for " one candidate and brought about the success of the Other. May he do so again, is our pious political Wish!</p>
        <p>SPRING NOVELTIES NOW SHOWING. W. T. WILLIAMS'</p>
        <p>NEW COSTUMERS, MILLINRRY,</p>
        <p>. 111tICSS1fl, COATS, Ize , .te. 4S, SANKEY S-TREET, WARRINGTON. t_..-------- , ..1 , Coming Events. The Rev. H. Dunnico will conduct the service i to-morrow evening in Parr [tall when he will deliver a..sPecial address on "The Church and The Working- -0 casses." Madame Christian will be the soloist. In . t„..,1,t;e morning Mr. Dunnico will preach in the Baptist Church, Golborne-street, and will conduct an infan dedication service at the close. Collection on behalf • Of local Infirmary. • The Rev. It. Wilkins Rees will take the revival lin IVales for his subject at Bewsey Road Wesleyan 'Church to-morrow. An open meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society , . Will be held to-morrow, the Rev. J. Yonge will pre- , ' 14cle, and the speaker will be Mr. W. ',Beecher Smith, '3l Manchester Y.M.C.A. Crosfield's Works Prize lland will give a number of selections during the Proceedings. A concert will be given in the Parr Hall to-night in • zaernory of the late Mr. Percy Randall. An excellent l'-v,ogramme has been arranged, the contributors to ..rfnich will be Mrs. A. Booth, Miss G. Pemberton, Miss --."- Houghton, Miss M. W. Collin, Messrs. J. Rabbitt, ~.; R. Draper, F. Fieldhouse, J. W. Bibby. and R. H. k-rearson, the Apollo Male Quartet and Mr. T. J. Dow , ,- -n a military orchestra. "PREMIER" CYCLES at 9 guineas for Best Grade, U.„..nsurpassed.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55,Bridge st., • Warrington.—Advt. VISIt to Messrs. Monks Hall and Co's Works. ,nv the kind permission of the firm the members of ilbthe uilding Students Association visited the works of Hall and Co., on Saturday afternoon. About students availed themselves of the opportunity of 00 :113..ecting these extensive works. .The party was .`,-'oucted through the various buildings by Mr. Pem- , ct • !!!rton, who explained minutely the various processes .. converting the scrap, and pig iron into wrought °ll. The process of tube and rivet making and fialvanizing were especially ',interesting, as was also 6 ',.!ice bedstead and !mattress factory. The new Mond ‘,... producing plant and its workings excited a deal of Interest. A hearty :vote of thanks to Mr. Pember- 0n , brought a interesting visit to a close. iiini.— , •</p>
