      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Notice to our Readers. On and after Friday, April 7th, the " Examiner " will be published on Friday afternoon at 4 o'clock instead of Saturday morning. Readers will thus be able to have their weekly paper at tea-time on Friday, and the newsagents will be found willing to deliver the " Examiner " alang with the evening papers. The new issue will contaia spa! features. A column of "Notes and notions" will be introduced, together with a " Childrnn's corner" and "Cycling column." The opening chapters of a new serial. The Workhouse Extension.</p>
        <p>- At a ineeting of the Property 0 \vners Association held on Tuesday a re4olution was :passed thank- ing the members of the Beard of Guardians who had supported the amendment, deferring the erection of further buildings in connection wij,h the Warrington Workhouse Infirmary.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASH TON'S LICA TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.—G 3. SAIIKEIC-S'TILEAT, Warrington.—Advt. Another Success for Messrs. To% We are pleased to announce that another success has been achieved by a Warrington Confectioner, Mr. Charles Toft, Bridge-street having again been awarded First Prize, Silver Medal and Diploma for cakes, exhibited in open competition at the Bakers and Confectioners' Exhibition held under the auspices of the Mapchester, Salford and District Master Bakers at the St. James's Hall, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Presentation to the Salvation Army Band-</head>
        <p>Presentation to the Salvation Army Band-</p>
        <p>master.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon Bandmaster Lee was made the recipient of a baton and whistle subscribed by the band, in recognition of his services during the past 14 years. In the evening,. Staff Captain Bali gave a lecture on "Army, social, and rescue work, and eight years experience in the West Indies." The Rev. J. Tonge, acting as chairman, spoke highly of the good work of the army, and an appeal on behalf of the social and rescue work was responded to generously ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGEI-CLASS MINERAL , ATERS.—Advt. Miraculous Escape. _ . - - •</p>
        <p>, Mr. Joseph ITolbr.ook,night inspector on the London and North Western Railway had a miraculous escape on Friday night. After proceeding along the plat- form to go to Arpley new siding, crossed two sets of lines, an up bay and the up main line. Ho avoided the coaches which were being moved, but forgot all about the main line. The 6.52 goods train from Bolton to Warrington was coming down at the time, and struck Mr. Holbrook on the right shoulder. His shoulder was badly damaged and his foot severely cut. He was noticed by a brakesman, and first aid was rendered, after which he was conveyed to the In- firmary.</p>
        <p>Stock Exchange Fever Results in Bankruptcy. Griffiths Summerfield Owen, of Fazakerley, Liver- pool, adm:tted, in the Bankruptcy Court on Thursday, that he had been a fool, but tried to soften the con- fession by suggesting that those who initiated him into the mysteries of " bulls" and " bears " were' something worse for permitting him go to on so long without requiring cover. Owen, who travelled on commission for a Warrington firm, has liabilities of £678, and assets amounting to £lB6. He started speculating with £3O, and attributed his bankruptcy entirely to dealing in shares on the Liverpool market. Opposition to Church Bazaars.</p>
        <p>Speaking at St. Michael's, Burtonwood, on Sunday evening, the vicar (Rev. A. M. Mitchell, M.A.) said be had received piles of letters respecting bazaars and amusements as methods of church finance. With one consent all the writers testified to the fact that what- ever systematic and proportionate giving is substituted for the fancy fair, the tide of financial church pros- perity immediately sets in. He had pleasure in men- tioning that one lady, an entire stranger, had sent ;£5O towards the new church, with '..te remark: "I heartily echo your sentiments with .egard to bazaars for any church purposes; thoy are unworthy of church people, and a disgrace to the age in which we live."</p>
        <p>"PREMIER" CYCLES at 12 guineas with change speed gear &amp;c., unequalled.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Progressive Labour League.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of tlTis .lel,.gue was held at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening, Mr. J. Elwell, jun., presiding. In the unavoidable absence of Councillor .Maginn, who has prornised to speak on the Education Question, an highly interesting address was delivered by Mr. Joseph Howes on "Some aspects of the Irish question." Mr. Howes dealt with the recent disclosures in regard to the Government's Irish policy and also strongly criticised the attitude of Mr. Robert Pierpoint, 112. P., who has expressed himself in favour of the removal of thirty Irish Home Rule members from Parliament. The discussion was taken part in by Mr. P. O'Brien, Mr. E. Plinston, Mr. G. Walker, and others. Next Tuesday Mr. Lloyd Jones will submit a paper on "An eight hours day and the unemployed."</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. Mr. Mathias, the president, occupied the chair at the usual fortnightly meeting of the above on Tuesday night last when there was a large number of members and friends present to hear one of the members, Mr. G. Smith, jun., read a paper on a "Holiday in the Scilly Isles." The description given of the islands as health resorts and their attractiveness from a photo- graphic standpoint owing to the beautiful rock and other scenery proved interesting and instructive. The lecturer also dealt with the great and growing indus- try of the islanders—the cultivation of bulb flowers, many of the beautiful slides shown being a record of the extraordinary extent of their production. Mr. Smith was heartily thanked for his lecture and the President expressed the hope that some of the other members would follow his example in passing on some of the pleasure obtained from their outings with the camera. The members were reminded of the forthcoming Exhibition with a view to the prepara- tion of their work, the awards for which will take the form of a specially designed plaque.</p>
        <p>ASK for CTIN. NINGIIAM'S HIGII-CLASb WINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Warrington and Knutsford Light Railway</head>
        <p>The Warrington and Knutsford Light Railway</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 25, 1905</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Scheme.</head>
        <p>Scheme.</p>
        <p>Speaking at the annual parish meeting for Thelwall Mr. Hatton referred to the withdrawal of the scheme for the construction of a light railway from Warring- ton to Knutsford. He had, he said, met the engineer of the scheme that day (Monday), and he told him that the case was going to be brought forward again as they had in view an alternative proposal regarding ClifTe-lane bridge. They proposed to build a steel bridge of their own over the canal but probably the owners of the canal would have a good deal to say about the matter and oppose any such proposal. Continuing, the Chairman said that the same people were the promoters of the Wallington and Northwich Light Railway and at the second inquiry the order was passed in that case. That ens obtained two years ago and not a single step had been taken towards making the railway. The real fact was, that those gentlemen who went about trying to get orders for light railways to be made, never had any intention of making them. Their object was to form a company, and persuade the public to buy the shares. When the scheme was brought forward again the Parish Council would give the subject their consideration, and do what they could to protect the interests of the ratepayers of the parish.</p>
        <p>" PREMIER " CYCLES at 7 guineas startling value.— J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.— Advt. Photography of Flying Bullets.</p>
        <p>- - - - - At a meeting of the St. John's Literary and Social Society, held in St. John's Schoolroom on Monday evening last, the Hon. President, the Rev. J. L. Milne, 8.D., in the chair, Mr. R. H. Albery, M.Sc. Physics Master at the Warrington Technical Institute, gave a lecture on the above subject. In opening the preceedings the chairman said Mr. Albery required no introduction to the members of the society ; they had had the pleasure of listening to him on two previous occasions, and he was consequently well known to them. Mr. Albery, who had an enthusiastic reception explained very graphically the methods used in taking photographs of moving objects, illustrating his remarks by means of a revolving disc and electric flashes in a manner which made the subject intelligible and interesting to the most unscientific listener. Photographs of moving objects taken with cameras and rapid shutters were first ihrown on the screen,and afterwards the complicated and delicate appliances used in the photographing of bullets in their flight were dealt with by the lecturer,and actual photographs of rifle bullets and small shot, as they travelled through space, were shown by means of the lantern, all being fully described by Mr. Albery. On the motion of Mr. W. E. Rees, Vice-president, the ecturer was accorded a hearty vote of thanks for his ectu re.</p>
        <p>-..</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE</p>
        <p>2. Women's Chat and Fashions. Labour Demonstration. Runcorn Rural Council and • Board of Guardians. Science Notes. 3. Football : Replayed Ties. "Grandmother's Delusion" by Watson Dyke. Sankey Station Dangers. G. Education Committee. A. Wife's Dependency: Peculiar Compensation Claim. "Earth bound," by John Strange Winter. Motor Notes, 7. Our Local Churches. Cricket Club Fixtures. Billiards. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Personal. At Ormskirk Parish Church on Sunday, the Bishop of Liverpool ordained Mr. John Morgan Price, BA , of St. David's College, Larnpeter, to the office. of deacon, and licensed him to the curacy of Holy Trinity, Warrington. Mr. J. N. Hartley, who since 1898 has served the Inland Revenue at Warrington as second class collector has been appointed first class collector at the Belfast office. He will be succeeded. by Mr. G. S. Sterling who has been serving as an inspector ' at Sonierset House, London. Mr. Walter W. Longdin, son of the Borough Sur- veyor has arrived at Kangoon, India, and taken up the position of civil engineer to the municipality. An interesting biographical sketch together with a , photograph of Mr. F. Podmore, a local musician, appeared in a recent issue of " The Bandsman." ' Alderman Sutton is still confined to his room with a severe attack of sciatica. He is in a precarious state of health, but a specialist who visited him on Saturday holds out hopes of his ultimate, though tedious recovery. Mr. Roger C. Parr, Grappenhall Hayes, has been appointed sheriff for the County of Herefordshire</p>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. A. Naylor and his Supporters. ' Mr. R. A. Naylor, in opening the new Conservative Clubroom at Glanadda, Bangor, on Saturday, said that if only Bangor Conservatives would unite there there would be a friendly instead of an adverse majority for Conservatives in the division at the next election. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington Church Society. The series of services to men only, given in the Parish Church under the auspices of the above Society, was continued yesterday evening, when the Rev. J. H. Thorpe (vicar of St. George's, Stockport) spoke on " The teaching of the Christ : Never man spake like this Man." Next Friday night the Ven. Archdeacon Madden will speak on "The work of the Christ : He went about doing good."</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY ST., WARRINGTON.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Hospital Sunday. To-morrow is Hospital Sunday and collections will be made in the various places of worship on behalf of the funds of the Infirmary and dispensary. We would once more commend this institution to the generous support of the congregations as help was never more urgently needed than at present. The report of the proceedings at the annual meeting of the governors, published in another column, affords convincing details of the work of the Infirmary. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Billiards.—lst V.B. South Lanc. Regt. Officers v. Sergeants.—This match was played at the Drill Hall, Warrington, last Tuesday, and resulted in a win for the officers by 120 points. Score : Captain R. Smith 2OO Col-Sergt.T.Jolley... 138, Capt. A. F. Bundock 200 Col-Sergt. W. Wood 128 Lieut. E.E.Ridgway lOO Q.M. Sergt. Wilcock 61 Capt. G. R. Crosfield 100 Sergt.-Major Ingram 60 Lieut.R. McM. Roberts 72 Sergt.-Inst.Allardice 100 Capt. P. N. Avery Major B. Fairclough 70 Sergt. T. Knight ... 100 Capt. H. G. Roberts OB Sergt. F. Goulden... 100 787</p>
        <p>007</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>"ACROSS THE ROOF OF FRANCE.'</head>
        <p>"ACROSS THE ROOF OF FRANCE.'</p>
        <p>Mr. Hubert Congreve's Interesting</p>
        <p>Lecture. A delightful holiday tour, interestingly described and beautifully pictured, was the impression left on one's mind by Mr. Hubert Congreve's lecture—" Across the roof of France "—delivered in the Bold-street Wesleyan schools on Tuesday evenin,g before a large audience. Last summer it was Mr. and Mrs. Congreve's good fortune to undertake a cycling tour through Auvergne, the southern central district of France, on which the above-quoted picturesque title has been bestowed by an enthusiastic admirer. Mr. Congreve is a skilful devotee of the camera, and he was able to possess himself of over a hundred excellent views of the romantic country which his journey embraced. These, transferred to lantern slides, served to illustrate the lecture and gave manifest pleasure to the assembly. As a lecturer. Mr. Congreve has a very taking style, and his vivid descriptions of the changing scenes unfolded to him during his visit to Auvergne were interspersed with racy anecdotes. Auvergne, Mr. Congreve explained, was a very mountainous and volcanic district, and in every feature it was more strongly marked than any other part of the country. The inhabitants were Celts of the Celts, that was decendants of the old original Gallic inhabitants of France. The scenery was purely volcanic,and the recently extinguished craters were characteristic features of the country, which were not found in any other part. There were great districts absolutely bare and covered still with stones, ashes and other projectiles from the old volcanoes. Hot and effervescent springs were to be seen, and generally the country had the appearance of having gone solid suddenly while on the bubble. The architecture, too, was quite distinct from that of any I other province of France, and he knew of no other dis- trict where four great churches on entirely similar lines to those he had visited in Auvergne were to be found. They had a perfection in plan and detail which classed them among perfected styles of Romanesque architec- ture.</p>
        <p>The audience was then conducted, in imagination of course, through Auvergne, and the charming scenery depicted on the screen was quite a revelation. Castles and villages perched on rocks which rise to a tremendous height, magnificently-built churches, and beautiful winding rivers were some of the most striking features. In some places the volcanic eruptions have produced fantastic effects and there is a quaintness about the towns and villages which is evidence of their antiquity. Incidentally ,14Mr. Congreve put in a plea for the nationalization of canals. He pointed out with what beneficial results the system had been attended in Prance and said that if English canals could be national- ized and made of uniform size he thought it would be a very great advantage to our trade. (Bear, hear.) The proceeds of the lecture were in aid of a most deserving object—the Silver-street Mission—which is doing a good work in a poor and thickly-populated neighbourhood. Mr. James Dodds, who presided, read some interesting particulars forwarded by Mr. R. Wood, an active mission worker. The present, it was stated, was the fourth winter session, and it had been decided to continue the services throughout the summer months with a view to making the work a permanent one. The room was crowded to excess at every service, and the class of people who attended were those who had not been accustomed to go to any place of viorship. It was encouraging to learn that during the last three months about 40 or 50 adult boys and girls had become scholars in the Sunday school, and were showing an ardent desire to learn the truth. The workers were seeing week by week a gradual improvement in the social condition of the men and women who attended the meetings. Mr. Dodds expressed the gratitude of the assembly to Mr. Congreve for his willing help and alluded to his en- thusiasm and skill as a photographer. The Rev. R. WILKINS REFS also spoke of the success which had attended Silver-street Mission and of the valuable services rendered by Mr. Wood and Mr. Parkinson who conducted the services. Ever since the mission started Mr. Dodds had shown deep interest and sympathy with it. At the close of the lecture Mr. Congreve was cordially thanked and a similar compliment was paid to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER BISCIJIT.4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovalist.",</p>
        <p>Cup-tie excitement is growing. Warrington are still in the running and Dame Fortune has drawn them at home for the third time in succession. Visions of the cup coming to Wallington at last are already rising before the disordered imagination of the more ardent enthusiasts, but it has to be recollected that Warrington will not be at home in any of the succeed- ing ties and also that there are one or two better teams still left in the competition. I don't think Warrington will win the cup, though they should at least reach the semi-final. They have so far not shown anything like the form which carried them into the final last April and there will have to be all startling advance ere much confidence can be safely put in the locals.</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact they are fortunate to be still in the Cup Competition. Strange to say, Keighley proved very tough opponents on Saturday and came within an ace of winning outright. As it was they profited by Warrington's weakness to draw the match, and a bit of luck at the close would have given them the victory. I need not say much about the match here. It was talked threadbare during the week-end, and Warrington have since atoned for their poor dis- play. The fault was apparent. Neither Bate nor Thomas rose to the occasion at half back and in fact they crippled the attacking forces of the team to such an extent that Warrington never looked like winning once the visitors had got on level terms. Keighley played a remarkably good game, Myers and Craven at half continually causing Warrington trouble, while the visiting forwards showed more earnestness than the home lot.</p>
        <p>The result of the contest filled the visitors with delight and they made no secret of their confidence in being able to win at Lawkholme-lane, where Salford were victims in Round 1. The feeling in Warring- ton was one of soreness, but the radical changes in the selected fifteen roused the hopes of the " Wire- pullers" once more. If they could not beat a second division club anywhere with full strength up then they ought to be shot, or something equivalent in the extinguishing line. The changes made were D. Davies and Brookes at half-back, Isherwood at centre, and Berry wing for Harris, while Thomas resumed his place with the forwards. It remains now to tell the tale of how Warrington went to Keighley and won.</p>
        <p>There were no less than 400 supporters of the team assembled at Bank Quay at one o'clock on Tuesday, all prepared to make the long journey into Yorkshire. The kick-off was fixed for 4 o'clock and the "special" was due at 3.38, but it crawled and burrowed under- ground to such an extent that 5 o'clock had turned before Warrington stepped on the field. What a round-about journey it was through Huddersfield, Halifax and Queeneberry ! The London and North Western seemed to have gone full tilt at every hill in the last.3o miles and bored through it. I never knew so many tunnels, the eager spectators were plunged into darkness every five minutes in the last hour, and many a match was struck to look for light and other things. Secretary Ashton talked to the engine-driver as if he were a cabby, and I am sure he livened him up at tiu.es. However all things come to a,i end, and Keighley was reached slowly and safely at 4.25. --- Then there was a rush to the ground and what a sight met the gaze of the eager Warringtonians. The small-sized-00 by GO—playing-patch of the Airedale club was full to the gates, but it held 400 more. A tighter-packed crowd never saw, and the Keighley club beat all records by accommodating nearly 10,000 Ispectators. And they were very patient considering the long spell of waiting. The hope of victory, how- ever, reconciles one to many discomforts.</p>
        <p>Warrington soon proved their determination to win once operations had commenced, and Keighley found themselves opposed to quite a different team from that of Saturday. Forwards and backs alike were full of energy and after ten minutes' play it seemed odds on the visitors. Davies and Brooks were particularly smart, and active in opening out the game, and it was the least Warrington deserved when they obtained a penalty goal through Preston after a hot attack. The kick was from near touch, but the famous forward judged the angle to a nicety, and the ball jest fell over the bar. Keighley made great efforts to secure the lead, but were quite outclassed, the defence of Hallam proving altogether too much for them. It was not long before an impetuous rush by Warrington ended in a try, Berry kicking across for Boardman to score far out. There was an element of luck about the point, but no question as to its legitimacy, and at half-time Warrington were " on velvet," so to speak.</p>
        <p>The second half was a repetition of the first. Both sides attacked in turn, but there was more method about Warrington, and the narrowness of the ground alone prevented a couple more tries being gained. The only score was a goal by Davies, who dropped very cleverly from the base of the pack. Keighley were well-beaten at the close, more so than the score .of 7 points to nil indicates.</p>
        <p>Warrington gave a vastly improved display in every respect. The forwards were irresistible at times, and the halves gave the utmost satisfaction. It is a long time since Di Davies showed so much "vim," and he appears to be in fine form just now. Brooks got through a tremendous amount of work and was ' always on the ball. The Keighley pair were quite overshadowed, and neither Myers nor Craven were as prominent as on Saturday. Indeed the latter was quite at sea on more than one occasion. The War- rington threequarters also did well in the limited space at their disposal, Berry alone appearing to dis- advantage, and he has the excuse of being quite out of his place on the wing. Hallam was superb, never making a mistake, and clearing his lines in a fashion which compelled the cheers even of the Keighley supporters.</p>
        <p>On the home side no one did better than Stacey, who is certainly a smart wing. Walker at full back, was decidedly off, and missed finding touch repeatedly. In fact, for once, Warrington distinctly shone in the kicking department. The home forwards worked hard, and punched hard —often enough at an opponent —while the amount of obstruction, especially of Fish, was discreditable. Mr. Smirk, the referee, was not half on the alert or this regrettable feature of present- day football might have been checked so far as this game was concerned. If a player deliberately fouls the man who has kicked the ball, he should be sent off.</p>
        <p>The Wigan and Leigh cup-tie which was ordered to be replayed owing to the ineligibility of one of the Leigh players, came off at Wigan on Wednesday, when the pievious result was reversed, Wigan winning by 5 points to rill. All one's sympathies go to Leigh, who have been particularly unfortunate. It is no credit to Wigan that they remain in the Com- petition after having once been fairly beaten. - -- There was a further sensation yesterday, when it was learned that Leigh had reported Wigan for a breach of the rules in connection with Wednesday's match. The protest was heard yesterday afternoon at Huddersfield, but no decision was come to, the meet- ing being adjourned. It was decided, however, that the Wigan v. Halifax cup-tie fixed for to-day must be postponed pending the result of the inquiry.</p>
        <p>There have been one on two League matches of late, Widnes winning at Runcorn on Saturday and being defeated at home by Broughton Rangers on Tuesday. -- The League table up-to-date is as follows —For— —Against— P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts Pts. Oldham 29-22— 6... 1...39 51-210...23 20-106...45 Bradford 29...20... 7... 2...36 64-264...22 32-140...42 Brou'en Et 28...19 7... 2...30 5(1-228...34 20-128.-40 Hunslet 28...16...11... 1...54 34—'210...26 32-148-33 ILeeds Waregt'n 27...15.. 10... 2...30 35-165...22 25-119...32 Wigan 27 .15...11... 1...26 40-172...28 28-140...31 Salford Widnes Hull K.R 28...13...15... 0...32 34-166...33 37-177...26 'Halifax Hull 27. 10 .13... 4...25 34-152...28 35-161...24 Leigh Swinton 29...10...18... 1...18 31-129_32 35-169.:.21 Wakefield 28... 9...17... 2...32 18-118...29 40-178...20 I &amp;dim 28.. 8...17... 3...30 22-126...39 42-201...19 St. Helens 27... 8...18... 1...31 28-146...41 69-289...17 Runcorn</p>
        <p>c</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts. Wigan 8 6.. 9 0 .I 0 17— 71... 8 7 37... 12 W'gton Leigh Widnes St. Helens 8... 2... 5... 1... 5 9 37... 8 12— 52... 5 Runcorn 7... 1... 6... 0... 7 5 29... 9 13— 58... 2</p>
        <p>Leigh have to go to Wigan on Good Friday in the return League match, and it is reported that their supporters have decided to boycott the engagement as a protest against Wigan's " unsportsmanlike con- duct " over the cup tie.</p>
        <p>With the exception of Wigan and Halifax, all the clubs return to League football to-day, Warrington being at home for the fifth match in succession. Their visitors are Hunslet, whom it is worth while making a special effort to defeat as there is a chance of War- rington climbing to fourth place in the table to-day. Then, too, the teams may yet meet in the Cup tourna- ment. Warrington are relying on Tuesday's team, with the exception that Kenyon displaces Berry.</p>
        <p>The team will be: Hallam, full back; Fish, Isher- wood, Dickinson and Kenyon, three-quarters; D. Davies and Brooks, half backs; Boardman, Preston, Shugars, Thomas, Cook, Jolley, Swift and Naylor, forwards. •</p>
        <p>A cheque for A',2B 4s. 7d. was forwarded to the secretary of the Runcorn Club as the proceeds of the Richardson memorial match, Mr. Abel has since replied expressing his appreciation of the Warrington club's interest in the matter.</p>
        <p>The Works Competition will be in full swing next week, and matches will take place every evening. Thirty-two clubs have entered and it will take until April 17 to get through the first round. From all appearances the Competition is likely to be a very successful one. The draw is as follows :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>FIRST ROUND.</head>
        <p>FIRST ROUND.</p>
        <p>Whits Lead v. Longford 607;Wc;rks, Monday, March 27; Referee. F. Turner. Guardian v. C.L.C., Tuesday, March 28; P. &amp; K., Dallam v. Castle Rubber, Wednes- day, March 29; Crostleld's v. Fletcher Russell, Thursday, March 30; Whitecross v. Bifurcated Rivet Co., Friday, March 31; Tramways v. Cable. Monday, April 3; John- son's v• Corp. Gas and Water, Tuesday, April 4; Robin son's v. Whymans, Wednesday, April 5; Wire Rope v. Greenings, Thursday, April G; Wilderspool v. Locker's, Friday, April 7; Davenport's v. Monks Hall, Monday, April 10; Melbourne's v. Robertson's, Tuesday, April 11; Edelsten's v. P. &amp; K. Bewsey, Wednesday, April 12; Rylancls v. Locos, Thursday, April 13; Post Office v. Longford Wire, Friday, April 14; Walker's v. Naylor's. Monday, April 17. Kick off 6-15. Admission id. and 2d. Season tickets price is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>BY R.G.P. Timperley paid a visit to Grappenhall road last Saturday,and drew with the Stockton Heath Ist eleven. The game was of a very pleasant nature, seldom have the referees been less in evidence than in this match. In the first half the visitors played with the sun at their backs, and were soon pressing the home defence. The goal obtained by Timperley was of a simple nature, as the homefull back was bustled by one of his own side when about to clear the ball, the result being that tke visitors inside right Not through.</p>
        <p>Just before the whistle went for half-time, S. Baxter shot through the best piece of combine play during the game, but was ruled off-side. Upon turning round the home forwards overplayed the Timperley backs, aryl it was not long before W. Richardson made the score level. Following this Timperley played up, and from now till the end of the game, had rather the better of the play, but failed to add to their score, and the final whistle sounded with the score 1-1.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath team showed a great improve- ment on the form shown against Birkenhead Wanderers, especially the full backs, whose tackling and hitting were quite a feature of the game. The home team were lucky to have the services of A. H. Rathbone, of the Latchford club, for he plays a game which adapts itself to the rushing tactics of the rimperley forwards.</p>
        <p>The second team journeyed to Timperley, the eleven being composed of five Stockton Heath members and six players of the Royal Club, and under the circum- stances the defeat of 4-0 was no disgrace. The Stock- ton Heath second string have done very badly of late, and it is to be hoped that some new scheme will be adopted for next season.</p>
        <p>This week the Stockton Heath first eleven receive a visit from Alderley Edge, and it is to be hoped that they will wipe off their defeat at Alderley by 3 to nil. The following will represent Stockton Heath Ist (at home) : R. G. Pierpoint, goal; P. B. Hawtin and E. Pierpoint, backs; G. Hatton, Eric Fairclough and L. Fairclough, halves ; C. H. Bond, Richardson, R. Pierpoint, W. Richardson and S. Baxter, forwards. Bully off 2.45. Referee, N. W. Bellamy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>The resolve of the Cyclists' Touring Club to devote the suns of .ClOO to the purpose of discovering a remedy for side-slip on bicycles is seemingly appreciated by inventors, many of whom have entered their devices Entries close on the 31st inst, Forms of entry may be obtained of the Secretary, 47, Victoria-staeet, West- minster, S.W.</p>
        <p>The spring-like weather of the past week has brought out cyclists from their winter retirement—that is as far as the use of their machines is concerned, Last year's mounts have been polished up, and given a con- tinuance of the present genial conditions, the favourite roads of picturesque Cheshire should soon be resound- ing with the whirr nt wheels. The members of the Warrington Cycling Club—which, t am told, is in a more flourishing state than ever—enter upon what is hoped will be a highly successful season to-day. Quaint old Warburton will, be visited and a large turn-out is confidently expected. Mr. D. Hodge again takes over the captaincy and from past experience the members know that nothing will be lacking on his part to maintain the present gratifying prosperity of the club.</p>
        <p>The energetic secretary (Mr. E. Pickavance) has just issued the club hand-book and special attention is called to the varied and attractive programme. The events of the season will include inter-club runs, Presi- dent's and Ladies' Day, fishing competition, bowling mains and tours and week-end outings to some of the finest resorts in the district. The Easter tour will be to North Wales, at Whitsuntide, Ellesmere and the Vale of Llan- gollen will be visited, while, during Bank Holiday Bux- ton and Derbyshire dales will be the ream. It should be noted that the Tuesday night runs commence on May 2nd, when a good muster is anticipated for a spin to Rainhill. On Saturday, July Bth the club will once more be entertained at Beeston Tower by the esteemed Pres- dent (Mr. John Naylor).</p>
        <p>The committee have secured the use of the bowling green at Sankey Chapel House for the exclusive use of members and their friends on Wednesday afternoons and evenings for the entire season. The first main will commence on Wednesday, May 31st, and members are asked to note the fact that all the roundsand final heats will be played off on the Sankey green, thus giving all competitors an equal chance without interfering with the Tuesday night runs.</p>
        <p>As an additional inducement to members to turn out rsgularly, prizes are offered for attendance —two for Saturday and Tuesday runs collectively, and one for Tuesday runs only. There will also be awards for the successful competitors in the fishing match and bowling mains.</p>
        <p>Thursday evening witnessed the opening smoking concert of the season at the Patten Arms Hotel, and the affair was a great success. Mr. D. Hodge presided, and there was hardly a chair unoccupied. The club may reasonably congratulate itself upon having such an excellent array of musical talent at its command. Most of the artistes are members of the organization, and the programme submitted was of the first order and gave manifest pleasure. Songs were rendered with fine effect by Ali% W. H. Ireland, Mr. Fred Fieldhousa, and Mr. J. W. Bibby all of whom are favourites at the club's concerts, and were rewarded with hearty applause. Violin solos were rendered with taste and skill by Mr. T. Ovenstone, and Mr. W. H. Podmore added much to the enjoyment of the evening by a well- executed flute solo. The humorous element was in the capable hands of Mr. W. W. Whittle, whose character sketches created great hilarity. Able accompanists were found in Messrs. T. R. Ford and T. Taylor.—The artistes were cordially thanked on the proposition of Mr. E. Pickavance, seconded by Mr. D. Davies.</p>
        <p>Mr. Picksvance is to-day attending the annual meeting of the National Cyclists' Union in London as one of the representatives of the Liverpool centre. It is interest- ing to note that in the annual report of the centre re- cently issued special mention is made of the services of Messrs. Pickavance and Garrett to whom the centre looks to engineer the series of social evenings.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING UP TIME. To-night Sunday</p>
        <p>~ Mo uday 7.36 Tuesday 7.38 Wednesday Thursday Friday</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>It is safe to say that, in Warrington at any rate, the royal and ancient game" of bowls is the most widely followed of summer pastimes. In the course of a few weeks local trundlers will figure prominently on the many greens in and around the town, and given favour- able weather the coming season should prove unusually successful, the greens, almost without exception, being in splendid condition. In some quarters it was feared thst the Warrington Works Association would not be continued, but such apprehensions have been without foundation. Any decision to abandon what has proved to be an interesting compe- tition would have been regretted by scores, perhaps by hundreds of working men to whom the exis tence of the Association has for several years proved a source of active enjoyment. The green attached to the King and Queen Hotel will again be the scene of the friendly rivalry between the works teams.</p>
        <p>Interest is being maintained in the Warrington and District Bowling League, as was manifested by the large attendance at the annual meeting held at the Brooklands Hotel on Tuesday. In all probability with the exception of one—the Brooklands—the clubs connected with the league last year will again rejoin and in addition several new clubs have expressed a desire to become associated. It was hoped that this year it would be possible to run a second division competition, but on account of there being an inadequate response to the suggestion, the scheme has for the present been abandoned. The membership of the Brookland's Club has been withdrawn, as Mr. Earle has decided to make the green an " open " one. Mr. Earle last year acted as treasurer to the league, but at his own request he was on Tuesday relieved of the duties, Mr. G. F. Freeman being elected to succeed him.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting Mr. A. H. Crosfield was unani- mously re-elected president of the League, whilst the following were appointed vice-presidents :—Mderman Evans, Councillor Wilkinson, and Messrs. J. Allison, J. Earle, J. Forster, J. W. Kaye,J. Newton, C. E. Parker, J. Rushworth, J. Roberts, W. J. Shaw, S. Taylor, W. Unsworth, J. E. Warren and J. Berry.</p>
        <p>Mr. Freeman, who last year acted as chairman was upon his own proposition succeeded in that office by Mr. J. W. Kaye. Mr. Edward Taylor, who has proved an energetic hon. secretary asked that someone else should take over those duties but the members declined to take that course and unanimously re-elected him to the office. The quer:don was raised as to whether one club could be represented by the two teams, but the opinion expressed was that such a step would not be desirable. It would perhaps be as well for all clubs to take into the consideration the question of running two teams. If it was found possible to do so the question of forming a second league could be formally settled. The fixtures for the season have not yet been arranged this matter being referred to a meeting to be held on April 6th.</p>
        <p>The date upon which the Corporation bowling green in Victoria Park will be opened has not yet been de- cided nor has the price which will be charged for a game, together with the use of " woods." In most towns where public greens have been provided, it has been found that the establishment of a small charge has led to increased receipts. In Edinburgh bowls are pro- vided and for their use a charge of one penny an hour is made. Many persons provide their own"woods" but this makes no difference in the charge of one penny imposed by the authorities. The smallness of the charge has resulted in an ever-increasing patronage of the greens, the number of players being more than doubled in four years. Last year the attendence on each of the fifteen Hdinburgh greens worked out at 13,200, and the income was sufficiently large to more than pay for the upkeep of the greens. It is impassible to estimate how many persons will take advantage of the Warrington Corpora- tion's provision in Victoria Park, but if the interest in bowling is maintained in all probability the establish- ment of a charge such as is made in Edinburgh will bring in a revenue to pay expenses. In any event., the Corporation would be justified in making the experiment</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Swimming.</head>
        <p>Swimming.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Swimming Club are again holding a Works Squadron championship for the Sir G. Greenall Challenge Shield open to teams four aside, entran3e fees 2s. per team. Entries close June 33. Further particulars can be obtained from Air. C. Ashton, Bridge Foot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MR. STANLEY WILSON'S NEW APPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>MR. STANLEY WILSON'S NEW APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>Presentations From Councillors, Officials and Friends.</p>
        <p>To-day Mr. Stanley Wilson, deputy Town Clerk, severs his connection with the Corporation after having ably served that authority for twenty-five years. At an early age he entered the Town Clerk's office as an office boy. He at once applied himself studiously to the work with the result that his promotion was rapid, and some four- teen years ago he was appointed to the position which he has vacated to take up the Town Clerkship of Leigh. During his Association with the Corporation he has earned the respect and good feeling both of his fellow officials and of the members of the council a iespect which was exemplified on Tuesday when he was made the recipient ofa handsome solid silver tea set, and solid silver tray. The pieces were of James I. design and upon each was engraved the borough arms and Mr. Wilson's initials. The tray, which has raised and beautifully chased borders, also bears the inscription : "To Stanley Wilson, Esq., on his appointment as Town Clerk of Leigh, from the members of the Council, and the officials of the borough of Warrington, March 25th, 1905." The articles, which were supplied by Messrs. Eustance and Co., Ltd.. Sankey street, will be on view , in their shop window during the week. 1 The presentation was made at the Town Hall, the largo attendance of the members of the Corporation and officials being eloquent testmony to M. Wilson's popularity. The presentation was made by the Mayor who con- gratulated Mr. Wilson on his success and testified to the esteem in which he was held by members of the Cor- poration and townspeople generally. Councillor FORSIIAW, Alderman BENNITI'T. and the TOWN CLERK also spoke. the letter said Mr. Wilson "had been not only an official but a comrade," and they wished him every success. Mr. WILSON feelingly replied, expressing his gratitude for the many kindnesses he had received at the hands of the members of the Council, the officials, and particu- larly the Town Clerk. With regard to the latter he had no hesitation in saving of Mr. Whittle that " he had been the best friend he ever had." PRESENTATION FROM THE WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At a largely attended smoking concert held at the Old Academy last evening, Mr. Wilson was the recipient of a pair of handsome silver candlesticks from the members of the Warrington Society. Mr. Wilson has been con- nected with the organization from its formation, and has rendered very useful service as a member of various committees. The gathering was presided over by Mr. L. Greening, chairman of the General Purposes Committee of the Society, who made the , presentation in suitable terms. He also handed to Mr. Wilson an address, signed by over fifty mem- bers of the Society, which read as follows :—"The members of the Warrington Society beg to convey to , Stanley Wilson Esq., their hearty congratulations on his appointment to the Town Clerkship of Leigh. They also wish to place on record their high appreciation of his efforts not only on behalf of the Society but on behalf of the social and municipal life of the Borough of Warrington." Several members spoke in high ap- preciation of Mr. Wilson's good qualities, and congratu- lated him upon his success. Mr. Wilson briefly returned thanks. An enjoyable musical programme, arranged by Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins was submitted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF A SUCCESSOR.</head>
        <p>THE APPOINTMENT OF A SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the special committee appointed to con- sider the question of tiff.. Wilson's successor was held at the Town Hall. on Tuesday, the Mayor presiding. it was decided to recommend the General !Purposes Com- mittee to advertise .for apelications from qualified men, the salary to commence at £175, and to rise by £l5 yearly to £250. The sub-committee also resolved to recommend the appointment of Mr. T. H. Spinks, the chief clerk in Mr. Whittle's office, to the position of assistant to the Town Clerk with an increased salary.</p>
        <p>Revival Meeting at Bold-street Church. A large congregation assembled at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Thursday evening, when a revival meeting was held. The principal feature of the proceedings was the description of revival meetings in Wales by the Revs. J. S. Hughes and R. Wilkins Rees The first named gentleman remarked that one of the most wonderful characteristics of the 'Welsh Revival was the obliterating of all sectarian differences. "If such a thing happened in Stockton Heath or Warrington "he 'id" I should think the- ium had come. I should</p>
        <p>saiL hardly believe it I should be in such a state of spiritual ecstacy that for the moment I should doubt whether I was standing upon my head or upon my legs." The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees dealt mainly with the effect of the revival upon public houses stating that most public houses were doing only a very small fraction of the trade which they formerly did. The meeting was continued with prayer from the ministers present but the response from the congregation was only small.</p>
        <p>!" Lord Kenyon has placed in a public-house on his 'estate near Bolton a manager who, in addition to his salamis to be paid a commission upon the sale of non- intoxicating drinks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS Whisky Order Causes Discussion.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS Whisky Order Causes Discussion.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians net at the Work- house on Wednesday, Mr. A. Brooks presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, the Rev. Dr. Mercer, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Hickman. J. Biltinge, S. Davies, T. H. Ashcroft, G. Mather, W. Houghton, G, Thompson, W. Flannagan, R. Bolton, M. W. Seton, T. Ritchie, J. C. Sutton (clerk), F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>THE. 1.11. TE MR. JOHN DIGNLTM.</head>
        <p>THE. 1.11. TE MR. JOHN DIGNLTM.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY said that since the last meeting a former member of the District Council, Mr. Dignum had passed away. He thought it would be in accordance with pre- cedent and certaihly a very graceful and proper thing, if the Board expressed by a vote of condolence with the family, their sense of the loss which the community had suffered by his death. Those who knew him both on that Board and the District Council respected him. He (Mr. Jolley) did not think anyone who knew Mr. Digunm had less than a feeling of warm respect and affection both for his character and his work. Mrs. CLAY seconded- Mr. Houorrrox, in supporting, said a truer gentleman than Mr. Dignurn never sat on the Board. Mr. THOMPSON said that as a friend and neighbour of Mr. Dignum's for many years he endorsed all that had been said. rt had been his pleasure to work with Mr. Dignum on the Board and Council some years back. Mr. MATITER spoke highly of Mr. Dignam's services as secretary of the hi gher technical authority of Winwick. Mr. HFCRMAN said Mr. Dignum was always faithful to his duties and respectful to his colleagues, and he gave all the time he possibly could to the work, The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said he knew Mr. Dignum during the whole of his public life and for] many years besides, and it would afford him satisfaction to perspnally con- vey the vote of condolence to the family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>CONTRACTS.</head>
        <p>CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>The House Committee recommended the acceptance of the following tenders for the ensuing half year:— Coal, Messrs. Pendlebury and Co.; brushes, Mr. 'George Ratcliffe; meat Mr. James Bowcock ; coffins, Mr. A. E. Mason; clogs, Mr. William Jones; boots and shoes. Mr.' Alfred Taylor; flour and oatmeal, Mr. Hallwood; tailors' goods, Mr. E. A. Jones; drapery, Mr. T. J. Lee; groceries, Messrs. Milling and Co., Limited. There were no tenders for leather and Mr. Pickavance intim- ated that he was unable to tender owing to the state of the leather market. The committee resolved to make what purchases are necessary at current market prices. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>The Board also r-tWrOZ-cl the acceptance of the following tenders for the supply of the goods named to Padgate Schools :—Groceries, Messrs. H. Milling and Co.. Ltd.; tailors' goods, Mr. T. J. Lee; drapery, Mr. 17. J. Men; flour and meal, Mr. J. Hall wood ; bread. Mr. J. Hallwood; meat, Mr. H. Singleton ; hrushes,Mr.S.N. Green; fuel, Messrs. Pendlebury and Co.</p>
        <p>D ESC USSION ABOUT WHISKY. The list of requisites for the House submitted by the Clerk included a qoart of whisky from Messrs.Pickmere. Mr. ASHCROFT said the Board was asked to sanction the purchase of a quart of whisky each month and he wanted to know what it was for. (Laughter.) It was no laughing matter. Mr. DAVIES : I hops Mr. Ashcroft does not infer that the committee drink the whisky. (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN: You get you: share, Mr. Ashcroft. Mr. ASHCROFT : I don't. I have never had whisky in this establisnment. The CHAIRMAN : you get as much as I do. • Mr. AsHcitoFT : I want to know what it is for. Is it the inmates or some of the Guardians who drink it?</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Mr. Ashcroft can sooa get at it if he looks at the medical officer's book.</p>
        <p>Me. ASHCROFT : whisky goes. Mr. DAVIES: I thiwk we all know very well that it goes to the sick people. Mr. ASHCROFT :—Let the Chairman answer. It is a question whether it goes to the sick people or not. The CARMAN: It is open to you to examine the medical officer's book. You can very soon see whether the quantity ordered by the medical officer tallies with the quantity named in the list of requisites. It may not be a laughing matter, but it is not a matter on which to get up and make insinuations. Mr. ASHCROFT : lam not making insinuations. If the whisky is for the hospital I am satisfied. The CHAIRMAN : It is for the hospital. The CLERK : No account of that kind can pass before the auditor without his being satisfied as to the use of the article for which it is charged. UNION RITES REDUCED,</p>
        <p>You ought to explain where the</p>
        <p>PROBA.BLE INCREASC OF COINFV R ITE. The CLFACK, in submitting the estimates for the coming half-year, said he proposed to call for a reduc- tion of Id. in the £. He hoped that the reduction of id. in the whole district, and also of id. in the rural districts which had been sanctioned by the District Council, making a total of 2d.in the £,would be sufficient to meet the purposes of the Board during the ensuing half-year. He must warn the oveseers of all places except Warrington that it was quite certain that the county rate would again be increased. He had just sent .E 7,700 in addition to the same amount paid for last half-year, and he felt quite satisfied that the county would add to the rates during the next half-year, at least Id. in the X. It might be more. That, of course, would be called for separately. The rate for the Board amounted to 7d. in the £</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN : How does that compare with last half 'gear? The CLERK : Last half-year it was Sd., and the pre- vious half-year 7d. but the latter amount proved insufficient.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. BiHinge the CLERK Said the assess- able value of Warrington had increased by about £1,200. They used to suppose that Warrington with a much smaller area would bring an additional £5,0D0 per annum but it was not doing that now, although the area of the borough had very much extended. The estimates were passed. INCREASE OF SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Cif Xi UntA-N. moved the adOP-tiOri.of the following recommendation of the sub-committee which has been considering the question of the relieving officer's duty "That it is undesirable to further re-arrange the Relief Districts. That in consideration of the satisfactory discharge of his duties and of recent additions to the Number 3 Relief District the Committee unanimously recormaeni in addition to the salary paid Mr. Ankland. That the s.tiary be increased from £ll5 to £125 per annum as from March 25th, 1905." Mr. JOLLEY seconded. _ _ .</p>
        <p>ille-R:111-;;;:sWid—heunderstood the ClllllllittA., WAS appointed to go into the whole question of the re-arranging of the districts, and the methol of administering out-relief. -</p>
        <p>The CITAIIi'MAN Not out relief.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said the committee was of the opiaion that it was not desirable to alter the areas of the dis- tricts. He explained that although there was a pressure on Mr. Garnett's district for the time being it was much lighter than it formerly was when other officers held the position. The committee did not agree that there was any ground for an alteration. Mr. Garnett's district was compact while Mr. Aukland's was scattered. Mr. BILLINGE contended that it would have been in</p>
        <p>the jut Tests of the poor, the ratepayers of the Guardians if a portion of Mr. Garnett's district had been allocated to Mr. Aukland and his salary had been increased by insteod of £lO. It would have relieved Mr. Garnett and given him more time for the inquiry and super- vision which the Local Government Board %veva asking Guardians to carry out more rigidly. After farther discussion the recommendation was approved.</p>
        <p>_ MA INTENA NC E CITAR G ES. It was resolved :—"That Mr. Shearer as collector be authorised to collect in cases of removals for non- lunatics the cost of maintenance recoverable during the 3 or more weeks of chargeability."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ANNUAL POLICE INSPECTION.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL POLICE INSPECTION.</p>
        <p>At eleven o'clock yesterday forenoon, Lieutenant Colonel Eden,.H.M. Inspector of Constabulary for the Northern District, made his annual inspection of the Warrington County Borough Police Force. Colonel Eden was met at Bank Quay Station by the Chief Con- stable and Superintendent Monaghan. The entire force with the exception of a few men detailed for duty in the streets, was assembled at the Police Station. whsre the inspecting officer was met (in the absence, on muni- cipal business of the Mayor by the Deputy•Nfa,yor (Alderman Fairclough), several members of the Watch committee and a representative body of justices and head officials. Colonel Eden made a thorough inspec- tion of the men and their kits, the latter being neatly arranged in front of the men as they stood in line in the parade room. The men were then marched to the open space in Wilson Patten-street where they proceeded to drill, being put through is number of movements by the Chief Constable and the Drill Inspector, Chief Inspector Phillips. The various movements were performed with military precision. The cells, offices and residential quarters were next visited by the Inspector, who carefully examined them, pro- bouncing them the finest he had over seen. Subsequently Colonel Eden proceeded to the Chief Con- stable's office where the whole of the police books, records and documents were produced for his inspec- tion. He subjected the whole to a mcre than ordinarily 1 searching audit, and pronounced everything in excellent order. At the conclusion of hi inspection, Colonel Eden complimented the Chief Constable very highly on the appearance of the men, the smart and satisfactory manner in which they drilled and their general fitness. lie also expressed his entire approval of, and admiration for, the manner in which the police books and records were kept, the up-to-date methods and system prevail- ing in the police office, and the high efficiency of the entire establishment.</p>
        <p>The Vitehouse Charity Concert. As the result of the concert recently held in the !Royal Court Theatre the stun of 10 has been handed over to the widow of the late Mr. J. H. Whitehouse, who for a long period held the post of bookstall clerk , at the Central Station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the country. The visitation of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greeter extent than almost any other I disease and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he has only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. ; It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Complaints mnst be ascribed to the baneful influence of Influenza, whose after-effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. I Year after year it has never since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages' and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will,! regain their former health. As a Preventative, we know , of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often proved effectual in warding off attacks of this Malady, and: always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from , Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes, Severe Head- ache, accompanied by difficulty in breathing, and! symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest, under and ' between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of , the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you I suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilym Evans' I Quinine Bitters. By common corsent Patients every where admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an attack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these ! miserable feelings is to take Gwilym Evans' Quinine ' Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has , treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds,Extreme Weakness, and Lan gour, with greater success than any other know remedy. We may also add that as a remedy I for Indigestion,Sleeplessness, Low Spirits, Nervousness, ; and Loss of Appetite, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters ; stands unequalled. It is sold in bottles 2s. 9d, and ' Is. 9d., by all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, I or sent direct, carriage free, for the above prices, by the Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Avoid Imitations. See the name "Gwilym Evans "on Label Stamp and Bottle.</p>
