      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WHAT CONSTITUTES A WIFE'S DEPENDENCY Important Point Raised at Warrington.</head>
        <p>WHAT CONSTITUTES A WIFE'S DEPENDENCY Important Point Raised at Warrington.</p>
        <p>An important point was raised at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., in the claim brought by Ann Dawson, Stockport,to recover from Messrs. Joseph Crosfleld:and Sons, Limited,the sum of .Ml, compensa- tion for the loss of her husband who was killed whilst following his employment at the respondent's works on the 20th November, 1904. The details of the accident were, briefly, that on the date named Dawson was en gaged examining the contents of an elevated settling tank, when he slipped and fella distance of fifteen feet to the ground sustaining injuries which proved fatal. Applicant was represented by Mr. Cuthbert Smith (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel), respondents being represented by Mr. Rigby Swift (instructed by Messrs. 'Richardson and Marsh, Liverpool) appeared for the Insurance Company with whom Messrs. Crosfield were insured. Mr. Cuthbert Smith said the facts of the case were rather curious. Mr. Swift : A man named Lawson was killed at our works. Mr. Smith Isay that man was the man named Dawson. Mr. Swift : After Lawson was killed a lady came along and said Lawson was her husband and that his real name was Dawson, and that she was dependent upon him. I say the man was not Lawson but Lawson. Directly after he came in:o the employment of the respondents he married another woman with whom he lived ever since and who is so far as I know his lawful wife. Mr. Smith : As a matter of fact the death certificate had been altered from Lawson to Dawson by means of . .</p>
        <p>a statutory declaration. Continuing he said that on the 20th November,l9o4,a married a man named Richard Dawson was killed and Mr. Steel was instructed to . . .</p>
        <p>... -- act on behalf of a woman who described herself as his j ' widow and on her behalf put in a claim for compensa- tion. It was then found that the man was not Lawson,J but that his proper name was Richard Dawson whose wife was living at Stockport. Mr. Steel then on behalf of the applicant interviewed Mr. F. R. Roberts,solicitor I to Messrs. Crosfleld, who said there would be no ques- tion as to compensation except as to amount. He was going to prove that Mrs. Dawson was in fact the widow. ghe married Richard Dawson in 1887. He would call a man named John Lawtor, brother of Ann Dawson, who was a witness of the marriage. The deceased man borrowed £lO from him, and he had not seen him since 1900. He saw the body of the man whom the respon- dent said-was Lawson, which he identified as the body of his brother-in-law. It was therefore prettyclear that the two men were one man. He contended that although Mrs. Dawson was deserted by her husband in 1900 she was wholely dependent upon him, or at least partially dependent upon him. In 1900 the deceased in Septem- ber,l said he was going ' 'Mafeking." , His Honour: To South Africa. Mr. Swift: He n--.2a.ris on the "booze." Mr. Smith: He meant he was going to celebrate the relief of Mafeking. Continuing, he said Dawson then left his wife who however rejoined him at her mother's house in Stockport. He then said he was going to ' America and again went away and was not again seen by his wife. Mrs. Dawson went to live with her relatives and helpel to maintain herself by going out washing. Her health gave way and her brother, who was an over- looker at a mill, gave her light work at the mill. Her mother and brothers assisted her to provide food and clothing for herself and the children. James Law ton then gave evidence of identity. Mr. Smith contended that according to the law a wife was always dependent upon her husband, and when a wife was deserted by her husband and had no private means she became an agent of necessity. Mr. Smith quoted cases in the Scatch Court of appeal which he contended supported his argument. Desertion was a continuous offence and if a contention that a widow was not a dependent because her husband deserted her five years before his death was good, it would apply equally if she had only been deserted a day before her husband died. Mr. Swift pointed out that the House of Lords had always decided that the question of dependency was a question of fact, and in that case he contended the applicant was not a dependent in point of fact. The , Compensation Act of 1897 provided that aperson should I be entitled to compensation'who is wholely orlpartially j dependent upon a man's earnings at the title of his ; death." In that case the applicant was not dependent upon her husband at the time of his death, nor had she been so for several years. She had lost nothing by reason of her husband's death. His Honour said his view was that the Compensation Act was in,2nded for the compensation of persons who were deprived of something by reason of a workman being killed. In that c9se the applicant had not been deprived of anything by the death of her husband. Mr. Cuthbert Smith again addresaed his Honour, con- tending that every married woman was dependent upon her husband. His Honour: The consequence of your argument would be that in order to get compensation a woman has Inly to prove marriage. Mr. Smith : Unless it can be shown that she has other means that I contend is so. When a wife is deserted, unless she has private means she is entitled to pledge his credit. The only reasoa why the applicant did not pledge her husband's credit is that he was tot traceable. If she was not wholely dependent I contend she was partially dependent. His Honour : My impression is that she was not dependent. but I would like to examine the Scotch cases quoted. My opinion of the Act is that it was to provide for cases where a loss is sustained through death. His Honour decided to reserve judgment, which for the convenience of Counsel he agreed to deliver in the Birkenhead County Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BLACKPOOL'S HIGH TIDES.</head>
        <p>BLACKPOOL'S HIGH TIDES.</p>
        <p>The highest tides of the year will be recorded at Blackpool this week, but the stinamerlike weather which prevails at present is rather calculated to rob them of their magnificent spectacular effect. For instance, on Wednesday, the high-water level reached an inch past the 30ft. mark, yet the water was as placid as a mill pond. Next week The Walter Scott Publishing Co., Ltd., will publish the absorbing work,Science and Hypothesis by -Mornieur PoincatAthe famous French mathematician and se • nt. This work is prefaced with an introduction from t: • pen of Joseph Larmor, M.A., D.Sc., Secretary of the Loyal Society.</p>
