      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>MR. F. MONKS RESIGNS THE CHAIRMANSHIP.</p>
        <p>Disappointing King's Scholarship Results.</p>
        <p>i;EADMISTRESS AND HER SALARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks, J.P., who has been chairman of the Warrington Education Committee from its formation, has severed his connection with the authority. The announcement of his impending resignation was made at the concluding meeting of the committee for the year which was held at the Town Hall on Monday evening. There were many expressions of regtet at the step which Mr. Monks has seen fit to take—the resignation having since been formally made in a letter to the Town Clerk. The Committee discussed several matters of importance, and the public will be interested in the Rev. Morley Stevenson's statement as to the somewhat disappointing results of the recent King's Scholarship examination. Mr. F. Monks presided over the meeting and there were also present :—Alderman Bennett (vice-chairman), the Mayor, the Revs. Morley Stevenson, N. Wilson and J. Yonge, Alderman Pierpoint,Councillors Barton, Brough, Evans, Hulse. Lord, Roberts, Sankey, Fulton Smith, Messrs. W. Bibby, J. D. Buckton, R T. Pairclough, A. J. Jolley, H. Kingdon, C. E. Parker, E. Plinston. C. H. Unsworth, J. Upson, G. Walker, J. Moore Murray (director), and John Fairhurst (treasurer).</p>
        <p>HEADMISTRESS AND HER SALARY. PROPOSAL TO WITH-HOLD £5 INCREMENT DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>At aSmeeting of the Finance Committee the following extract from the proceedings of the General Purposes Committee, at their meeting on the 20th Februau,l9os, was submitted, viz.: —" That the minute of the Finance Committee, with reference to the salary of the head mistress of the mixed department of the Trinity National school, be referred back to that committee for re-considesation." And, after some discussion, it was resolved, that the annual increase of salary of the head mistress of the mixed department of the Trinity National school be with-held for one year, and that, subject to the conduct of the school being satisfactory in the meanti.ne, the matter be ,re-considered, without prejudice in January next. Mr. A. JOLLEY moved and Mr. J. D. Buckton seconded the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON said he rather thought Mr. Jolley would have referred to the question of the salary of the head- mistress of Trinity School. Apparently, however, be did not think the matter worth mentioning, but why, he could not understand, because he (Mr. Plinston), was conv7nced that some explanation ought to be given for withholding a teacher's increase of salary. Therefore he moved that the minute be referred back. He did that be-muse, so far as he could understand, the teacher In question had not been guilty of anything which would justify the withholding of her increase. If the committee was going to punish teachers because the managers of a school might get their backs up a little bit at the officialism of the Education office or a sub-committee, he said that was not the right policy to pursue. The CHAIRMAN: I think I must correct you. The explanation is that it was in consequence of the irregu- larity of the entries in the book. Mr. PLINSTON, continuing, said he believed it was a fact that the head mistress had carried out the work at the school in a very satisfactory manner ; that the re- ports had been excellent, and that the higher grant had been obtained. He thought in view of the importance of the matter, they ought to have an explanation as to why the teacher was being penalised in the manner sug- gested. He knew that the chairman of the Finance Committee was in the habit of reckoning people up very severely, and talking about principle there was a principle at stake in this case. Some members might be taunted with the fact that they were too ready to listen to outside complaints. Persorally he was willing to listen to complaints when he believed anyone was being improperly treated. (Hear, hear). Councillor LORD seconded.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Join: YONGE asked if the committee's resolution meant that the increment was only being held over until the year's en,'. when it would be granted if things went en all right in the mesntime, or whether it meant that she would not receive the increment at all and be fined £25 for that very small offence against the Education Committee. Mr.JoLLEY said the committee's desire was not to fine the teacher .£25 but to withhold the increment until the ends of the year, and decide the matterlon its merits then. Their hands were absolutely free, and they would reconsider the question at the end of the 12 months. They did not bind themselves to anything. Mr. UPSON said that was not very satisfactory to him. If the Finance ;Committee 'would say that the !incre- ment of £5 would be given at the end of the year if the school was properly conducted it would be all right. The Rev. John Yonge : I understand Mr. Jolley to mean that.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: No ;he said it weuld be an open question. Tne Rev. J. YONGE : if it is an open question I object to the explanation.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON, continuing, said they discussed the matter very thoroughly in the General Purposes Committee a month ago, and a vote was recorded in favour of the matter being referred back. He took it that what was in the minds of those who on that occasion was that the increase should be given, and that the teacher would be sufficiently punished if a reprimand was given. He sup- ported Mr. Plinston, and protested strongly that the minute was against the weight of opinion of the whole committee. He did not think the Finance ',Committee I had power to deal with the question, as the power of dealing with the salaries of teachers was vested in the School Maragement Committee. He folt that the teacher was being unjustly dealt with, and that a repri- mand would be sufficient punishment. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON said he thought they would be satiated if they could have some assurance that the reconsideration of the matter, when the time came, would be entirely in sympathy with the granting of the increment, provided the teacher's work was satis- factory. Councillor JAMES EVANS thought the Finance Com- mittee were out of order in dealing with the question, which he thought was purely and simply a matter for the School Management Committee. He understood that the Holy Trinity School earned the highest grant last year, so that he did not think things could have been so bad after all. He thought the irregularity did not by any means deserve the punishment which it was proposed should be meted out. Councillor LORD said he would like to know what the penalty was for, whether for bad management or an error in the stock book? He would also like to know whether Miss Davies had had an opportunity of defend- ing herself? If she had not, a gross injustice had been done to her. The Rev. JOHN YONGE said he believed the fact of the matter was that this head teacher had on two occasions had some trifling discrepancy, for which he understood she was not personally responsible in the stock account, in regard to keeping stock, the present one, he though, amounting to Be., for which the Finance Committee proposed a month ago to practically fine her £25. That was very disproportionate indeed to the offence alleged. He believed it was also right that the lady had never had an opportunity of explaining her position, and further, it was right that the Inspector had spoken very highly of the school. He strongly urged that they ought to have an assurance from the chairman of the Finance Committee that tee most favourablo consideration should be given to the matter at the end of the year, or that it should be decided at that meeting. He did not wish to force the Finance Committee into an unpleasant position, but he thought it was due to the opinion of the majority of the com- mittee that such an assurance should be given. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he could assure the Rey. John Yonge that it was not unpleasant for the Fina ace Com- mittee to do its duty. Some little warmth had been engendered by some of the sp-akers, who seemed to think the action of the committee was out of order, but if they remembered the circumstances of the case they would see how the case primarily arose. The head teachers were partly responsible to the School Manage- ment Committca with respect purely to instruction and with respect to the stock accounts they were respon- sible to the Finance Commitcee. If that committee was to be so bitterly attacked and assured that their con- duct was disgraceful every time they did their duty the working of the education system in Warring- ton was going to be made very difficult indeed. They bad 42 head t2acher3 in the town. He was a strong advocate for efficiency as well as economy, and he objected to anything which looked like putting a premium upon incompetence. It was very unpleasant and painful for him to say anything which might be a personal reflection upon a teacher,but it was not accurate to say that there had been only one complaint. There bad been others. The desire of the com- mittee was to exclude the personal element, and all they wished was to do their duty. There was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>No Break-fast Table complete without</head>
        <p>No Break-fast Table complete without</p>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious and Economical.</p>
        <p>a principle at stake. The interests of the teachers as a whole and of the ratepayers had to be an r—luarded in reference to the stock of the school. It w ;iota ques- tion of ss. or 65., but was a matter of principle. He was not in a position to give a pledge on behalf of the Finance Committee. The giving of increments should not be regarded as purely automatic, but as a reward for good services, and he thought it would be wrong to reward the incompetence which had be an manifested for the last two years. Mr. PLINSTON thought Mr. Jolley had hardly been fair. He would like to know whether it was not a fact that the school was under staffed.</p>
        <p>The CIiAIRMAN : If every little action of the com- mittee is to be brought up on personal grounds— Mr. UPSON : I beg your pardon. Have you any right to speak? .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN': lam not saying anything more. Upon the amendment being put to the meeting it was carried by fifteen votes to ten. The other minutes were agreed to. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE. THE RECENT KING'S SCHOLARSHIP EXAMINATION. DISAPPOINTING RESULTS.</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of the minutes of the School Management Committee the Rev. Morley STEVENSON alluded to the recommendations with regard to the physical instruction of children. The committee, he said, were strongly of opinion that it was far better that the physical exercise of the scholars should be given in the playgrounds of their own schools whenever possible, and that they should not be taken- to the Gymnasium for that purpose. There were no fewer than 169 of the assistant teachers in the employment of the committee who possessed proper and regular drill certificates and the Education Committee would, there- fore, see, that they had not a staff which was either inadequate or incompetent: to give the instruction. Mr. Stevenson proceeded to refer to the following table in which the results in the King's scholarship examination for several years were laid before the com- mittee for purposes of comparison : Class First Second Third</p>
        <p>DIVISION.</p>
        <p>H. M. IV. V. Total successes 0 0 0 2 2 7 4 3 4 22 6 8 2 5 26</p>
        <p>Number of Warrington students accepted for admission to Training Colleges:-18, out of 19 who were eligible for admission. ANALYSIS OF RESULTS OBTAINED IN PREVIOUS YEARS. PASS•D. PAILED. Ist per- 2nd per- 3rd per- per-. Sat. cl. cent. cl. cent. cl. cent. No. cent 3 1893 19 0 0 4 2105 10 526 5 267 1899 17 3 17.6 10 58.8 2 11.7 2 11* 1900 11 2 18.2 4 36.4 5 45.4 0 0 1901 16 3 18.7 10 62.5 3 18.7 0 0 1902 17 6 35.3 6 35.3 4 23.5 1 58 1903 29 3 10.3 10 34.4 14 48.2 2 61 1904 54 2 3.7 22 40.7 26 48.1 4 7.4 1904 All En.;land, 14644, 1892, 12.9, 3359, 26.3, 5733, 39.0, 3150, 21. °Year before centre opened. He did not hesitate to say that the results as such were very disappointing to the committee who were the managers of the centre. The weak point, the blot on the results, was the exceeding smallness ofithe first-class, and to that fact they could not shut their eyes. The second and third classes did not compare unfavourably with the results in other towns as a rule. The number of first class passes throughout the kingdom was materi- ally, less than last year, and there was no doubt that the standard of the examination had been raised. To a certain extent that accounted for the smallnesl of the first class passes, which was felt all over England. During the year the pupils at the centre had been under three different headmasters, and they must not lose sight of the fact that such frequent changes had a serious effect upon any school. No blame could attach to the present Principal of the centre, wlso only came into office three months before the pupils entered for the examination, so that they must hold him quite clear of any responsi- bility. The committe had taken the matter into their very earnest consideration' and in conjunction with the principal were engaged in taking such steps es they all hoped would produce better results next year. (Hear, hear.) They bad in Mr. Robson a very able man who was devoting himself heat t and soul to the work of the Institute in all its branches and he was fully alive to the situation. He had no doubt that, with the help the committee were prepared to give him, they would have a much better result next year.</p>
        <p>THE USE OF THE BATHS BY SCHOOL CHILDREN Alluding to his subject and the refusal of the Town Parks and Baths Committee to grant the free two of the sezond class plunge bath to school children, the Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON said he did not profess to I know much about the baths, but for a considerable time I during the year the second-class baths were practically out of use. It seamed a pity that the children who would appreciate their use and to whom the use would prove beneficial, should not be allowed to use them. Owing to some error the reply to the letter of the com- mittee was not receiscl for several monblis, but when it came it was in the negative and no reason wrq given. The School Management Committee asked the Baths Committee to reconsider the matter,and if they did not see their way to grant the full request at all events to furnish them• withlthe reasons or allow a compromise. He was sure they had the sympathy of every member of the committee in their desire that a healthy exercise should be provided for the children. FREE SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>Mr. STEVENSON proceeded to refer to the minute of the committee recommending that a limited number of children, the circumstances of whose parents would otherwise preclude their entering the school,be admitted to the Secondary School free of charge (books also being provided), on the recommendation of the head teachers and the managers of the public elementary schools. The object of the committee was, he said, to help the clever child in the elementary school to pass up the first rung of the ladder into the Secondary I School. They wanted the held hacher of every school to keep his eyes open for those children whose p irents were not able, perhaps, to afford the fees of the Second- ary School. (Hear, hear). The genius °along poor elementary school children was a very rare individual but when he could be discovered it rested with the education authority to see that he had every chance of advancing himself. The Rev. Father WILSON seconded. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH asked a question he asked at a pre- vious meeting with reference to the closing of certain schools owing to outbreaks of measles or smallpox. It seemed wrong to pay teachers when the school was closed and give them the full term of holidays as well. The Rev. M. STEVENSON, in reply, said the matter had not been considered by the committee. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH : I shall be quite satisfied if it is considered. (Laughter.) . .</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT emphasized Mr. Stevenson's appeal to the Chairman of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, and trusted they would be able to give the School Management. Committee the facilities they desired to make it easier for the ch'ldren to learn how to swim thon was possible at the pre:ANA time. He felt the matter was of such importance that they would have been justified in providing the baths accommodation required in connection with the new schools if the rates had not been so high; in the circumstances the scheme was impracticable. The MAYOR said the Baths Committee had to render an account of rounds, shillings ond pence of all they did. If the Education Committee thought they were going to have something for nothing they were under a mistake. (Laughter.) If the Education Committee were prepared to make an offer with regard to the expense the Corporation would corsider the question. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>NEW SCHOOLS. PLANS APPROVED.</head>
        <p>THE NEW SCHOOLS. PLANS APPROVED.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Committee recommended that the plans for the "Bolton" schools which bore the distinguishing mark" Number I" be adopted, and that their author be requested to submit. an amended elevation for the approval of the Committ3e, and tb%t a fee of £lO 10s. be paid to each of the four unsuccessful competitors. The committee also recommended that plans bearing the distinguishing mark " Number 1" for the "Beamont "school be aoceptcd. The MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes of the committee, and alluded to the plan of the " Bolton " Council School. That product I was, ho said, the best and would meet the requirements of the Board of Education. Alderman PIERPOINT : What is the total cost to be? _ _ _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Sorr-•thing like ten guineas a head. It was, he added, though., that the unsuccessful competi- tors ought to be paid something for their trouble, and it had been agreed to give them ten guineas c .ch. The committee had been very much satisfied with the work of local architects and he did not Oink they could have got better plsns if they had gone out of 'town. (Hear, hear.) The successful competitors were, for the Bolton school, Messrs. Wright, Garnett and Wright, and for the Bearnont school Mr. Percy Silcock. He thought that what had been done would show the Board of Educa- tion that the committee were doing all they could to provide the new schools, and that they would be ready by the time which had been named. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH seconded the passing of the minutes. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The VICE-CHAIRMAN thought the plans of the new schools were, on the whole, highly ratisfactory. In them were practically embodied all the ideas they were able to get during their memorable visits to Birkenhead and other places. They bad the satisfaction of knowing that most of their ideas had been adopted and incor- porated in the plans which bnd been submitted that night. They would, he believed, have two schools, of which they would have resson to feel proud. (Hear hear.) The minutes were then agreed to. GENERAL PURPOSES CONIMITTEE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>FUTURE OF SANKEY BRIDGER WESLEYAN</head>
        <p>THE FUTURE OF SANKEY BRIDGER WESLEYAN</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the question of the transfer of the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan School to the Education Authority, was brought forward. After careful consideration,the terms for the proposed transfer to be submitted to the trustees were approved, and it was decided that notice should be given of the intention to transfer the school. —The terms for the transfer of the Silver-street Wes- leyan School were also considered and referred back to</p>
        <p>the parties interested for revision, a meeting to be held for this purpose at a later date.—The committee also decided to appoint Mr. Matthew Cunningham a repre- sentative manager of the Silver-street Wesleyan School, in the place of Mr. Peter Edgar, resigned. The minutes were approved on the motion of Mr. Monks seconded by the Mayor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CHAIRMAN'S SERVICES ACKNOWLEDGED.</head>
        <p>CHAIRMAN'S SERVICES ACKNOWLEDGED.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in moving a vote of thanks to the Chair- man for his services during the year eulogised the ability and zeal which Mr. Monks had brought to bear in the discharge of the duties. He had attended the greater number of the meetings of the committees, and he (the Mayor) had no doubt he had found it no sinecure to be chairman of the Warrington Education Committee, especially when he had been kept in harness. He had to come into contact with people who had ideas of all sorts, especially in a town like Warrington, which was composed of many reiigious bodies, but, he had sailed out of the "Slough of Despond" with the greatest possible credit te himself, the members of the committee, , and the town. (Applause.) The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON seconded and re-echoed all that the Mayor had said. They saw tbit Mr. Monks notwithstanding his numerous engagements and hard work, had attended no fewer tint' forty-three meetings during the yell.. When they remembered that some of the meetings had been of considerable duration, it would be seen that the work of the committee bad been no light tax upon the time and energy of Mr. Monks. He thought that, on the whole, the members of the committee had been good boys—(laughter)—but per- haps they owed the goodness of their conduct partly to the kindly fairness of their chairman. The resolution was carried with applause. MR. MONKS'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRssN said he was very much obliged to the Mayor and Mr. Stevenson for the kind words they had said about the work he had been able to perform during the year. He was sorry he would not be able to con- tinue it, as he had reaeled that time of life when his hearing was affected, and he found he could not give the necessary time to the work of the committee, He hoped, however, that at the next meeting they would appoint someone who would be able to devote more time to the duties,because he could see from the nature of the work that it required someone to be constantly in touch, not only with one committee, but with almost every sub-committee. He was particularly glad that during the two years he had had the honour of presid- ing over those meetings, no religious questions had been raised, and that although they might have had little differences of opinion there was no one in the room recognised more fully than he did the right of every man to exercise his own opinions on any matter. He (the chairman) might have made mistakes. He was sure he had, but at the same time he knew they would give him credit for the sincerity of his motives. He had had to let go a lot of work that he should have been glad to have continued if his health had been satisfactory. In conclusion, be thanked all present most warmly for the kindness which had been extended to him. (App.ause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>VICE-CHAIRMAN THANKED.</head>
        <p>THE VICE-CHAIRMAN THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said the words of the Chairman had cast a shadow over them. (Hear, hear.) He had to propose a vote of thanks to the Vice-Chairman for the able manner in which he had supported the Chair- man at the committee meetings and for the active interest he had taken in the sub-committe• work.. Alderman PIERPOINT seconded, and expressed the deep regret with which he had just heard the Chairman's announcement of his resignation. He did not think there was anyone in that room who had had such a long acquaintance with Mr. Monks as he had had, either in public or private, and he spoke with all feeling when he said that a better man than Mr. Monks never walked up the streets of Warrington. (Applause.) He felt when Mr. Monks was appointed chairman that he was thh one man with strong feelings on many poin; s who would be best able to bring that committee into a state of efficiency, which would keep them apart from all religious questions and he deeply rejoiced when he was placed in the chair. Now he was sorry to ' hear that Mr. Monks was about to leave it. The remarks of the Chairman applied to him (Alderman Pierpoint). His membership with the Education Committee would cease, and he should go out gracefully with Mr. Monks. Fortunately, he was not as old as the chairman, but with his own multifarious duties and the onerous duties of the committee, he should have to ask the Corporation of Warrington to relieve him of the obligation of attending those meetings. He did not altogether agree with Mr. Bennett on many occasions—(laughter)—but he admitted that he was earnest, energetic, and honest in maintaining his opinions, as far as his abilities allowed, and they were great. The motion was carried unanimously. Alderman BENNErr, in reply, said he had endeavoured as far as be bad been able, to discharge the duties of his office as vice-chairman to the best of his ability,but his work had been rendered extremely easy under the presidency of Mr. Monks. It had been an honour and a privilege to co-operate, in however small a degree, with Mr. Monks. He had heard the announcement he had just made with more than ordinary sorrow—a sorrow which he knew would be felt by everyone in the town, and particularly in respect of the cause. He had no hesitation in saying there was not a single man in Warrington who had rendered more genuine and consis- tent ser Aces to the town than Mr. Monks had done, and he was sure he would retire into private life with the best wishes of all of them. (Applause.) He was also sorry to hear of Mr. Pierpoint's impending resig- nation, and if it was not too late he hoped both gentle- men would be induced to reconsider thgir decision. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>...-.4,...__..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EFFECTS AND DANGERS OF ANAEMIA.</head>
        <p>EFFECTS AND DANGERS OF ANAEMIA.</p>
        <p>Anemia is having too little blood. It causes great weakness, shortness of breath, pale lips and gams, Headache, Indigestion. Neglected, it leads to consump- tion. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People cure Anzeinia just as food cures hunger ; they make blood, and Anternia cannot resist them.</p>
        <p>"Six months ago life was a burden to me," said Mrs. Winifred Eades, 30 Mount-street, Walsall (who suffered for twelve years with advanced Aniemia and Indigos- tiou), to the "Walsall Advestiser." Mrs. Eades gave the following account of her plight : "My lips were the same colour as my facie—ghastly white. My appetite was very poor. I bename posi- tively afraid to eat, for everything I took lay upon my chest like lead. There was never any warmth in my body; I seemed quite bloodless. I was always crouch- ing over the are, I was so cold. ' I went to the best physicians, If I had known then that my troubles would vanish when I got new blood, and that Dr, Williams' Pink Pills would make new, rich blood, I should not have taken so much ordin- ary medicine. The splendid set of teeth I used to possess have become decayed through continually taking iron. But my Anannia only grew worse, and I was at the point of desperation., , "Relief came when my mother bought me a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. can You judge by myappearance now how much I owe to these pills and how deeply grateful I am." "When did you note an improvement ?" "After tallpag the first few doses. The colour began to return to my lips and cheeks,l was able to sleep, and I had a wonderful appetite. People are struck with the change, and cannot understand it till I tell them t is due to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>"I feel better than ever I did in say life!" These are wonderful words from a woman to whom life had been a burden. All Mrs. Eades' troubles arose from lack of blood. Everybody knows the pain in the stomach when "loud hunger calls," but in Mrs. Eades' case every vein and nerve in her body was crying out for its natural food—blood. It was by making new, rich blood that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cured Mrs. Eades. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are famed for the way they have cured not only Anemia but Con- sumption, Pits, Rheumatism, Paralysis, and the frequent ailments which women endure in silence, Genuine pills cure; substitutes don't cure. Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, London, will send a box post free 2s. (six for 13s. 9d.) ; but Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cart be had at all medicine shops if you take care to ask for, and to see in red on the pink package the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Canada and Its Doukobors.</head>
        <p>Canada and Its Doukobors.</p>
        <p>This formed the title of a lecture which was delivered at a meating held at the Meeting House of the Society o Z Friends, Buttermarket-street on Satur- day evening. The Lecturer painted out that the Doukobots left Southern Russia, on account of the persecution they were subjected to, and went to Canada for the purpose of obtaining freedom. They were, he said, very honest, truthful, and trustworthy people, and those with whom they traded were perfectly satis- fied. Regarding their education, the problem was a difficult one, as the Doukobors refused to have anything to do with our Government schools. If they would allow themselves to be taught in Government schools their many difficulties would be overcome, and the only way was to send out people to teach them the English language.—The lecture was illustrated by means of an excellent series of limelight views, the lanternist being Mr. Thomas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>HEN'S LARGE EGG.</head>
        <p>A HEN'S LARGE EGG.</p>
        <p>A hen belonging to. Mr. W. Wyse, of Mi nfrordd,Glyn- dyfrdwy, has just laid an egg the measurements of which are ;—Girth, 7 in. ; transverse length, Blin. ; weight, • 4ioz. The hen is a cross between a golden wys ndott e and a langsban.</p>
        <p>MORE UNEARNED INCREMENT. Two London companies, says Sir John Puleston, each came years ago into possession of land worth £2OO. One sold when the value reached .£l,OOO, and the other did not. The one which held its hand is now drawing .C 20,000 a year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>TALL RIGIITS RESEEIVED.4</head>
        <p>TALL RIGIITS RESEEIVED.4</p>
        <p>EARTH-BOUND</p>
        <p>A MYSTIC STORY,</p>
        <p>JOHN STRANGE WINTER (Author of " Boot lc' Baby," " Iloup-la." Eta.)</p>
        <p>I don't think that people really know what ghost is, and they certainly do not know .what feels like to be what is called a ghost. All the same, when we have p:tssed over, you must un- derstand that we never think of ourselves ao " ghosts." No, we are ourselves just the saint as if we had remained on earth. But sometimes our passing over is brought about in such a way that all of us does not get to the other side, and that part which remains behind oftentimes takes a material form, and then those who are still oc- cupants of the planet call it—a ghost. I have long been anxious to explain the truth on this subject to such as are interested. in those still lingering about the scene of Iheir earthly life, and for many years I longed for the power of putting down my words in black and white, so that I might actually explain these things to the world I have left, which you know, as a rule, ghosts have no opportunity of doing. Ghosts who can materialise the voice might dictate their wishes, but not many people would have the nerve to take pen and paper and transcribe the utterances of a ghost ; and vet, if they knew us, they would understand how harm- less we are, how little we wish to injure those with whom we crave touch and speech, with whom we crave any kind of communication. In my times on earth I. of course, knew the terrible stories that were told of Glamis Castle and other ancestral homes, and I, too, shared with my brothers and sisters the indefinable horror which attaches itself to the personalities of those who are no longer, humanly speaking, alive. Let me explain myself a little more clear- ly. My name was Dorothy Trench. I lived a couple of hundred years ago in my father's house in the fair county of Worcester. There is a tab- let in A.shburton Church which says : " To the tender memory of Lady Dorothy, only daughter of Gilbert Trench, Earl of Ashbury, who died November 30th, 1702, from the result of an accident. She was young and very fair. place on earth and in the heart; or rowing parents can never be filled by another. I have seen my poor mother sitting in her ac- customed corner of the great pew in Ashburton Church, her eyes fixed upon that pathetic monu- ment, while my heart was breaking to comfort her. v_ _</p>
        <p>And how did I come by my earthly end? Well, it happened one day that I was about to descend the great flight of stairs leading to the large hall. Somebody had shut the iron dog- gates at the foot of the last step, and I, tripping, fell, striking my head against the trellis work of the gates. For a moment I knew nothing, but when I came to my senses again I was standing, with a curious, empty, dazed feeling, as if I were light-headed. I heard screams from various parts of the house, and the sound of running feet. I tried to tell them that I was not really hurt, and then, looking down, I saw a young girl, lily skinned and golden•haired, lying huddled up in a heap just within the dog-gates. I was so be- wildered from the effects of the blow that I stood speechless. I watched them raise her, saw the head with its wealth of trailing golden hair fall helplessly down, saw the golden locks which crowned it dabbled in blood. They carried her reverently to one of the great settles in the hall, and I followed, a little unsteady on my feet, a little light of limb and head, wondering who it was and why she was there.</p>
        <p>I remember so well the old butler and my own old nurse standing trying to restore her. " She is dead," said Andrew, " she is dead." _ _</p>
        <p>Thin my old nurse broke in with a terrible cry. " Who is to tell her ladyship 2" "That," said Andrew, "is your work, Goody." " Mine—mine " she cried. And then she flung her apron over her head and sobbed, "Oh the day, the weary day !" I understood what had happened when my mother came. Can you imagine what I felt, I standing there myself, with all my own feelings, my own senses about Inc. and yet unable to attract the notice of any of those persons present 2 And there that other self lay, pale and motion- less, while my mother's heart broke. Oh, if she was unhappy, I was unhappy too. I somehow did not understand all the truth. I had had an idea all my life that when people died, as they expressed it on earth, when people died they went either to a shining heaven or to a desolate hell. I was in neither. I had been hur- ried out of my earth life, and so I became neither a shining nor an evil spirit, but a poor ghost. I was a prisoner in the halls of my ancestors, my only company a poor knave who had been hanged upon the battlements for some trivial offence of which he was wholly innocent. Well, the years went by. You know we ghosts are not cognizant of the flight of time as we were when still upon the planet ; day and night are all the same to us ; our new life is wholly dif- fmat to the old one ; and while we are still bound down by the ties of love or of any other deep feeling to the life we have lived upon earth, we are wholly tuicon4cioun of the actual flight of time. But we have, as it were, beacon lights on our way.</p>
        <p>_ I watched my mother, day by day, change from a beautiful and comely woman, barely middle- aged, until she was old and decrepit. A great many years mnst have passed ere the summons came to her that her earthly career was at an end. Week after week I watebed her sad eyes fixed. upon that memorial tablet, day after day I was there to receive her when she entered my room night and morning, as she had always done ; and then, when she, too, had passed over, oh, sorrow of sorrows, I wits still hard bound upon earth, and I was left alone. They all went out of the old house one by one, and new faces took the place of those I had loved—new faces, new ways, new manners, new customs, and I and the poor knave t'r ere all alone, with, them and yet not with them, kin and yet strange, mere shadows 11110,14 e to al Inlet the SMIIIIOSt attention, unable to arouse any feeling but fear. Oh, my ease was aid, ten. sad. I was alone, and yet always in it crowd. I was still so young. I longed for someone to pet me, to make much of Me, to motllol* ins ; and I was always alone— quite alone. If J or ha ve passed over into the mean if all of me, if the sentient world, I should hove jirriednnkr.„F.dnar'ed or nothing, craved for nt.".lting ; but whether my Own craving to comfort my mother, whether my mother's yearning for me had scitived to make me earth-bound, I do not know, 1 only know that one by one those that I had known and trusted and loved vanished away from the old house, and I was left alone. I did not count the poor knave who had been hanged a couple of hundred years before the day on Al hid) I tripped and fell down the great stairs. lie and I had ot king in com- mon. He wits earth-bourn d too, and he never ventured to address me unlei,s I passed the time of day with hint. So two hundred years went by. Yes, I know that it was two hundred years, because one day I was wandering disconsolately iii the great pic- ture gallery, and I heard one of the daughters of my house explaining ray portrait. " She was called D,irolhy .Trench,'' she was saying. " She was killed, •you know, at the foot of the stairs two hundred years ago. That was why they took down the dog gates, but the stanchions are there still, and the gates we gave to the church. They stand on either side of the chancel."</p>
        <p>" Then you were called after her "i" ead one of her beaters.</p>
        <p>" Yes, I was called Dorothy Trench after her. I often tell my people it was an unlucky omen, because I have grown up so much like her." " She was very lovely,'' said the other. " Oh, you mustn't say that. You might think me begging the question. You couldn't help giving that answer, but I dread the fate that may be in store for me. I have a feeling that I, too, shall die young, that I, too, shall come to my end by accident, as she did. I go down stairs at. wa3rs with my hand on the balustrade. I would rest my other hand on the wall, only the stairs are so wide that I cannot reach to both. I often co and look at my little ancestress, the other Dorothy Trench, but I wish they had milled me after any- one else." - Then they passed on down the long gallery, stopping here and there to look at the different portraits, joking and laughing between themselves; and finally they all went downstairs into the great hl below. All, did I say? No, my neinesad,dDorolliy, and her attendant cavalier, 1616.ing so different to what he would have looked in my day, lingered behind and wandered some- how adown the great gallery once more. I fol- lowed—no, I didn't follow .exactly, for I linked my arm in hers, feeling that at last I had got an interest in life, feeling that at last I had found someone who loved me, someone who would be interested in me. someone who wouldn't be</p>
        <p>frightened if I could materialtFe and show myself, I, in my habit as I usod to be. They used to call it " playing gooseberry " in my day ; it's a timid:loss task. Those two took no more heed of zoo than if I had been the air through which we walked ; and yet I was my- self, tangible, warm, palpitating. I had my pretty blue gown on ; my hair tied up with a blue ribbon. As we passed a great looking-glass I saw myself —Oh, so like the Dorothy whose sun I held. We might have been twin rosebuds on a single stem ; :nal yet, they took no heed of me.</p>
        <p>I left them at last. I could not bear to hear the lovers' thnder whisperiTlgs. I could not bear to see her half shy, half coquettish upward glances.</p>
        <p>" You should have no fear," be said, " the other lady Dinothy never had a lover by aI ac- counts. You have taken your fate into your own hands. Your lino of life will be turned away from the old house very soon now. History does not repeat itself in every detail, and if it did—if it did, I don't think, my Dorothy, that I should live to bear my sorrow very long. I don't think I could go on for thirty, forty, fifty years, as the mother of that poor little girl did. I should make a short and sharp end of it." " Oh," she shuddered, " how cold it is, Geof- frey. Let us go down to the hall ; it will be warmer there."</p>
        <p>I had not the heart to follow them. I stayed behind and looked at my portrait. Then I went and gazed at myself in the great sheet of looking- glass, and then I wandered away to a dark cor- ner that I knew of, and I wept. Oh, the tears that poor spirits weep ! There is no comfort in them ; they give no relief. In the spirit world tears are not the emblems of hope. Yet even as I sat and wept, hope did come to me, a ray of light crept in upon my earth-bound brain. If I could meet with some human sym- pathy I might be released from these centuries of weary standing still, and I might pass over to that other world where I should find those who must have been seeking me for nigh upon two hundred years. The question was bow could I touch the great fount of human, living sym- pathy ? I had heard, by what had fallen from the other Dorothy Trench's lips, that she was inter- ested in me. She was sorry, grieved, when she thought of the sad story of my suddenly ended youth.. If I could once materialise myself in her presence and could establish communication with her, the rest would be easy. I had not very long, a matter of a few years. possessed the power of materialising myself. I felt that day that if I could only find Dorothy's own room, I should accomplish my task easily. But I had seen Doro- thy and other members of the family disappear into a comparatively modern wing of the house, one into which I had never ventured to intrude. I felt quite nervous as I bent my footsteps thither; I had not the most remote idea which was Doro- thy's chamber. The air felt different, unaccus- tomed. I didn't know my way. I tried to step across the threshold of the long corridor which gave upon the new wing. No, I was frightened. I drew back, and then I caught sight of a light at a little distance. Oh, I knew where that came from.</p>
        <p>I went down the stairs, shivering as I passed over the place where the dog gates had bung. I crossed the great hall, and I pushed open the door of what used to be c4lled " 7ely Ladyes' Parlour." I don't think that I had been in that room for a century. It was quite different. I remembered very well, a long time ago, going down to see if my mother was there. I found a strange lady with a Spanish looking face, and the room that had used to be decked in dainty chintz, and the wall painted pink, was hung with rich deep King-cup yellow. I remember so well that I turned with a cry and fled, and I heard as I passed the portal a petulant voice saying " Oh, what draughts there are in this old house !"</p>
        <p>Yet the change was even greater now than that of a century ago. The walls were pale vivid green, the curtains a deep rich crimson, the chairs ;tad couches all covered in scarlet leather. It was a hard room for a lady's parlour. There v‘ere curiolls old engravings On the walls, and a ;.crcat round table littered with papers, and a man sitting in a. big ihriir, smoking,. I stood ittite mimed, when suchlmily be put down Ii is paper tel ktitrist at me with a look of the most iutetise astouishimmit.</p>
        <p>" L:idy I)urotity ho said. sni:led. It %vas so delicious to be spoken k Pjr iii name,. . _ „</p>
        <p>" Vi711:1 21 re yen (loirig (town here ? Do you me., !he ?" lie glaueed at the elook cli tht• ehhar.oy -holf. I looked at it, too. It perm*, 10 the hour of three.</p>
        <p>The man ;rot np. " Yon ou...titt to he in bed;' ho said. " What rill Killronpion say f"' "If ilhampton What eilmitld he say ?" Then I stretched out my haml. " Oh, you don't un- derstand, you don't understand. I have come o you for Sy 11/PU thy." "To me —to we F My God ! Lady Dorothy, an back, go back." He turned and put out a hand to warn me off. "Go back to your room. Forget that you came in here at all. Everyone has gone to bed ; nobody will know. This is madness." " Oh, no," I said, " no. It is new life." I moved towards him. He edged atty. " Oh," I bait), in a frenzy of terror that after my years of loneliness I had been brought into t,mclt with a warm, living, palpitating human ising to no purpose, " Oh, don't go away. You eoold not he so cruel. You don't understand. !Tow shall you 2 I have been waiting cen• (cries for this moment."</p>
        <p>".L;nly Dorothy," be said, and he spoke in ,n‘l a stern tone that I shivered where I stood, " you don't understand what madness this is. It ICllltantpton —" " Kithampton is here," said another voice. _ . . _</p>
        <p>I turned. At the • door stood the young man that I had seen in the gallery with the other Dorothy. He looked at me with eyes which made tny blood freeze in my veins with terror. " I heard everything that you said," he went tm. " I was not eavesdropping. I came down again to write a note to you, that I might send it to you early in the morning. I have no need to write it now. I must apologise, Lady Dorothy, for having made you the recipient of my unwel- come attentions." I lifted my hand to' my throat. I tried to soeaii —I did, not understand what he meant. Tin o ords died on my lips. Oh, how strange people are now that I have come in touch with theto itzaitt ! A great wave of feeling came surging over me that I, who had longed for two big weary centuries to have touch once more with the living, had better have remained in the eon,- piny of tile poor knave in the western wing. The man called Kilhampton turned to the other 711:01 whom I had found in the MOM. " Mur- ray," he said, " I must do you the justice to say hat I beard every word that passed between you, told I must exonerate you from all blame in 'this matter. At the same time, Lady Dorothy, to far as I am concerned, is free from this int, wont."</p>
        <p>The man called Murray made a step forwaro "Killomptori, I swear to you—" he began. " You needn't swear. I believe you withoni it. Lady Dorothy, I will bid you farewell." There was an instant's pause. Oh, my heal heal so hard it choked the words that came twill,- hag to my unaccustomed lips. " I see," he went on, " that you have d,,. ,•arded the ring that I gave you. You wil Imitless scud it to Inc in the morning." He never waited for my reply ; he turned and went. And I stood there not comprehending, imt exactly understanding—Oh, no, not tlw:, understanding at all. " Don't vela think. Lady Dorothy." sa Murray, " that you had better g• back to yotX room r _</p>
        <p>_ I looked at Lim. I wrung my hands in my dis- tress. "Would you explain to me," I said, " would you explain all that that gentleman said ?"</p>
        <p>_ "That gentleman? Lord Kilhampton ?" "Is he Lord Kilhampton ? I don't know. / have seen him about the house. He spoke as if he knew me. He said he had given me a ring. I have no ring. Look i" And I put out my hands to show him that there was no jewel of any kind encircling any one of my fingers. "He never gave you a ring? Oh, yes, Lady Dorothy, indeed be did, I can vouch for it, for I t,aw it the first day that you wore it."</p>
        <p>"But you have never seen me before," I cried. "I • have never seen you before My dear Lady Dorothy—oh, this must be looked into. Let me go and rouse your father and mother. Let me fetch Kilhampton back again. She must be walking in her sleep," he said. He spoke as if to himself.</p>
        <p>"I am not walking in my sleep. You cannot fetch my father and mother. They've been dead two hundred years and more." " This is horrible !" he exclaimed He was already at the door. " De you mind wait hug here until I return ?" " Yes, I will wait." He seemed to be gone some few minutes. You know we poor ghosts have no great knowledge of time.</p>
        <p>Presently I heard them coming back. I heard Murray's eager voice. " Oh, out of her senses, my dear fellow. She's walking in her sleep ; she's not rational. I thought she was until you</p>
        <p>had gone out or the room. Nhe says her rather and mother have been dead two hundred years." I turned and locked at them as they came in. Lord Kilhampton came straight across the room to me. " Dorothy," he said, "you don't feel well." "Yes, I feel as I always do. I was so happy when I found—that—l could speak and hold communication with—with this gentleman. But be says you gate me a ring. You never gave me a ring. I never had a ring in my life." " Indeed I did give you a ring last week, Dorothy:" __ _</p>
        <p>"No." I held up my band to show him that it was ringless. He caught at it. "That is not Dorothy's hand. Look, you know her so well, Murray. The fingers are quite different. She had a long third finger. This is a short one. Look—the shape of the nails, the colour, the size. Who are you F" " I am—l ain—l was Dorothy Trench once." " Dorothy, Trench ! Not my Dorothy Trench ? It's not the Caine hand, Murray. I tell you it isn't the same band. And her clothes—Dorothy hasn't got a dress like this. This is a fancy dress."</p>
        <p>"Lady Dorothy was wearing a blue dress last night at dinner." " Yes, a blue dress. Not like this, not this col- our. It isn't the same. Who are you ?" " I told you I was Dorothy Trench once." "But who are you non . "I suppose I am Dorothy Trench now." . "What do you mean by your father and mother having been dead for two hundred years ?" "Oh, don't you understand? I was that Dorothy Trench who was killed at the foot of the , stairs there. I have been wandering about this house unseen, unsuspected for two hundred years. I can't get over to the other side, where my near , relations are, and so I have been hovering about 1 for two hundred years until to-night. And then I came down here—l think I came to look for the ' Dorothy Trench that lives now, the girl I saw you walking with in the gallery only to-day, the girl who was talking about me. Don't you un- derstand ?"</p>
        <p>" You don't mean that you are a ghost ?" " Well—l—l think I am myself. I am not dead, because I am here ; I am not alive, because nobody has seen me for two hundred years, and I thought this gentleman," stretching out my hand towards the other man, "I thought this gentleman would help me. I never knew till to- day why I was so lonely, why I could not go where all my near relations went when they left this planet. I know now, though I don't ex- actly know bow the knowledge came to me that I have been earth-bound. You see, I was 'tilled —I mean—l can't explain it, but if somebody would love me just a little, it would help me to get out of this terrible condition, and to go on- ward to my own people." His face changed. " Nay," be said, "that seems a pity. Since you have come, can't you stay altogether ' The suggestion broke the spell. With a cry I turned and fled back to the gallery where I had lived in loneliness for two long centuries. I sped straight to the place where a desk stood. Yes, there was pen, ink and paper. Rut the time had gone L. I could not trace a single word. I felt weak, exhausted, powerless, and some strange voice came to me saying—" Rest for a little while, Dorothy Trench. Later on the power will come, so that you can write down your strange story, unit then you will rest again, and in that time of peace you will be wafted over to the ether side."</p>
        <p>I</p>
