      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>• It cannot be pointed out too strongly to racing motor- has that tho main roads leading out of big cities aro not the right places to choose whereon to test the speed of their racing cars,particularly when the time selected is during the hours on Saturdays and Sundays when such thoroughfares are always thronged with cyclists and motorists.</p>
        <p>The Great North and the Holyhead Roads are very favourite selections with these who are anxious for a little training spin en a high-powered racer ; but, unless this can be done at night-time or in the very early hours of the worning, the proceeding is distinctly un- fair to other users of the highway. It is absurd to argue that the remedy lies with the police. The police are powerless to apply it, for the speed of such races is usually far too fast for anyone to be able to take the number, whilst the idea of stopping the driver of a 70 h.p. which is going "all out " by the raising of the august official hand has only to be mentioned in order to be ridiculed.</p>
        <p>One almost feels disposed to suggest that upon a fine Saturday or Sunday the main reads ought to be closed both to novices and racers, for both are a nuis- ance to everyone else. But when the two meet, as they sometimes do, on a narrow stretch of road, it is the ' novice who needs praying for. His attempts tosget ofT the earth would be ludisrous if he were not in such deadly earnest. It seldom seems to occur to him that, when feeling at all nervous over the matter of steering past another vehicle, the safest and easiest thing to do is to stop dead and leate the onus of steering upon the approaching car. This is, of course, assuming that he is keeping strictly to the left. If he has been bowling along in the centre with an occasional dive towards the oft side, as is very customary with a novice who just knows enough to be a terror to every other user of the road, he had better draw in gently to his own side and there rest until the cause of his fright has passed him by.</p>
        <p>That always practical paper, the " Autoear," has rendered excellent service to motorists generally by procuring and publishing in its columns, the opinions a amateur riders concerning the utility of "reliability trials "from the point of view of the man who is about to buy a car. A few of the correspondents frankly acknowledge that from the actual buyers' standpoint, they see no use in such trials, at at present organised. The expense involved in such undertakings is, of course, tremendous, and fot all such heavy outlay the clients eventually pay.</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact, we ourselves consider that these trials have now outlived theirusefulness. In times gone by, there were undoubtedly instrumental in strengthen- ing the wavering confidence of the awe-strickee amateur who was then most familiar with the motor-car in a dismembered condition by the roadside ; but in the present year of grace serious trouble with the engine on the road is seldom met with. Besides, where will this craze for long distance reliability runs stop ? It five thousand miles can be accomplished without real trouble—and the Siddeley has recently proved that this is possible—why not ten, or twenty ? Wky,indeed,stop at all, before the engine drops to pieces, en masse, or the driver dies from old age on his perch?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</head>
