      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.,</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.,</p>
        <p>--- NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENT. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases sent their names and addresses to the Editor i as a guarantee of good faith. Matters intended for publication must be written on aute only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not 1 ater than Thursday. Councillor Stott and the Next Election. ! Sir,—My letter to you of the 11th inst. has brought a long, rambling reply from one "Fairplay." I am I afraid I cannot assist him in solving such perplexing problems, and lam not altogether sure whether to attempt to follow him in his wanderings would be help- ful to anyone. He asks me several questions, however, which I will briefly answer. First : " Has not Mr. Stott a perfect right to help whom he likes ? " Answer: Certainly not. A person may like another, but may entirely disagree with the objects of the party to which he belongs. He sacrifices the good of the individual for the benefit of the comm unity. It is very Maly that there will be a straight fight in Warrington between a Liberal and a Tory—A free Trader and a Protectionist. Can any man conscientiously say that because a Free Trader likes an individual who represents Protection that he must forego his principles to " help whom he likes." Second: " Now, why should Mr. Stott, who belongs to the Labour Party, be expected to give his support to the Liberal cause any more than you would a Conserva- tive, or vice versa ?" " Fairplay " would do well to consult any " primer "on Political Economy and, first . of all, learn something of the elements of Government. Third: "Well,' Is.unqualm; whet will the Liberals of Warrington say if some of the Tory majority of the last election vote Liberal ? " I should imagine that they would think that they got over the "Mafficking " of the war and that they resented the introduction of Chinese Labour into South Africa. It may be that even Conservative workingmen would prefer a big loaf instead of a little one. Now my very good friend "Fairplay," when you tell us that in the embodiment of the whole Labour move- ment. . . lies the true Christianity," you are teaching us something. It may be that I have either read my Bible wrong, or else the " Clarion " is only joking when it pretends to be serious. Perhaps " Fairplay ' will point out to us more clearly what he means by that statement. I do not condemn any movement which has ideals as " Fairplay "would have us believe the Labour move- ment possesses. I only regret that such individuals, as 4" Fairplay " for instance, should know so little of what they profess to teach,—Yonrs truly. NEINQUALM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Mr. Bonar Law and the Question of</head>
        <p>Mr. Bonar Law and the Question of</p>
        <p>_ Wages. Sir.—l was privileged in hearing the address of Mr. Bonar Law at the Parr Hall, last Friday. It was a calm and reasonable presentiment of the tariff reformers, case, and although in my opinion the case is a deplor- ably bad one, this is no reason why I should not ack- nowledge the pleasure experienced in listening to a speech, admirable alike in spirit and in manner. There appear to me to be several faults in the reasoning, and a total ignoring of theadvantages which we derive from a Free Trade policy, but I have no intention to dwell on these. I should not have asked for any of your space, but for the last words used by Mr. Law, and which contain a kind of challenge to Free Traders on a point which, with monotonous reiteration, is advanced in the public utterances of every tariff reformer. The challenge springs out of Mr. Law's reference to the import of steel goods upon which, if made in this country,it was stated about six millions would have been paid in wages. Mr. Law said :—" Their opponents said they had got something else in exchange. He would say to them now,and would ask that meeting to say to them. "Prove it, tell us, and tell us in a way we can understand what we have got in exchange for these wages,which have undoubtedly been lost.'" (Loud cheering.) Let me take up the challenge. International trade is international barter. Cash plays a very small part in this barter, and is useful more as a means of measur- ing values than as a means of payment. The imported iron is paid for not in sovereigns, but by exports of goods, or in service, of which wages form the principal item. This is made easy by the international banking system whereby the iron is paid for not in cash, but by draft which is sent to Germany, paid into German banks, or to brokers, who clear or balance the drafts against drafts in Berlin for goods bought by Germans from English firms. For such goods wages have been paid in England, which are the set off against the wages which would have been paid if the iron in question had been made in England. This is very elementary, as ray friend Mr. Willcox would say, but it as true as it is elementary, and I think it ought to conform to Mr. Laws condition to "tell us in a way we can understand" what we have got in exchange. If you tax:this imported iron until it is kept out, you at once set up a barrier against our exports, nnd make it harder for our exporters to find a market. This thin culty manifests itself in the movement in the rate of exchange between the two countries. Suppose you tax German goods coming to this country to such an extent as to keep them out, and Germany goes on taking our goods as heretofore, the result would be that drafts on Berlin for goods sold could not be cleared, or balanced, by drafts on London for goods bought, then Germany would have to pay in gold. But we do not need gold, its excess, as in all other commodities, would lessen its value to us. The Bank of England would lower its rate to get rid of the surplus, the German bank would raise its rate to guard its reserve. The relation between the sovereign and the mark would alter. Everything measured by the sovereign would get dearer, or to put it in another way the sovereign would get cheaper, and the mark dearer. This would have the effect of making it easier for us to buy German goods, and make it more difficult for the Germans to buy from us. This would continue until ola buying from Germany, or Germany's ceasing to sell to us, righted the exchange between the two countries. In this illustration I have, to make the matter clear, dealt only with trade between the two countries. The prin- ciples are the same however many countries are trading together, the only difference being that our exports to one country need not be paid for directly by our imports from the same. Imports from Germany may be paid by exports to India, but paid for they must be, and our exports bring more wealth and well-being, than if we tried our- selves to make those things which we can get better by exchange. The correctness of this argument is estab- lished by the statement in the first Government Memoranda page 97. If this be true how absurd it is to talk as Messrs Chamberlain, Law and Bindloss do about the loss of wages, as though it were national loss. I grant and I that freely, that while the nation gains 'and gains more than any other, in throwing wide open the door for all the bargains the world has to offer,this entails displace- ment of labour at certain points, and a call for labour at others. For example if se import blooms and billets so cheap that the trades that use them prosper in consequence of the cheap raw material, and are enabled to assist home industries with cheaper goods and to export to better advantage than any tariff burdened country, the advantage is to a certain extent discounted by the consequent depression of those industries engaged in making similar goods to those imported. This entails a transference of labour from one industry to another which causes a certain amount of inconvenience and temporary hardship. It is a question as Mr. Law says of advantage versus disad- vantage "and he would be a bold man who essayed to prove that from a national standpoint the country was not a gainer by a wide open door for all that English- men care to buy of the best value the world can offer. I would respectfully suggest to Tariff Reformers that if they desire to think imperially they must learn to think Nationally. At prevent in considering only their own trades, they think parochially, sectionally, and whether they realize it or not, selfishly. Luton thinks in straw hats,l Greenwich in sugar refining and Sugar Conventions, 1 Rochester in cement. The hope of the tariff reform 11 movement lies in the ability of the speakers to induce ' Their hearers to shut their eyes to th 3 national advan- tages, and especially to the advantages of the consumers, and to magnify out of all true proportion to the national gain the effect of foreign competition on isolated in dustries. Protection has been lately well described by one of our leather men as "the insecure refuge for incapacity, and the delusive hope of decrepit industries." This may be a little rough, but it will be a grave misfortune if particular trades strive to achieve prosperity by legislative action which will enable them for a time until the spending power be crippled to enrich themselves at the expense of their customers,and cease to depend on their own adaptability, energy, and skill, and turn a blind eye to the general good. CHARLES E. PARKER. i Penketh, March 23, 1905.</p>
        <p>Auctioneer's Claim for Damages. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, Mr. Robert Lowe, auctioneer, Bank-street, brought an action to recover from Mr. Parkinson, hay and straw dealer, Union-street, .£3l damages for injuries and loss of business occasioned he alleged by the negli- gence of the defendant's servant. Plaintiff was represented by Mi. Unswortb, Mr. Browne appearing for the defendant. Plaintiff's case was that on the lrith of December he was proceeding along Union- street, when he stumbled and fell over the shafts of a lurry which he alleged had been left in a dangerous position by the defendant's servant. The result of the accident was that he suffered considerable pain and also a financial loss through not being able to attend properly to his business. In answer to his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands Mr. Lowe said he saw the lorry hut expected that the shafts would be fastened up. The defence, with which his honour agreed, was that having seen the lurry Mr. Lowe was i guilty of contributory negligence n not taking .steps to avoid any possible accident. Defendant paid the sum of £8 14s. fid. into court with a denial of lia- lility and upon that sum being handed to Mr. Lowe lagment was given for the defendant with costs,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>LARGE INCREASE OF PATIENTS.</p>
        <p>Additional Hon. Medical Officer to be Appointed.</p>
        <p>In order to cope with the increasing number of patients who avail themselves of the skilled treatment afforded at the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary, an additional hon. medical officer is to be appointed. This course was rastived upon at the annual meeting of governors of the institution which was held at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon. The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) presided over a fair attendance ani was supported by the hon. treasurer (Mr. G. Dakin) and the secretary (Mr. J. H. J. Hampson). THE YEAR'S WiiIRK.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the Board cg Management afforded striking evidence of the splendid work which the institution is doing. There has been an increase in the number of patients treated in the house of 32, the average number of beds occupied being 344 as against 30.81, and the average cost per bed was £62 14s. Id., as against £66 ss. fid. in the preceding year. The follow- ing statement shows the numbers, for the last three years, of patients who received treatment at the Infirmary and Dispenary -- - •</p>
        <p>• 1902. 003. 1904. Hospital In-patients 483 ... 490 ... 522 Home Patients Out-patients Accidents 2891 ... 2798 ... 3202</p>
        <p>7,262 7,620 10,103 j The figures of the year ending 31st December, 1904, " 'detest • . ' -otal increase</p>
        <p>are the highest yet reached, and represent a tott, of 2,183 patients on the figures of the previous year. FINANCE. -IrcU</p>
        <p>been £3,1:49</p>
        <p>The receipts from ordinary sources have 7s. 4d., as against £3,077 12s. Gd. last year. The pay- ments, including the debit balance brought forward, have been £3,723 12s. 9d., as against £4,121 us. 3d. last year. The year under review began with a debit balance due to the expenditure and income account of £43 18s. 9d.; it closed with an increased debit balance of £574 ss. sd. In explanation of a debit balance due to the expendi- ture and income account, it should be stated that in the j early part of the year the Board, having a large credit j at the bank, decided to invest £350 with the Corpora- tion of Warrington, so that the real debit balance is £224 is. sd. In addition to a special expenditure of £ll7 Ms. 6d. on electric lift and lighting installation, j the extraordinary increase of over 2,400 in the number of patients treated during the year, has caused a largely increased expenditure, and has converted what under normal conditions would have been a credit balance into a balance on the wrong side of the account. There have been additions to the endowment fund by ' legacies from the late Miss Annie Robinson of £5, and Mrs. Mary Marrow of £226 16s. id. The number of Life Governors has been increased by the addition of the names of Messrs. &amp;gar Owen and James Dodds. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>MEDICAL CUMI.IITTEE'S REPORT. MORE ACCOMMODATION WANTED.</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL CUMI.IITTEE'S REPORT. MORE ACCOMMODATION WANTED.</p>
        <p>The Medical Committee in their report called special attention to the increased number of patients. Many medical cases were, of necessity, treated out- side the hospital, although they ought, with sufficient accommodation, to have been admitted into the wards, and many cases were now awaiting admission which should be received. On only too many occasions the hospital accommodation had been proved inadequate and there were no beds available. The increased work was shown in every department. In operations alone there were 218, as against 163 in the previous year—an increase of 55. Amongst home patients there was an increase of 1,187, and in accidents (treated as out- patients) of 404. The total number of accidents and emergencies reached 3,202. This is about ten times the number of such cases twenty-five years ago. The deficient accommodation for the nursing staff had repeatedly been brought before the Board, and to emphasise this the committee called attention to the fact that with three nurses sleeping in one room (a most unsanitary and undesirable arrangement), one nurse contracted scarlatina, thus rendering the whole dormitory of three beds unavailable. The lodging of nurses ' outside the hospital, as now obtained, was also directly against modern views of sanitationand efficient manage- ment. Though regretting the necessity of such an undesirable report as the preceding, the Committee con- gratulated the hospital on its increased usefulness and work. This also showed the necessity for an increase in the staff to cope with the increased work. As a special subject the Committee reported that the X-Ray treatment had been found to have given most satisfactory results. There had been 326 exposures daring theyear, and though this entailed much extra work on the resident staff, and some expense to the hospital, a commensurate benefit had accrued to the patients. The Medical Committee wished in conclusion, to ' express their satisfaction with the manner in which the resident medical officers, Dr. Hodgkinson and Dr. Leigh, had discharged their very responsible duties. The CHAIRMAN moved the adoption of the reporti and briefly commented upon the more important features. He boped the collections at the churches to-morrow (Hospital Sunday) would amount to at least £3OO. They had to thank the workingmen very much for the excellent efforts they made to support the insti- tution. (Hear, hear.). The Rev. Jolts YONGE seconded. With regard to the income from Hospital Sunday, it did not, he thought, commend itself to the governors as being as good as it, might be. They had, of course, been passing through a series of years of financial difficulty. Trade had been slack and wages low; but they hoped there was now an improvement and that they might confi- dently expect a considerable increase in this year's Hospital Sunday collections. It was a matter for con- gratulation that the workingmen responded so well to the claims of the hospital. Since the workingmen took a distinct and separate part in the support of the institu- tion they had raised no less than £13,000 which was eminently satisfactory. (Hear, hear.) The RECTOa pointed out that since 1894 the number of patients had almost doubled, whilst the expense was only one third more. The report was adopted. ADDITIONAL HON. MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>Dr. Fox moved :—That rule 50 be altered so as to read : "No gentleman shall be eligible as an Honorary Medical Officer who is not legally registered to practise Medicine and Surgery, and who has not practised at least one year within the Borough."l(The period speci- fied previously was three years) ; also" That owing to the onerous increase in Out-Patient and other work the appointment of an additional Honorary Assistant Medical Officer should be made." Dr. Fox said the work had increased so enormously in the out-patient department as to necessitate more assistance. i Councillor Jolts ROBERTS seconded and the motion was carried. The further consideration of the matter was left with the Board of Management. ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of ttae-fee;.-i-V. iii:CAOECAMP, seconded by the Rev. C. HARVEY Coox, Mr. George Dakin was re-elected treasurer. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. H. COOK remarked that the reading of the annual report had to him been a matter of deep pathos, and suggeited the question of whether it was not time we gave more attention to the study of public health and disease. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Jackson Holmes MIS re-elected hon. auditor, and the following hon. medical officers were also re-elected :—Hon. Consulting Medical Officer, Mr. E. A. Fox; HonVedical Officers,Mr. G. W. Joseph,Mr. C. E.Richmond, and Mr. Ernest E. Bowden ; Hon. Assist- ant Medical Officers, gr. R. Smith and Mr. E. J. Fox. At a later stage of the meeting Mr. J. Charlton Parr was re-elected president, and the tfollowing vice-presi- dents were re-elected :—Mr. Thomas Henry Lyon, The Rev. Canon Willis, M.A. Mr. Frederick Monks, Mr. Charles White, Mr. John White, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Messrs. George Dakin, John Fairclongh, Ziba Armitage,Roger Charlton Parr, Arthur H. Crosaeld, and W.*Peter Rylands. Mr. JonN FAIRCLOIIGIT suggested that the Rev. John Yonge's name should be added to the list of vice- presidents as he had done a very good work for the institution. ' - '</p>
        <p>The Ret. W. BRACECAMP seconded and the proposi- ti on was heartily approved. In returning thanks the REV. JOHN YONGE said he had been nearly twenty nine years in Warrington and during the whole of that time he had in some way been connected with the management of the Infirmary. KS.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN WHITE moved a vote of thanks to the Board of Management, House Visitors Finance Com- mittee, Ladies' Committee, Hospital Sunday Committee and Ballrommittee. Mr. L. Wain seconded, and the motion was agreed to. The Workingmen's Committee was accorded hearty thanks on the proposition of Mr. C. WrrrrE, seconded by the Rev. Father WHITTLE, the hope being expressed that the Committee would soon attain its ambition by raising .El,OOO per annum. Councillor ROBERTS briefly returned thanks, and said the Committee which commenced with a membership of ten or a dozen now comprised from 120 to 130 represen tatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BOARD OF MANAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE BOARD OF MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>lem‘</p>
        <p>.-140/0</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. F. MONKS seconded by Mr. W Fletcher the following were elected members of the Board of Management: Revs. Fl. V. Pigot, E. C. E. Carleton,iFather J. P. Whittle, W. Bracecamp, and G. H. Carnson. Messrs. T. Birchen, H. Thornton, J. D. Backton, G. Thompson, W. J. Forshaw, C. Jackson-Holmes, John Roberts, Thomas Burton, C. M. Watkins and I'. A. Wilkinson (the four last-named representing the Workingmen's Committee). Votes of thanks to the Mayor for the use of the Council Chamber and the Rector for presiding con- cluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>4,</p>
        <p>- • , . ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 25, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>LIBERALS ORGANIZING.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS ORGANIZING.</p>
        <p>JOINT MEETING OF WHITECROSS &amp; ST. AUSTIN'S WARD COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>Interesting Speeches.</p>
        <p>The work of organization proceeds apace in the Liberal ranks, and encouraging evidence of the enthusiasm with which the preparations for the forth- coming contest are being entered upon was afforded at the annual social gathering in connection with the Whitecross and St. Austin's Ward Committees which was held in Thewlis-street Schools on Saturday even- kg. A. large company sat down to tea, the catering being in the hands of Councillor James Evans. Afterwards an interesting meeting was held, Councillor Tiunion presiding. The attendance included Councillors Evans, Brotigh, Burton and Barton, and Mr. Henry Roberts. Tha CHATRMAN said he considered that Whitecross and St. Austin's were undoubtedly the best wards in the town. - (Hear, hear.) They had the best representatives in the Council—(hear, hear, and laughter)—and they had also the advantage of possessing an enthusiastic body of workers in the Liberal cause. (Applause). Matters affecting organization were then discussed, several suggestions being offered. The representatives of the ward in the Town Council afterwards delivered brief addresses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>COUNCILLOR EVANS AND MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S PROSPECTIVE VISIT.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR EVANS AND MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S PROSPECTIVE VISIT.</p>
        <p>Councillor EVANS began his speech with an allusion to the Conservative meeting held in the Parr Hall on Friday evening. He was told by those who were present that it was not a very enthusiastic gathering, but one or two remarkable things were said. Personally he had no hard feelings against their friend Mr. Pierpoint, and their only differences were on political matters- The member had let the electors of Warrington know clearly where he stood regarding two important items in the next programme. He was ready to express his opinion upon the fiscal question, before he knew what that question was. (Hear, hear.) He (Councillor Evans) thought events had proved that Mr. Pierpoint had an idea that the whole world was going to follow Joseph—(laughter)—but he had since found that he was mistaken. It was stated that Mr. Chamberlain was going to visit Warrington again. He (Councillor Evans) hoped he would do the same for the Tories as he did for the Liberals when he came to War- rington before. If he did, the Liberals would come out of the election with flying colours. (Applause.) Mr. Chamberlain fairly sold them, and he (Councillor Evans) hoped he would sell the Tories. So far as he could judge, the probability was that that would be the ease. There could be no mis- take as to how Mr. Pierpoint intended to deal with Irish politics; and he (Councillor Evans) hoped his Irish friends would bear that in mind. The speaker went on to advise his hearers to work earnestly and to" keep their powder dry,"and he hoped that when the election was over he would have livei to see three Liberals returned for Warrington.(Applause.) He was in the fight when Mr. McNlinnies showed them how to win, and he hoped he would be in it when Mr. Arthur Crosfleld was returned as member for 'Warrington. (Applause.) Councillor Evans went on to speak of the ill-effects of Protection, in the shape of high prices for food, which he himself experienced in his younger days and asked his audience to remain true to the principles of the grand men who had passed away and who, in their life-time, were in the fore-front of the battle for progress and freedom. What should be dealt with first of all in this country was the land question, and if that were effectively tackled great benefits would accrue. Councillor Evans proceeded to deal with municipal matters and to urge the importance of keeping an eye on public expenditure, so that money should not be wasted. He urged the desirability of an alteration in the conditions of " compounding " in regard to the erection of property which, he believed, would considerably increase the income of the borough in a short time. He thought there should be a bath in every workingman's house. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>COUNCILLOR BURTON AND TETE TAXATION OF FOOD.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR BURTON AND TETE TAXATION OF FOOD.</p>
        <p>Councillor BURTON said that he and his fellow repre- mtatives of St. Austin's and Whitecross Wards had always done their best in regard to questions affecting the working-classes, and had endeavoured to further the interests of that section af the community. He urged the ward committees to take up the work of organiza- tion with all the enthusiasm of which they were capable. Alluding to the fiscal proposals, he considered it would be a shameful thing to tat the bread of the English people to benefit the inhabitants of the colonies who had something to spare already.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SPEECH BY COUNCILLOR BARTON.</head>
        <p>SPEECH BY COUNCILLOR BARTON.</p>
        <p>Councillor BARTON said his experience in political life dated from the 1868 election when he recorded his vote on the right side, and he had never regretted it. (Hear, hear.) Everything they valued and placed a high estimate upon to-day had come from the party which they served. He hoped they would have the pleasure when they met for their next annual social of waving the flag of victory on the return of their good friend Mr. Crosileld. (Applause). Though they might differ sometimes on little matters, he was proud to see they were at one on the great question of the day, and he hoped they would continue united. Let them stand true to their principles and do their best for their native town. (Applause.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>COUNOILL- OR BROUGT-1 AND LOCAL EXPENDITURE.</head>
        <p>COUNOILL- OR BROUGT-1 AND LOCAL EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>Councillor BRO1:GH in a short address asked his hearers to brighten their armour for the contest which was probably near at hand. It was patent to everyone that the Government would hold together as long as they had a thread to hold to, but in the very order and nature of things he did not think they could hold together much longer. The thread was bound to be broken and they themselves would tumble to pieces. If there was one thing he was more anxious to see than any other, it was to have their good friend Hr. Crosfleld as the representative of the town in Parliament and Mr. Roberts once more at the head of the Council.(Applause) He had not one unkind thing to say about the member or his followers, but he believed the Liberals had the best cause and that, in Mr. Crostield, they had the best candidate. (Applause). The name of Crosfleld was respected throughout the town and he had no doubt, if all Liberals worked enthusiastically, as to the result of the next election. His opinions in regard to municipal life had undergone a mighty change. He used to think that any man who desired to be good and to do good should keep out of the Council chamber. His opinions in that direction had very much altered. They wanted the best men in the Council that they could get—(applause)—men with clean hands, men who wonld scorn to do a mean act, and he ventured to fissert that such men they had on that platform, as the repre- sentatives of St. Austin's and Whitecross wards. He believed every man who advocated the various schemes launched and the spending of money had done so with the best of motives, and for the interests and good of Warrington. But he considered the time had come to halt a little. He was very pleased to know that the Guardians, thanks in a great measure to the efforts of Messrs. Hickman and Thompson, had been courageous enough to fight against thelaunching of a scheme that would have cost £lO,OOO or £12,000. The rates were high enough, and now was the time to halt. He had made up his mind that he would oppose every scheme for the outlay of large sums, which could possibly be avoided. (Applause). SPEECH BY MR. HENRY ROBERTS. DEFENCE OF FREE TRADE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry ROBERTS, who hada very hearty reception, then delivered a lengthy speech He would like, heisaid, to express his great 'pleasure at seeing such an excellent attendance. A lot of his contemporaries were there that night—men who had grown grey inLiberil service. and he was glad to see they were being supported and seconded by a large number of younger men. Might he say a word or two on MUNICIPAL MATTERS?</p>
        <p>Apart from the merits or —den'i-e-iftS of any question on which the town had been agitated, he thought he was entitled to ask their serious attention to the question as to whether the agitation had resulted in sending into the Council the sort of men whom in ordinary circumstances they trusted with the municipal government of this town. (Cries of" No.") The Chairman had had during the last few days especi- ally to call attention to a matter which, the more it was ventilated and discussed, and the more disgusting it appeared. He was not going to suggest for a moment that the matter to which he referred was in any sense the outcome of a policy on the part of their political opponents. He did not believe the leaders of the Con- servative party in Warrington were men who would allow the party flag to he dragged in the dirt in order to support a party man. The whole scheme had originated in one man's brain, and'had been largely supported by his manner of advocating that kind of thing which he (the speaker) did not attempt to describe. But he did not that when the question of the appointment of any official—he did not care whether he was one of the front rank men or one of the second or third—became a mere question of betting it was a pity. If municipal govern- ment in Warrington was to be made the catspaw of gaining a few shillings or pounds on the subject of a bet, the whole public life of Warrington was degraded, and the sooner it was lifted out of such a filthy slough as that the better. A good deal was being said about high rates. He was very glad indeed to hear Councillor Evans mention in a way which entitled it to its hearty reception, the necessity on common grounds of decency that every workingman's house in the town should be supplied with a bath and a bathroom. He thought the town could well afford on grounds of public health, public morals, and personal decency to give clear water with- out an extra charge for such a sanitary purpose. The times were moving and the position of workingmen would move with the times, at least it would be their own fault if it did not. To the workingmen of the country, for good or evil, the future Government of the country bad been entrusted. The rates, continued Mr.</p>
        <p>1.7.) Roberts, wore high and that brought him b Ick to the question : Did they think they were going to have the town governed in such a way that ttviy were going to secure the highest eiciency with the grettest economy, with the sort of men that the non-trading agitation sent into the Council ? (Cries of " No.") Messrs. Crosfield's works, he believed, were the highest assessed I works in the town. If they were not, they were within a few pounds of it. Their rates I came to nearly £2,000 a year—and that was some- thing to think about. It was of tho greatest importance to the firm and to their work people that the town should not be extravagantly rated. He quite agreed with the policy that as trade generally in the country was not good, whatever expenditure which might be desirable on its own merits, but was not absolutely urgent,should certainly be deferred until a more convenient season. He did not believe Mr. Thompson or any of those who had opposed the Workhouse extension scheme opposed it on its merits, but because they did not consider the time was expedient. There was something a great deal more important than municipal matters , to consider. In municipal affairs, if they made a ' mistake one year they could remedy it when three , years had expired. If they sent the biggest blackguards on the face of the earth into the Council—he did not say they had done anything of the kind—they were only in for three ye trs,and they could repair the mischief as far as getting rid of them was concerned. But the question before the people of this country, which would have to be settled at the next general election, was one that could not be righted, if it were put wrong now, in three years nor in thirty. Every man who was in business knew that if for any reason he diverted any serious part of his trade, if he allowed his opponents to get it, if he was too high and mighty and independent to look after it, it was a frightfully difficult job to get it back again. (Hear, hear.) They were now at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>-_THE PiRTD.iG OF THE WAYS.</head>
        <p>-_THE PiRTD.iG OF THE WAYS.</p>
        <p>They had had a policy of Free Trade for GO years, or nearly so. It was a policy which two men more than all others practically laid down their lives to carry through. It was a policy which gave to their grand- parents, their fathers and mothers, free food. It was a policy which had carried down to the present generation free food, cheap clothing, good wages, and a thousand other things. They had not had the experience, he had not, of knowing what it was to be unable to get sufficient food or decent food. Ihey had escaped all that, and were they going to throw away their birthright, their inheritance, for something worse than a mess of pottage? They would be the biggest fools on the face of the earth if they did. (Applause.) The Liberal Party had been called a divided party, a party without a policy, without a leader; everything in the shape of ridicule and condemnation that could be poured upon their leaders had been poured on them. But what about the Conservative Party ? (Laughter.) He was told that Mr. Chamberlain had consented to come to Warrington shortly. By all means let him comae and let them hear him. If they did not, they must read what he had to say and think about it and apply some commonsense argument to it and he had no fear of the result. If Mr. Chamberlain came to War- rington with the same result that followed his visit a I few years ago when he came on behalf of the Liberal I Party, he (Mr. Roberts) would be devoutly thankful. The Conservative Party was hopelessly divived on the question of Free Trade. They had in the Conserva- tive ranks two parties, three in fact. One was the Con- servative Free-trader and he must say that if there was I a politician to whom he would take off his hat, it was the honest Conservative Free Trade M.P. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>LA.TR LORD SALISBURY</head>
        <p>THE LA.TR LORD SALISBURY</p>
        <p>in regard to Witi;-)m-tlieiw-E-4:e-botinit-e-i€W the greatest admiration and respect, was up to the end of his life a convinced and pronounced Free Trader. Such a thing as Retaliation, Tariff Reform, or Protection hardly dare be mentioned in his presence. He had only been dead a few years, and what was the position of the Conserva- tive Party to day? It was this, one man of strong will, supreme ability—as to his conscientious convictions he would say nothing—ha I put the question of Protection or Tariff Reform in such a way that no other man had ever put it before the people of the country, and he had so used his influence with the Free Trade Conserzative Party that he had practically dominated the members of Parliament who represented Conservatism ; he had secured the Conservative organizatio in Warrington as all over the country, and Protection and Conserva- tism in the main now stood for the same thing. Men like Lord Goschen and Lord Hugh Cecil were being practically driven out of the Conservative Party, unless they would bow the knee to Mr. Chamberlain. He was devoutly thankful that they still had am mgst them on both sides of the political river men who would not sacrifice their conscientious convictions or bow their intellects before a man whose experience and knowledge did not exceed their own, and he said it with profound gratitude that he was glad to think there were still on the Conservative side the best the ablest, and the most experienced men still true to the great cause of Free Treacle. (Applause.) The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>POSITION-6P THE PRIME MINISTER</head>
        <p>POSITION-6P THE PRIME MINISTER</p>
        <p>was pitiable in the extreme. It was pitiable to think that a man to whom was entrusted in such a large measure the control of the destinies of the country should continue to refrain from coming out and saying what was his honest opinion on that important question —a question before which all the other questions, to his (Mr. Roberts's) mind,sank into insignificance. (Applause.) If our trade went, everything else would follow. That Mr. Balfour should still try to make people believe he was a Free Trader on the one hand, and then allow Mr. Chamberlain to say without contradiction that they were agreed on the question was a paradox which he (Mr. Roberts) could not profess to understand. At the samo time he thought it was a great scandal upon the political life of the country that such a thing should be possible. The divided party to-day was not the Liberal party. The Liberal party was united. He (the speaker) was a Nonconformist, and he felt strongly about</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>He did not say that he took up absolutely the platform the most advanced advocates of the Nonconformist position took up. He did not see any reason why, as a result of a conference between Roman Catholic Liberals and Protestant Liberals, some way of arranging to res- pect the religions convictions of the Roman Catholics on the one hand without doing injustice to the conscien- tious objections of Nonconformists on the other hand, should not be brought about. (Hear, hear.) He be- lieved it was practicable, and if it was so it certainly ought to be done. The question of education—great and important as it was—the Nonconformists could safely leave to the treatment of the next Liberal Government. They need not be perpetually talking about it, it would settle itself, in fact no Liberal Government could stand for five minutes that would attempt to deal dishonestly with Nonconformity in this matter. (Applause.) A great deal was said at the meeting at Parr Hall on Friday about</p>
        <p>THE ALIENS HILL. There was no Liberal in the country who would hesitate to vote for a Bill which would exclude dishonest and diseased foreigners, but they were not going to let the Conservative party claim that they alone saw the mis- ehief which followed the indiscriminate introduction of that class of people. At the same time he could not understand the consistency of the Conservative Party in making such a tremendous noise about the shocking state of things which followed the introduction of poor foreigners into this country when they themselves had not simply permitted but had made possible and had sup- ported the importation into British colonies of forty thousand Chinamen to compete with white men. A white man was a white man, whether he was in England or in South Africa. He was a citizen of this great Empire quite as much as if he were at home, and if his interests at home needed protection from the introduc- tion of a proscribed Russian, the wretched miserable Jew and others, what about the white man who had left his home and gone three thousand miles in search of work and found himself put on one side in order that a man with a pigtail could be brought in? (Laughter and applause.) He appealed to them not to be hoodwinked about this matter.He was onelof those Liberals who took a certain position in regard to the war. No one ever heard him express an opinion after he was elected to the principal 101-ace of the town as to the merits of the war in South Africa, either one side or the other. He did not think it was his duty or right to do any such thing. He was bound to occupy a perfectly neutral position, and he claimed that hq did so, but he had always said that inasmuch as we were in for the war, we were bound to see it through at whatever cost, and we did. But, who- ever dreamt for a moment that no less a sum than two hundred and fifty millionpounds would be spent and thirty orlforty thousand British lives sacrificed, ostensibly for the protection of the Empire, in order that the mine- owners might bring in the cheapest possible labour to swell their already overflowing pockets? (Applause.) The position of the Conservative party in trying to Veep out of England miserable half starving Russian peasants and at the same time putting in competition with our friends and brothers in South Africa, Chinamen who would work for a mere pittance was absolutely untenable and illogical If they were to win the election in Warrington it would not be on the question of the candidates at all. The Liberals claimed they had as good a man on their aide as the Conservatives had on theirs. If youth, ability, reading, and study counted for anything—and everyone knew his (Mr. Roberts) personal regard for Mr. Pier- point as a man and as a gentleman—on those grounds with regard to Mr. Crostield they could go one better. (Applause). Alluding to TEI FISCAL QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERTS said that if they sent the Conservative party back again to power one of three things miist happen. We should either stick to Free Trade, which was doubtful, go in for retaliation, which was infinitely more probable, or we should go in for Protection,which was quite possible if Hr. Chamberlain and his party were strong enough. In proof of his assertion that the Conservative party was hopelessly divided on the ques- tion, Mr. Roberts quoted from the letter of the Tariff Reform League in which Mr. Balfour' s retaliation pro- posals were termed a " colourless policy" and strongly I criticised, and also submitted an extract from a speech by Lord Hugh Cecil dealing with the condition of the people in European Protected countries and urging British workers not to depart from the policy of Free Trade. The question of ME SUGAR CONTENTION, continued Mr. Roberts, was a very important as well as a very simple matter, but unfortunately it was not as well understood as it might be. The ladies understood it because they had to pay the piper. There was not a woman in Warrington who did not know perfectly well that she</p>
        <p>had to pay from :7 d . to 11d. per lb. more to-day for sugar than she paid a short time ago. The Sugar Convention was a most remarkable exemplification of the utter incapacity of tae Government to conduct the affairs of the country on business lines. The Government, osten- sibly for the purpose of assisting the West Indian sugar planters, got a Convention called together in order that the sugar countries—Russia, Germany and France— should agree to cease giving bounties upon exported sugar, and we should agree to refuse to receive bounty- fed sugar. Mr. Roberts showed how the effect of boun- ties was that the foreign exporter was able to send his sugar here cheaper than he could sell it at home, and the result was that we benefitted by receiving cheaper sugar and more of it. The Covention said that for the sake of the West Indian planters these bounties must not be given,and they were stopped. Trades in England which depended very much on cheap sugar had suffered in consequence. Ten thou- sand men who were previously engaged in chocolate, confectionery, and jam-making industries were now walking about doing nothing, 25,000 were working half- time, and hundreds of girls in London who used to work in the jam factories had had to go and compete with the poor little lasses who cleaned the doorsteps in the suburbs. Germany and France, the two countries who had been producing so much sugar and sending it here were, under the terms of the Convention, at liberty to I take bounty-fed sugar from Russia and Argentine while we could not take an ounce. Could they imagine a more insane and indefensible policy than that? If there was any sane principle involved in the Convention the, proper thing would have been to say to Germany and France "We will shut all bounty-fed sugar out, and you will shut it out too." Instead of that we shut it all out, and left them to take it all in. The result was that English manufacturers of machinery for confectionery and chocolate-making industries were as busy as they could be making machinery to be fixed in Germany and France in order that chocolate and confectionery factories might he set up to compete with British manufacturers all over the world. Incidentally dealing with taxation Mr. Roberts said they used to brag about a free breakfast table, but they would hardly have any- thing free directly. The whole trend of the arrange- ment of taxation in this country had been to put not simply the greatest amount but the greatest proportion on the people who were least able to bear it. The worst of it was that the taxes were collected indirectly so that the working classes did not know they were paying them. The wise-acres who made the arrangement to exclude bounty-fed sugar made a curious over-sight. They allowed the Germans and the French to send their chocolate and confectionery here and duty was paid only on the amount of sugar which was consumed in the manufacture. We sent confec- tionery and chocolate to Germany and France and we had not only to pay something in the shape of duty on the sugar but we had to pay duty on the whale article. What would his hearers have done if a man employed by them in a confidential position had arranged a contract for them and given an enormous advantage of that kind to the other side? They would have discharged him at once. There was not a man in the room whose life had not been blessed and whose future in every Way had not been brightened by the labours of men like Cobden and Bright—(cheers)—and all who were associated with them sixty ago. They had, said Mr. Roberts in con- clusion,</p>
        <p>A DUTY_ LAID UPON THEM</p>
        <p>as Liberals. They-la-a-A come into --;it:;at inheritance, and it was their duty as well as his own to see to it that those who had to come after them should not be able to say of them that they had shirked their duties upon such important matters as those. (Loud applause.) COUNCILLOR TINNION AND THE OBJECT OF THE FISCAL AGITATION.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he believed there was no sincerity behind Mr. Chamberlain's campaign. His opinion was that if the Conservative Party got into power with Mr. Chamberlain at the bead, they would never hear any more of the fiscal question. He hoped that when Mr. Chamberlain came to Warrington he would take advantage of the opportunity which be had not yet taken, of explaining his views on his mission to South Africa. He thought the whole idea and intention of springing the fiscal policy and other matters on the country was to draw away the attention of the British nubile from the terrible blunders which had been com- mitted in South Africa. (Applause.) Mr. Chamberlain had spent considerable time and had spared no expense in going from one end of South Africa to the other,and from one side to the other, but he had never, so far as he remembered, referred to the terrible blunder and bargaining he made when he carried out his policy in South Africa. He hoped when Mr. Chamberlain came to Warrington, in addition to the fiscal question, he would give his own private and honest opinion as to whether South Africa was worth the two hundred and fifty million pounds spent and from thirty to forty thousand lives which Aad been sacrificed. (Applause). During the evening enjoyable musical items were given by the Apollo Quartette Party (\fessrs, B. W. Cook, P. George, W. Woodcock and W. Critchley), and other vocalists, Mr. T. Brough supplying the accom- paniments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FORTHCOMING ELECTION. Assistance of Women Wanted in Warrington.</head>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING ELECTION. Assistance of Women Wanted in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH HOWES addressed a meeting of the War- rington Women's Liberal Association on Monday evening, with the object of enlisting the active interest of the women in the forthcoming general election. The day for ignoring women in political work, he said, was gone. For many years past they had rendered valuable services and it was now difficult to find a political con- test in which they were not actively engaged. He pointed out that women owed a great deal to the Liberal party, as it was the party which had given them the married women's property act, and made it possible for the first , time for married women to hold property in their own names. At the next election they wanted to enlist as much of the practical work of women as possible, I especially as Chamberlain by his interference and endeavour to re-establish protection had made the principal question to be decided by the electors one of bread and butter for every home in the land. Mr. Chamberlain had been perfectly candid and had said that his proposals meant the taxing of the food of the people but he (Hr. Chamberlain) contended that such a sacrifice was necessary for imperial purposes. If the Government wanted money there were many ways in which it could be raised without taxing the food of the people. A splendid revenue could be realized by the taxation of ground values and royalties, and the increasing of the death dues. People making great fortunes could afford to contribute something tangible while the weekly wage earner could not afford to pay. (Hear, hear.) The next great question to be considered by the electors was the question of education. In the matter of education we were being passed by continental countries, and therefore it became necessary that opportunities should be provided for the development of the intellectual abilities of the poorest child. It was the duty of the Government to provide those facili- ties, and to do so without interfering with creed. Creed was a question which should be considered too sacred for Parliament to touch, and any Government which used national funds for sectarian purposes was betray- ing the trust deposed in it, and should be turned out of office. (Applause). Out of national funds we wanted a national system of education, completely under the control of the public. The old Radical doctrine that taxation without representation was a fraud and must be resented, again come to the front. The position of the drink trade in ' the country was another great question which had to Ibe faced. He knew of no organization which exercised a more wonderful power at general elections than the drink trade. Although established and controlled by Act of Parliament the" trade "had been given a vested interest by the present government, and by the creation of that vested interest, in a trade which did more harm than any industry in the world the Government had not worked for the purpose of establishing virtue but for the establishment of vice. (Hear, hear.) The three questions he had named were questions which affected the home life and which should therefore give rise to great enthusiasm amongst the women of this country. In Warrington there existed a healthy Women's Liberal Association and it was desirous that the women should be stimu- lated to increased action. The Liberals had a splendid chance of winning at the coming contest but in order to win a great amount of work was to be done. The Liberals had an excellent prospective candidate who would not hesitate to do his part of the work and therefore the women must not hesitate to do their part. The coming election was purely a women's battle as whatever the result the home life would be affected,and therefore the women must work enthusiastically until the election was over. (Applause.) Mrs. C. JACKSON HOLMHS, who presided over a large attendance, expressed the hope that the women would all take an active and intelligent interest in the work. (Applause.) Questions were invited but the only one asked was as Ito how the women could help in forwarding the Liberal programme, to which Mr. Howes replied that the immediate work was the assisting in the distribution of literature. Mr. O'BRIEN, in seconding a vote of thanks to Mrs. Holmes for her services, pointed out that in the United States a license to sell drink was granted for twelve months, and at the expiration of that period the licensee had to close his house until a fresh license was granted. He also pointed out that on the day of an election all public-houses were closed. Mr. HOWES said the magistrates in England had the power to close all licensed houses on the day of an election, and speaking from personal experience he could say that it -nada a wonderful difference when all public houses were closed. The ;vote of thanks was heartily accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PRICE ON THEIR HEAD.</head>
        <p>PRICE ON THEIR HEAD.</p>
        <p>The Goverinien-t-of-Burma are waging War upon the wild animals of the Burma hills, and the following rewards are offered : Full-grown tiger. £3 ; tiger cub, leopard, or panther, £1 10s. ; leopard or panther cub, 15s. ; wild dog, 6s. Rewards are also offered for the killing of dangerous snakes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>UNNE</p>
        <p>_21.4..,.,,;:,•,:py,',:,..:?.,:.',:':4.:-.. ~„','.,,',...'`,.,1,4...•.,;,.f:.:'.:•-•-• ..“'-'.:-'.'',''';t:Tl.'4....::7:-;:`...</p>
        <p>NAbt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>VISIT. An Offer by Mr. R. A. Naylor.</head>
        <p>VISIT. An Offer by Mr. R. A. Naylor.</p>
        <p>An unique distinction which " Paddy " says belongs to him is that of having been in attendance at the last interment in the Parish Church yard and of having been about the first to drive a vehicle into the present Cemetery. In local annals the launching of the ill-fated vessel "The Tayleur " in the Mersey at Arpley will always stand out prominently, for those were the days when the prospects of the ship-building industry here were full of hope. Patrick was one of the great crowd which assembled to see the launch and he still pictures the scene of excitement when the vessel took the water, causing considerable flooding of the banks. The good ship " Tayleur " was destined to an extremely short service, for she was wrecked off Ireland on her first voyage. Another event which some of the present genera- tion may not be aware of, but which impressed itself ' on Patrick's mind, was a disastrous railway collision near Warrington about forty years ago. The casual- ties were heavy and Patrick played a useful part in conveying some of the injured to Warrington. As showing the confidence which " Paddy " com- manded, it may be mentioned that many years ago he was employed by one of the local banks to convey large sums of money to Bedford Leigh.. Precautionary measures, he says, were taken against highway robbery, as inside the vehicle sat armed men ready to repel any attack. Now the days of adventure ale over but in the midst of his hum-drum duties "Paddy" retains a buoyancy and light keartednesss which look always to the bright side of things. When I last saw him he was contemplating the celebration of the anniver- sary of Ireland's patron Saint. Not even St. Patrick's Day, however, sees him absent from his post, but Patrick, as a loyal son of Erin, hoped to do honour to the occasion by "the wearing of the green."</p>
        <p>MEM</p>
        <p>I put on second speed, and sent the car swiftly - between, and past, two converging vehicles, and, catching sight of Phyllis, narrowly missed colliding with a cab, which issued from a side street. The first time, I thought (unconscious, then, of the egoism from which it sprang—the lover is the most consistent, if harmless, egoist) she has seen me driving it. This incident of the day before was recalled by the opinion she expressed in her note—" I don't think, you know, motor-cars are quite safe." And then, "Do. you ? "</p>
        <p>Tlie great god Circumstance had (by means of the traffic) decreed that I should not speak to Phyllis, though she was so near. This particular phrase in her letter was rather curious, for she made no direct reference to having seen me. Was Phyllis prejudiced ? We inherit prejudice, which man dissipates in proportion as he exerts his. reasoning powers ; woman oftener prefers it 9hould be dissipated for her. With regard to one thing both sexes frequently have a prejudice, which, however, should not be interfered with—the woman in favour of 'dainty dresses, the man in favour of old coats. Phyllis' nature is so sweet, I cannot permit her too many prejudices, though there is one other—one also "in favour of "—which must remain sacred. Love has nothing to do with reason. (It is often triumphant unreason.) I must see her, therefore Phyllis must be open to conviction. Besides, she asks for my opinion. Of course I could write, but if one wishes to make a convert it:should not he attempted by correspondence. It is necessary to mark the effect of an argument before proceeding with another. And the eyes are often more truthful than lips. Moreover, the best argument in favour of the motor-car is a motorcar—so I took mine. Phyllis was not a bit surprised when she saw it. Far from being of the rakish, torpedo build that is apt to excite timidity in the novice, my car is a Titan, combining strength and comfort with speed. All the same Phyllis looked a bit doubtful. " It is very susceptible to sympathy," I said as she took her seat. Some women have in a peculiar degree the gift of imparting a softening influence on their surroundings, and this is never more n than with a motor- car. When a car carriei ,:ming woman, its lines seem to. become almost feminine.</p>
        <p>"Don't distract me more than you can help, Phyllis,' I said. "A motor-car is—well, a motor-car." "Why," she said—having been quietly observant until we got into the suburbs, when I put on first speed as requiring less attention—"why, if your'e not a speed enthusiast, it should be simple enough. You turn the wheel this way or that, and the car acts." "Your 'should' is a reservation that covers a multitude of possibilities for a motor-car. A motor- car," I said, "is like a woman—it requires delicate handling, and even things go wrong at times." " Why ? " "They have complex natures, you see. No two cars are exactly alike. They may be of similar make, build, and power, and all that—but each has its own idiosyncrasies." "I don't see the parallel," she said. "One marries a wife. She is like other women, desiring a fine house and all its adjuncts; she wishes to entertain, to have nice and well-known people visit- ing her "—some people think it is not necessary they should be nice, so long as they are well known—" but, with all this, she is different from the rest of her sex." "How ? " asked Phyllis. "Perhaps," with spirit, "you think she wants a more expensive car than any she has seen? Something inlaid with mother-of- pearl, diamond-studded, with silk hangings, and lighted at night with rose-coloured lamps?" "No," I said. "Despite certain joints of similarity, every woman has her own temperament, which must be studied."</p>
        <p>" You've not been driving a car more than three months," she said, with a mo ve. " True; but the fact was forced upon me early, and one gets more in sympathy with one's car every day." "I don't know anything of motor-cars, and—and you may be right—but how can you speak with authority on women? Your're not married." True, nor an I a parson, a doctor, or a West-End salesman. But, right or wrong, I began to have fore- bodings as to the wisdom of having said so much. "Some people would say "—non-committaly— "you had supplied the reason why my opinion should be valued."</p>
        <p>Phyllis would not look at me now, which materially assisted me to realise the enormity of my offence. "1 don't understand you," she said. "Why, they would consider me a disinterested, and, therefore, unprejudiced looker-on." "It's a wonderful position to be in, and I feel uncomfortable," she said. "I thought "—purposely misunderstanding her— "the upholstery was very good." " Not the car—you; your philosophic detachment.' "Philosophy, Phyllis. impossible! Perfect philo- sophy is not for man—because of woman, who is the onty bar to its complete attainment. Don't be hard on me ; my intentions were good." Conscience is the true measure of guilt. And I felt very guilty. We had been on the return journey for some time. Suddenly the traffic compelled a stop,and I had a light difficulty in restarting. " The car and I have lost something of the good understanding that existed," I said. ' Won't you sympathize with me the least bit ?" " I ought to withold it —but I can't." I thought Phyllis was going to forgive me. Then she added, " because while I am in the car I am an interested person ; and as you say women and motor- cars have characteristics in common, I must not pre- vent you making peace with your car." " You asked me, Phyllis—do you remember ?—if I thouglit motor-cars suite safe. I don't. And I don't like them—in certain circumstances. They're not convenient for two—nor three," as an after- thought. " You haven't said one nice thing to me all the afternoon."</p>
        <p>"Have you deserved it ?" she asked. " What I said of women was in sheer desperation." Though I believed it, I ought not to have said it ; ta Phyllis above all. " But it was quite true," she said ; " and so vra% your last remark about motor-cars. They are not-- nice. Let us go home fast."</p>
        <p>A motor-car—requires Jot of attention. And when Phyllis is present I consider such presumption. (To be continued.) (These articles originally appeared in the "Lo ndon Echo.")</p>
        <p>In all probability Mr. Chamberlain will visit Warring- ton on Saturday June 3rd to speak at the conference in connection with the Lancashire and Cheshire Workinr men's Conservative Federation. A number of towns la this district have been competing for the privilege of receiving Mr. Chamberlain, but by reason of its rail. way facilities and other advantages Warrington is con- sidered to have the prior claim. On Saturday morning at a meeting of prominent Conservatives it was stated if Mr. Chamberlain consented to come to Warrington a building to accommodate from 6,000 to 7,000 persons would have to be provided, and a substantial sum wad promised towards the expenses. The matter has now advanced a step further, Mr. 5' A. Naylor having it is stated offered the Federation tho use of one of the large timber sheds at Howley for the, meeting. The shed will accommodate 6,000 persons. The final decision rests with the Federation which meets in Liverpool to-day, but it is considered practic- ally certain that Warrington will be the town selected for the gathering.</p>
