      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SIXTY YEARS ON THE ROAD.</head>
        <p>SIXTY YEARS ON THE ROAD.</p>
        <p>A CHAT WITH WARRINGTON'S OLDEST DRIVER.</p>
        <p>A sturdy, cheery-faced character is Patrick Clarke familiarly known to his comrades of the whip and to a wider public as "Paddy." His seventy odd years sit lightly upon him for, although white locks betray advanced age, there is no sign of decrepitude. On the contrary Paddy displays an alertness and activity which sometimes excite the envy of much younger men. "I can mount a hansom with any man." he- is heard to declare with pride, and fellow-Johns ad miringly bear testimony to his surprising nimbleness. Every day, be it wet or fine, he takes his stand before the Patten Arms Hotel where he has come to be regarded as quite an indispensable adjunct. His is indeed a long working day. Abroad soon after seven in the morning, it is about eleven at night when he turns his steps homewards. An interesting, eventful life can this veteran driver tell of, and to hear him relate vivid narratives of his experiences to an all- attentive group of comrades is an entertainment not readily forgltten. For cbse upon sixty years Patrick Clarke has been "on the:road," and that he is now hale and hearty is, perhaps, testimony to the heathful- nese of an active, open calling. The distinction of being Warrington's oldest driver, if not one of the oldest in the kingdom, must certainly belong to him. When I looked him up the other day (writes an " Examiner " reporter), with the object of inducing him to chat on bye-gone times, he was sitting in a somewhat reflec- tive mood before the small, bright fire in a harness room. While drawing upon his store of reminiscences, he puffed at a short pipe, an old and trusty companion. Naturally age has impaired the'memory somewhat,and his story was not very connected on that account. This brief sketch is an attempt to weave the frag- ments of recollection into narrative form.</p>
        <p>As his name betokens, Patrick Clarke is a native of the Emerald Isle, but he came over to Warrington at the age of twelve or thirteen. " And what of the Warrington of those days ? " I queried. " Warrington. Why there was no Warrington then ! " was the somewhat startling reply. But Patrick was only delivering himself thus forcibly to indicate the great transformation be has lived to see in the aspect of the good old town. When our friend took up his abode in Warrington the Patten Arms Hotel, as we now know it, did not exist. There was, however, an hotel higher up the road which has gradually developed into Parker- street. The building now in the occupation of the London and North-Western Railway Company as offices then served as the hotel, and a Mr. Hughes was the landlord. Here it was that Patrick Clarke entered upon his career as a driver. In those days driving was a more exciting and eventful occupation than it is now, for the perfecting of the railway systni has entirely altered the perspec- tive of things. For about twenty years Patrick acted as a postillion and he must have ridden hundreds of miles in the saddle. He still preserves the rather picturesque costume, with buck-skin breeches and peaked cap, which he always wore at that time. Memories of dashing journeys through the country at all hours and over treacherous roads still linger in his mind. In one respect Patrick may account him- self particularly fortunate for, with all his , varied experiences,he was never involved in a serious accident until quite recently, and then it was the laying of the tram lines in Winwick Road which caused the over- turning of his cab. He was an inmate of the Infirmary for a considerable period, but is now quite well and strong again. • It is on the incidents of the postillion and long distance driving days that Patrick prefers most to dwell. Well-to-do travellers then were not so anxious to avail tbemselyes of the railways facilities which were of a somewhat primitive kind, and Patrick had to drive them from Warrington to all parts of the country. It has been his lot on more than one occasion to undertake the journey to London which was accom- plished in stages. He makes a proud boast of the fact that many dis- tinguished families have availed themselves of his services. "There are very few noblemen in the land , that I have'nt driven "he declares. What he esteems as perhaps the greatest honour of his life is that of having driven the King's carriage. That was in the days when His Majesty ,as Prince of Wales came down to Manchester to open an agricultural show and stayed at Worsley Hall. Patrick Clarke was one of the postillions selected to ride one of "the leaders" attached to the royal con- veyance. He has also been to Chatsworth to Owe for the Duke of Devonshire and a circumstance which he recounts with a sort of melancholy satisfaction is that of having attended the funeral of Lord Frederick Cavendish who was murdered in Phcenix Park, Dublin in 1882. The heads of many well-known county families in-this district have at one time or another, patronized Clarke. He carefully treasurers some pearl buttons taken from a coat which one local worthy,now deceased, presented him with. "Old Warrington," as Patrick pictures it, must indeed have been a quaint place. "I wonder how many people" he exclaimed "can remember having seen a thatched cottage in Bridge-street ?" Patrick can recollect the time when such old- fashioned structures were to be seen in the now busy thoroughfares, and, he told me, there used to be a bowling green on the plot of land where Mr. Ralph Johnson's shop stands. " All about here " indicating the hotel stable yard " was cowered with trees " added Patrick. The Mersey was then a clear stream, unpolluted by discharges from works, and Patrick declares that he has seen fish taken from it. He has also a recollection of the existence of those ancient instru mcnts of punishment—the stocks—near Trinity church " Paddy" is fond of relating an amusing story of a hoax perpetrated on Rev. W. Qirkett, the late respected Rector of Warrin,gton, in which he was un- intentionally an accomplice. Some local wag it appears, conceived the it:;ea of dispatching orders to numerous tradesmen for the delivery of goods of all descriptions at the Rectory. He even went so far as to give instructions for a carriage to be sent from the TPatten Arms at a specified time. " Paddy " was dispatched on this errand but, he affirms, he was quite unable to reach his destination as Church-street was blocked with all manner of con- veyances belonging to numerous other victims of the practical joker. Who was responsible for the affair it was never discovered. As all Warringtonians know, our Town Hall was once the residence of the late Lord Wlnmarleigh. Judging from" Paddy's " description that neighbour- hood must then have worn a semi-rural appearance. That was long before the Infirmary was built, and Clarke speaks of the road which now forms Legh- street being known as the "rabbit run." He claims to have driven the late Lord Wininarleigh on many occasions.</p>
