        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS. PROFESSOR KARL GILBERT, Of Brooklyn, has prophesied that the Niagara River would probably run dry in 3,500 years, because Lake Erie would find another outlet. -- ACCORDING to Professor Kankine Kennedy, the mode of propelling ships by a jet of water astern would utilise 76 per cent, of the power, whereas the screw propeller never utilises more than 71 per cent. KRTPTOL is a mixture of carbon, alumina, silica, and carborundum. When heated by the electrical current it acts like a fire, and the Kryptol Company of Berlin supply furnaces, radiators, baths, plate, and other heaters. SUNLIGHT AS GERMICIDE. Experiments made by the Bureau of Health, Massachusetts, have proved that sunlight is a more powerful germicide than was thought. Thirty minutes in the sun sterilised thin layers of water containing the germs of typhoid and the bacillus coli. ABOUT ALUMINIUM. Aluminium bronzes containing less than 6 per cent, of aluminium warm quickly and wear quickly' by friction with metal. The hardest bronzes, con- taining less than 89-6 per cent. of copper, wear best. These and other facts useful to makers and users of aluminium can be found in a report of the Bureau of Tests of Material of the Ecole Polytech- nique, Zurich. ARTIFICIAL ODOURS TO FLOWERS. Professor Baillif, a chemist, gives odours to flowers, natural or artificial. Fading flowers are immersed in a weak solution of chloride of ammonia, which revives them, and then sprayed with artificial perfume. If the natural odour of the flower is to be reinforced it is put into a glass containing glycerine, and enclosed in a box through which is sent a current of carbonic acid gas charged with the odour in question. FULL MOON AND FINE WEATHER. It is a saying in weather lore that the full moon fs accompanied by fine weather, and Mr. A. B. MacDowall, F.R.M.S., finds, by curves drawn from meteorological observapops at Ben Nevis and Greenwich, that in sunlmer there are few days of low barometer just after full and new moon, but many such days just after the quarters. Observa- tions made at Berlin, China, Constantinople, Jersey, Cayenne, and Kansas appear to bear this out. AN UNSINKABLE RAFT. Mr. Robert Chambers, of Dumbarton, has invented an unsinkable life-saving raft of wood, 201 t. long and 6ft. broad. It has three longitudinal bulk- heads, dividing the raft into thirty-two air-tight compArtments. A sea anchor and hawser prevent the raft from drifting to leeward in a heavy sea. It can be launched from a ship at a moment's notice, and has rowlocks for rowing and steering by oars. Each carries forty to fifty people, and lifelines round the side permit others to hang on. The raft has been sanctioned by the Board of Trade.</p>
