hero by sacrificing his life to rescue two fellow-workmen. Mr. Wallington also recounted an incident in the American Civil War, when a single young man volunteered to go to the battlefield in place of a married friend with a family. He fell in battle, and his friend had inscribed upon his grave stone the words : "He died for me." Mr. Blane, of Carrington was the speaker, and addressed the meeting upon a "hood Name." We often heard it said" What's in a name ?" In the Biblicalhistory, names had their special mean- ings. A good name Was not the exclusive possession of the rich, for it adorned the poor man as well as the rich. Let them try to make their names worthy of remembrance. To possess a good name they must be kind, with kindly thoughts of God andsif man.—Mr. Blane appealed to his hearers to be kind both in words and actions, and also impressed upon them the value of punctuality.—Rev. Bourman and the Secretary took part in the meeting. Mr. Payton as usual presided at the organ. Master Harry Griffiths sang sweetly "The Choristers."—The Secretary welcomed 11 new members. Ebenezer Church, Buttermarket Street. Special services were held on Sunday in celebration of the anniversary of the church. Mr. Wynne Jones preaching both morning and evening. 4n the afternoon a sacred cantata "captive maid of Israel " was ren- deredby the choir and friends, under the conductorship of Mr. Peter Smith. The principal soloists were : Mrs. Dutton, Misses K. Bowyer, M. E. Hughes (sopranos), E. Lee (contralto), and Messrs. R. W. Cook (tenor), and N. Cook (bass). Mr. B. Smith presided at the organ. On Monday evening a tea meeting and entertainment took place. Mr. Thornton presided, and an excellent programme was gone through, arranged by Misses Lee and Holmes. The programme opened with a mandoline solo by Miss Elpelvt, well played. A quintet for violins, mendolines, and piano by Miss Turner and others' was heartily encored. 'Miss Plinston was re- calls dfor a fine interpretation of " Good-bye." Miss A. Furness displayed good elocutionary powers in the ren- dering of The owl critic," while a sketch by Mr. Wilkes entitled " The butcher's daughter," was well received. Miss Plinston was heard to advantage in ' The last milestone," and Mr. R. V. Cook scored a success in "Bonnie Mary of Argyle." Miss Dorothy Turner gave a pianoforte solo, also a violin solo, and Miss Turner played a mandoline solo with ability. The boys of the school sang two choruses, which were well received, and the girls sang " Goodbye, little Yo-san," Miss E. Aikenhead singing the solo part. The programme was brought to a close by a humorous dialogue "Seaside lodgings,' by the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Dialogue Party. The Rev. John Yonge and the Revival. Preaching to a large congreation at Wycli le Church on Sunday the Rev. John Yonge took for his subject "General Booth's call from Jerusalem, and Dr. Horton's call from the chair of the National Free Church Council for universal prayer and consecration to the service and salvation of men." Basing his remarks on the words "From the days of John the Baptist until now ' the kingdom of heaven suffereth violence, and the- violent take it by force." Be said nobody, desired to speak needlessly disparaging 'words of his own times, but was it not unquestionable that the condition of religion, in this religious country, had for a long period been depressed, lethargic, apathetic, and indifferent ? Religion itself had not been the supreme principle of conduct. But now, there was a change occurring. Even the most unwilling were becoming sensible of it, per- I lisps to their discomfort. In some places the breath I had come, and they were spiritually living men and I women who but yesterday,as regards their real life,were absolutely dead. He had been moved to this discourse by I three events which had occurred during the past week The revival of religious fervour and zeal in the churches was the question of the time. God had a definite work for that church and congregation and school to under- take. Had not Wycliffe been to them far more their church than Christ's Church? They had cared for Wycliffe when it had met their notions, but they had not learned to care for it as Christ's Church. If it was Christ's Church, then it was far less theirs and far more everybody else's than they had realized. Should they seek to change their attitude and henceforth combine together, pastor and people, to make that church an attractive centre for the people outside? The second event on which he had something to'say was the call to prayer, addressed to all the Free Evangelical Churches, by Dr. Horton, of London, the president of the Free Church Council. That great federation held its meeting in Manchester the week before last. At that conference the supreme theme was revival. No note aroused such profound sympathy. In a letter to the Press that week Dr. Horton, as the Free Church leader, asked the churches to observe three days of prayer " that God may visit our whole country with spiritual quickening." Did they pray for the church which they attended, of which they might be members. Had they not abundant reason to believe that if only they., as he lievers, exer- cised prayer more truly and widely. the whole world would be the better? They had not, because they asked not, or because they asked amiss. Dr. Horton's call to prayer should be answered by them, not Puiy in their meetings, but in their secret devotions. The third event which had occasianed that discourse was the message addressed from the city of Jerusalem by General Booth "to all who named the name of Christ throughout the world." With the call ringing in their ears he hoped they would let the days of apathy pass and the time of earnestness begin. Let them accept the Gospel, live it and preach it, and by the grace of God make that church powerful for the conversion of sinners, the building up of Christians, and the saving of the people to the life divine.</p>
